3702:
** ὀπτός**, ή, όν,
**A** **roasted, broiled,** σῖτός τε κρέα τ᾽ ὀπτά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.21, compare 16.443; νῶτα βοὸς.. ὄπτ᾽ ἐν χερσὶν ἑλών 4.66; σάρκες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1097}}; ἑφθὰ καὶ ὀπτά boiled meats and **roast,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 358 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 404c.}}
**A.2** **baked,** βοῦν καὶ ἵππον.. ὀπτοὺς ἐν καμίνοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133}}; of bread, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.92}}; of fish, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 66.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; also of bricks and pottery, **baked, burned,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.180, 186, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.12, “PAmh.” 2.99 (a) 9 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; of soil, **parched,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 16.13: _superlative_ ὀπτότατος}} **best dressed** or **done,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 143.}}
**A.3** of iron, **forged, tempered,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 475.}}
**A.4** **scorched,** τὰ ὑπέρθυρ᾽ ὀπτά {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.65.}}

3703:
** ὀπώρ-α**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ὀπώρ-η**, ἡ: sometimes **ὁπώρα**, compare χεῖμα χὠπώραν, i.e. καὶ ὁπ-, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 76 (χειμάχωι πάραν, etc. codices)}}; _proper nouns_ Ὁπωρίς {{Refs|“IG” 5(1).1497, “Hopora” “CIL” 6.21782}}; compare μεθόπωρον, μεθοπωρινός:—**the part of the year between the rising of Sirius and of Arcturus** (i.e. the last days of July, all Aug., and part of Sept.), **the latter part of summer**; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus names θέρος and ὀπώρη}} together, θέρος τεθαλυῖά τ᾽ ὀπώρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.192}}; Σείριος being the star of ὀπώρη, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.27}}; compare ὀπωρινός.—In later times it became the name of a definite season, **autumn** (see. ὥρα {{Refs|1.1 c)}}, but was still used sometimes to denote **summer** (autumn being distinguished as φθινόπωρον or μετόπωρον), ἀρξάμενος ἀπὸ τοῦ ἠρινοῦ χρόνου πρὸ ὀπώρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 709, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 348a1; ἐπ᾽ ὀκτὼ μῆνας Κυρηναίους ὀπώρη ἐπέχει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.199; νέας δ᾽ ὀπώρας ἡνίκ᾽ ἂν ξανθῇ στάχυς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 304.7.}}
**II** **fruit,** γλαυκῆς ὀπώρας.. ποτοῦ χυθέντος.. Βακχίας ἀπ᾽ ἀμπέλου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 703; τέμνεται βλαστουμένη καλῶς ὀ. [prev. author] “fragment” 255.8; σικυούς, βότρυς, ὀπώραν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 569.1: so in Prose, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844d, 845c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 606b2, 629a2}}: in this sense also in _plural_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.43; 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus (75)}} even calls **honey** κηρίνα ὀπώρ; ἐαρινὴ ὀπώρα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus “fragment” 6.10.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, life's **summer, the time of** youthful **ripeness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.5}}; τέρειναν ματέρ᾽ οἰνάνθας ὀπώραν (see. οἰνάνθη) {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 5.6}}; **ripe virginity,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 998, 1015; ὀ. Κύπριδος 4th c.BC: Chaeremon Tragicus 12.}}

3704:
** ὅπως**,
_Epic dialect_ also and _Aeolic dialect_ **ὅππως**, _Ionic dialect_ **ὅκως**, _Doric dialect_ **ὁπῶς** _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 173.11: correlated to ὡς and πῶς.}} _adverb of Manner_, Relat. **as, in such manner as,** and with _interrogative_ force **how, in what manner,** rarely _indefinite_, see below {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus V.}}
**B** FINAL CONJUNCTION, **in such a manner that, in order that.** _adverb of Manner_, **how, as**:
**B.I** Relat. to ὥς or οὕτως (like ὡς), **in such manner as, as**:
**B.I.1** with the ordinary Constr. of the Relat.:
**B.I.1.a** with _indicative_, ἦ τοι νόστον, ὅπως φρεσὶ σῇσι μενοινᾷς, ὥς τοι Ζεὺς τελέσειεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.111; οὕτως ὅ... 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 330; ὧδ᾽ ὅ. [prev. author] “El.” 1301; οὕτως ὅ. δύνανται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67: sometimes an analogous word replaces the antecedent}} _adverb_, με τοῖον ἔθηκεν, ὅπως (for οἷον){{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐθέλει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.208: frequently without any antecedent expressed, ἔλθοι ὅ... ἐθέλω (i.e. αὐτὸν ἐλθεῖν) 14.172; ἔρξον ὅ. ἐθέλεις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.37, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.145; χρῶ ὅ. βούλει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.46; ποίει ὅ. ἄριστόν σοι δοκεῖ εἶναι [prev. work] 4.5.50}}; ὅ. ἔχω **as** I am, **on the spot,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 819.}}
**B.I.1.b** with _future_ _indicative_, especially after Verbs of seeing, providing, taking care.., **in the manner in which, how, that,** οἱ Περσικοὶ νόμοι ἐπιμέλονται ὅπως μὴ τοιοῦτοι ἔσονται οἱ πολῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.3; ποιέειν ὅκως μηκέτι κεῖνος ἐς Ἕλληνας ἀπίξεται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.23; ἐφρόντιζον ὅκως μὴ λείψομαι τῶν πρότερον γενομένων [prev. author] 7.8.ά, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29e; ἔπρασσον ὅπως τις βοήθεια ἥξει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.4; τοῦτο μηχανᾶσθαι ὅπως ἀποφεύξεται πᾶν ποιῶν θάνατον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39a; τούτου στοχαζόμενοι, ὅπως.. ἔσονται [prev. author] “Grg.” 502e (compare below 111.1 b, etc.)}}: this _future_ _indicative_ may become _optative_ after a historical tense, ἐπεμελεῖτο ὅπως μήτε ἄσιτοι μήτε ἄποτοί ποτε ἔσοιντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.43, compare “HG” 7.5.3, “Cyr.” 8.1.10, “Oec.” 7.5, “Ages.” 2.8}}; and ὅπως is frequently used interchangeably with such forms as δι᾽ ὧν, ὅτῳ τρόπῳ, etc., εἰσηγοῦνται μὴ δι᾽ ὧν.. ἀσκήσουσιν, ἀλλ᾽ ὅπως.. δόξουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.4, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.11}}: this sense easily passes into a final sense, **so that,** τοῦτο ἀπόβαλε οὕτω ὅκως μηκέτι ἥξει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40}}; οὕτω δ᾽ (i.e. ποίει) ὅπως μήτηρ σε μὴ 'πιγνώσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1296, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 905, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.25, “HG” 2.4.17}}; see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**B.I.2** with ἄν (_Epic dialect_ κε) and _subjunctive_ in indefinite sentences, **in whatever way, just as, however,** ὅππως κεν ἐθέλῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.243 (but ὅπως ἐθέλῃσιν (without κε) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.349, 6.189); οὕτως ὅκως ἂν καὶ δυνώμεθα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.143; οὕτως ὅπως ἂν αὐτοὶ βούλωνται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.2, compare “IG” 22.1.13 (5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116a, “Smp.” 174b, etc.}}
**B.I.2.b** with _optative_ after historical tenses, οὕτως ὅ. τύχοιεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.95; ὅ. βούλοιντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.13}}; in a gnomic statement, εἰκῇ κράτιστον ζῆν ὅ. δύναιτό τις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 979: when ἄν appears with the _optative_, it belongs to the Verb and not to ὅπως, ὅ. ἄν τις ὀνομάσαι τοῦτο}} **however** one might think fit to call it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.4.}}
**B.I.3** a very common phrase is οὐκ ἔστιν ὅ. (οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὅπως) there is no way **in which..,** it cannot be **that,** οὐκ ἔστι ὅκως κοτὲ σοὺς δέξονται λόγους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 18, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.208, +others}}; so οὐκ ἔστιν ὅ. οὐ, **fieri non potest quin,** οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὅ. οὐ πιστὸν ἐξ ὑμῶν πτερὸν ἐξήγαγ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 97, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 116, “Eq.” 426, “Th.” 882, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 27e}}; οὐδαμῶς ὅ. οὐ, in answer, **it must positively be so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 160d; so also οὐκ ἂν γένοιτο τοῦθ᾽ ὅ... οὐ φανῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1058}}; οὐ γὰρ γένοιτ᾽ ἄν, ταῦθ᾽ ὅ. οὐχ ὧδ᾽ ἔχειν (_anacoluthon_ for ἔχει or ἕξει) [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 378: so in questions, ἔσθ᾽ ὅ... ἔλθωμε; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 471 (variant{-οιμεν})}}]; ἔστιν οὖν ὅ. ὁ τοιοῦτος φιλοσοφήσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 495a, compare “Phdr.” 262b, “Tht.” 154c: so, besides _indicative_ of all tenses, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὅ.}} may be followed by _optative_ with ἄν, οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως μί᾽ ἡμέρα γένοιτ᾽ ἂν ἡμέραι δύο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1181, compare “V.” 212, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.156, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 184c: by _indicative_ with ἄν, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅπως ἂν.. κατέστησαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.206, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.28: ἄν}} is omitted in οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὅπως λέξαιμι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 471 (variant{ἔλθωμεν}).}}]
**B.I.4** in Trag., etc., like{ὡς} in comparisons, κῦμ᾽ ὅπως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1001; γῄτης ὅπως 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 32, compare 442, 683}}; ὅπως δρῦν ὑλοτόμοι σχίζουσι κάρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 98 (_anapaest meter_); ὅπως ἁ πάνδυρτος ἀηδών [prev. work] 1076 (Lyric poetry),compare “Ph.” 777, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1140; ὅκως τις καλλίης κάτω κύπτων 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.41}}; so in _Locrian dialect_ Prose, ὅπω(σ)ξένον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).334.2 (5th c.BC).}}
**B.I.5** like{ὡς} or ὅτι, with _superlative_ of Advs., ὅ. ἄριστα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 600, “IG” 12.44.8, etc.}}; ὅ. ἀνωτάτω as high up **as** possible, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 207}}; in full, οὕτως ὅ. ἥδιστα (i.e. ἔχει) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 330.}}
**B.I.6** with a _genitive_ added, σοῦσθε ὅ. ποδῶν run **as you are off for** feet, i. e. as quick as you can, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 837 (Lyric poetry, where however [ἔχετε] should probably be added); see below 111.10, ἔχω (A) 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.2b.}}
**B.I.7** sometimes of Time, **when,** Τρῶες.. ὅπως ἴδον αἷμ᾽ Ὀδυσῆος.., ἐπ᾽ αὐτῷ πάντες ἔβησαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.459, compare 12.208, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.373: frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} with _optative_, **whenever,** ὅκως μὲν εἴη ἐν τῇ γῇ καρπὸς ἁδρός {{Refs|1.17, compare 68, 100, 162, 186, 2.13, 174, +others}}: in Trag. and Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 198, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 749, “Tr.” 765, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 60}}: with _superlative_ of Advs., ὅ. πρῶτα **as soon as,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 156; ὅ. ὤκιστα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 427; ὅ. τάχιστα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 230.}}
**B.I.8** of Place, **where,** uncertain in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.75.}}
**B.II** ὅπως is sometimes used to introduce the substance of a statement, after Verbs of saying, thinking, or perceiving, **that, how,** λόγῳ ἀνάπεισον ὅκως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.37; οὐδὲ φήσω ὅκως.. [prev. author] 2.49, compare 3.115, 116}}; τοῦτ᾽ αὐτὸ μή μοι φράζ᾽, ὅπως οὐκ εἶ κακός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 548, compare “Ant.” 223, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 296e}}; after ἐλπίζειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 963, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 1051}}; after Verbs of emotion, ἐμοὶ δ᾽ ἄχος.., ὅπως δὴ δηρὸν ἀποίχεται grief is mine, when I think **how.. ** (i. e. **that.. **), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.109, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 169 (Lyric poetry)}}; after θαυμάζω frequently in _Attic dialect_, θαυμάζω ὅ. ποτὲ ἐπείσθησαν Ἀθηναῖοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.20, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43a.}}
**B.II.2** οὐχ ὅ... ἀλλὰ or ἀλλὰ καὶ.. is **not only not.. but..,** and is explained by an ellipsis of λέγω or ἐρῶ (compare ὅτι IV), οὐχ ὅ. κωλυταὶ.. γενήσεσθε, ἀλλὰ καὶ.. δύναμιν προσλαβεῖν περιόψεσθε **not only** will you **not** become.., **but** you will **also..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.35, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.34, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.9; οὐχ ὅ. ὑμῖν τῶν αὑτοῦ τι ἐπέδωκεν, ἀλλὰ τῶν ὑμετέρων πολλὰ ὑφῄρηται 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.26; οὐχ ὅ. τούτων χάριν ἀπέδοσαν, ἀλλ᾽ ἀπολιπόντες ὑμᾶς εἰς τὴν Λακεδαιμονίων συμμαχίαν εἰσῆλθον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.27, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.131, 53.13; οὐ γὰρ ὅπως.., ἀλλὰ καὶ.. [prev. author] 21.11; οὔκουν ὅπως.., ἀλλὰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.12; also οὐχ ὅ..., ἀλλ᾽ οὐδέ.., οὐχ ὅ. ἀδικοῦντες, ἀλλ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἐπιδημοῦντες ἐφυγαδευόμεθα [prev. author] “HG” 2.4.14; οὐχ ὅ. τῆς κοινῆς ἐλευθερίας μετέχομεν, ἀλλ᾽ οὐδὲ δουλείας μετρίας τυχεῖν ἠξιώθημεν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.5; διμοιρίαν λαμβάνων ἐν ταῖς θοίναις οὐχ ὅπως ἀμφοτέραις ἐχρῆτο, ἀλλὰ διαπέμπων οὐδετέραν αὑτῷ κατέλειπε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 5.1; οὐχ ὅ. ζημιοῦν, ἀλλὰ μηδ᾽ ἀτιμάζειν.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42: so sometimes μὴ ὅ. (where an _imperative_ must be supplied), μὴ ὅ. ὀρχεῖσθαι ἀλλ᾽ οὐδὲ ὀρθοῦσθαι ἐδύνασθε}} **do not think that** you could dance = **so far from being able** to dance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.10.}}
**B.II.2.b** οὐχ ὅ. rarely follows another clause, **to say nothing of.., let alone..,** πεπαύμεθ᾽ ἡμεῖς, οὐχ ὅ. σε παύσομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 796}}; μηδ᾽ ἐμπίδα, οὐχ ὅπως ταῦρον ἔτι ἄρασθαι δυνάμενος.. **let alone** a bull, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 8, compare “Prom.” 8, “Pr.Im.” 7, “Pisc.” 31.}}
**B.III** in in direct questions, **how, in what way** or **manner**:
**B.III.1** with _indicative_,
**B.III.1.a** ἔσπετε νῦν μοι ὅππως δὴ.. πῦρ ἔμπεσε νηυσίν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.113; εἴπ᾽ ἄγε μ᾽.. ὅππως τούσδ᾽ ἵππους λάβετον 10.545; εὖ μοι κατάλεξον ὅπως ἤντησας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.97; ὅπως ἠφανίσθη οὐδὲ λόγῳ εἰκότι δύνανται ἀποφαίνειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.26; Ἀλκιβιάδης ἀνήχθη.. ἐπὶ κατασκοπὴν.. τοῦ οἴκαδε κατάπλου ὅπως ἡ πόλις πρὸς αὐτὸν ἔχοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.11; οὐδέ τί πω σάφα ἴδμεν ὅπως ἔσται τάδε ἔργα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.252, etc.}}
**B.III.1.b** notably _future_ after Verbs of deliberation (like the _subjunctive_, see{{Refs|below 2), φραζώμεθ᾽ ὅπως ἔσται τάδε ἔργα 4.14}}; φράζευ ὅπως Δαναοῖσιν ἀλεξήσεις κακὸν ἦμαρ (variant{ἀλεξήσῃς}){{Refs 8th c.BC+|9.251, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.376, 386, 19.557, 20.29, 39.}}
**B.III.2** with deliberative _subjunctive_ after Verbs of deliberation, taking care, and the like, λεύσσει ὅπως ὄχ᾽ ἄριστα.. γένηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.110}}; ἐνόησεν (gnomic _aorist_) ὅππως κέρδος ἔῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.225; ἀλλ᾽ ἄγεθ᾽ ἡμεῖς οἵδε περιφραζώμεθα πάντες νόστον ὅπως ἔλθῃσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.77, compare 13.365; οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὅπως.. φῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1367, compare “Aj.” 428, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 91d; ἐπιμελητέον ὅπως τρέφωνται οἱ ἵπποι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.3, compare “Oec.” 7.36, 37, 9.14, 15.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 515c.}}—Sts. the _future_ and _subjunctive_ are conjoined without difference of meaning, ἐπράττετο γὰρ.., πρῶτον μὲν ὅπως μὴ περιμείνητε.., δεύτερον δὲ ὅπως ψηφιε̄σθε.., τρίτον δὲ ὅπως μὴ ἔσται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.65, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 7.7, “Mem.” 2.2.10.—On ὅπως ἄν (κεν), see below 5.}}
**B.III.3** with _optative_ after tenses of past time, τῶν ἀδῄλων ὅπως ἀποβήσοιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.3.2, etc.: after Verbs of deliberation, being virtually indirect speech, μερμήριξε.. Ἥρη ὅπως ἐξαπάφοιτο (direct speech πῶς ἐξαπάφωμα;) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.160; μερμήριζεν ὅπως ἀπολοίατο πᾶσαι νῆες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.554, compare 420; οὐ γὰρ εἴχομεν.. ὅπως δρῶντες καλῶς πράξαιμεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 271; ἐπεμελήθημεν ὅπως ἐξαλειφθείη αὐτῷ τὰ ἁμαρτήματα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.39, compare 13.32, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.11.}}
**B.III.4** with _optative_ and ἄν frequently expressing a wish, which in _direct speech_ would be expressed by πῶς ἄν, σκόπει ὅ. ἂν ἀποθάνοιμεν ἀνδρικώτατα [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 81 (variant{ἀποθάνωμεν}),compare “Nu.” 760; βουλευόμενοι ὅ. ἂν τὴν ἡγεμονίαν λάβοιεν τῆς Ἑλλάδος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.33, compare “Cyr.” 2.1.4; τῶν ἄλλων ἐπιμελεῖται ὅ. ἂν θηρῷεν (variant{-ῶσιν}) [prev. work] 1.2.10: the _optative_ with ἄν}}] and _subjunctive_ sometimes appear in consecutive clauses, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 3.2.1.}}
**B.III.5** ὅπως ἄν (κεν) with the _subjunctive_ is used after _imperative_ or _infinitive_ used as _imperative_, πείρα ὅπως κεν δὴ σὴν πατρίδα γαῖαν ἵκηαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.545; φράζεσθαι.., ὅππως κε μνηστῆρας.. κτείνῃς 1.295; σκοπεῖτε.., ὅ. ἂν ὑμῖν πρᾶγος εὖ νικᾷ τόδε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 233, etc.; φύλασσε.. ἔπειθ᾽ ὅ. ἂν.. ἡ χάρις.. ἐξ ἁπλῆς διπλῆ φανῇ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 618, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 539: in Prose, ἐπιμεληθῆναι ὅ. ἂν.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a; μηχανᾶσθαι [prev. author] “Phdr.” 239b, “Grg.” 481a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 917.}}
**B.III.6** rarely with _infinitive_, ἐπιμελήθητε προθύμως ὅπως διπλάσια.. σῖτα καὶ ποτὰ παρασκευασθῆναι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.37 (variant{-εσκευασμένα ᾖ}),compare “Oec.” 7.29, “HG” 6.2.32}}]; so later ὅπως παρακολουθῆμεν (_Doric dialect_ _infinitive_) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 1.170.18 (compare p.138, Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; ὅπως.. ἔχειν, ὅπως.. εἴργεσθαι, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.4, 85; ὅπως πέμπιν “PTeb.” 315.30 (2nd c.AD).}}
**B.III.7** after Verbs of fear and caution, ὅπως and ὅπως μή are used with _future_ _indicative_ or _aorist_ _subjunctive_:— the readings are frequently uncertain: the following (among others) are made certain either by the metre or the form,
**B.III.7.a** with _future_ _indicative_, δέδοιχ᾽ ὅπως μὴ τεύξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 112; παντὶ λόγῳ ἀντιτείνετε εὐλαβούμενοι ὅπως μὴ.. οἰχήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91c; φόβος.. ἔστιν.. ὅπως μὴ αὖθις διασχισθησόμεθα [prev. author] “Smp.” 193a}}: sometimes the preceding Verb is omitted, ὅπως μὴ οὐκ.. ἔσομαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Men.” 77a.}}
**B.III.7.b** with _aorist_ _subjunctive_, τὴν θεὸν δ᾽ ὅ. λάθω δέδοικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 995; φυλάττου, ὅ. μὴ εἰς τοὐναντίον ἔλθῃς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.16}}: rarely with _present_, οὐ φοβεῖ ὅ. μὴ ἀνόσιον πρᾶγμα τυγχάνῃς πράττων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4e}}: sometimes the preceding Verb is omitted, with _aorist_ _subjunctive_, ὅκως μή τι ὑμῖν πανώλεθρον κακὸν ἐς τὴν χώρην ἐσβάλωσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.85}}: with _present_ _subjunctive_, ὅπως μὴ.. ᾖ τοῦτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 430d.}}
**B.III.7.c** with _optative_ representing _subjunctive_ after a historical tense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.3.}}
**B.III.8** this Constr. is used in admonitions or commands: in the originally Constr. a Verb implying caution or circumspection precedes, ὅρα ὅκως μή σευ ἀποστήσονται Πέρσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36; ἄθρει.. ὅπως μὴ ἐκδύσεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 141; τηρώμεσθ᾽ ὅπως μὴ.. αἰσθήσεται [prev. work] 372: but this came to be omitted, and ὅπως or ὅπως μή}} with _future_ _indicative_ or _aorist_ _subjunctive_ are exactly = the _imperative_, ἔμβα χὤπως ἀρεῖς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 378 (Lyric poetry): most frequently with}}_future_ _indicative_, ὅκως λόγον δώσεις τῶν μετεχείρισας χρημάτων, ={δίδου λόγον}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142}}; ὅπως παρέσει μοι, ={πάρισθι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 131; ὅπως πετήσει [prev. author] “Pax” 77, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.3, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.21, 12.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 489a, etc.}}: rarely with I _person_, ὁποῖα κισσὸς δρυός, ὅπως τῆσδ᾽ ἕξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 398, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 297 (Lyric poetry)}}: very rarely with _aorist_ _subjunctive_, ὅπως μή τι ἡμᾶς σφήλῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 296a codices; ὅπως μὴ.. ἐξαπατήσῃ [prev. author] “Prt.” 313c; ὅπως μὴ ποιήσητε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.20 codices}} —The codices frequently vary, as between διδάξεις and **-ξῃς** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 824}}; τιμωρήσονται and **-ωνται**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.56; πράξομεν and -ωμεν [prev. work] 82}}; θορυβήσει and **-σῃ**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.14, etc.}} —Since the _future_ is frequently, and the _aorist_ (whether 1 or 2) rarely guaranteed by metre or form, the _aorist 1_ forms should probably be rejected, both in _signification_ 7 and 8, in cases where codices vary.
**B.III.9** as the echo to a preceding πῶ; in dialogue, A καὶ πῶ; B ὅπως"; [do you ask] **how?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 128; A πῶς με χρὴ καλεῖ; B ὅπω; [prev. author] “Nu.” 677, compare “Pl.” 139.}}
**B.III.10** with a _genitive_ (see.{{Refs|above 1.6), οὐκ οἶδα παιδείας ὅπως ἔχει καὶ δικαιοσύνης}}**in the matter of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 470e, compare “R.” 389c.}}
**B.IV** in **direct** questions, **how?** ἔπραξας ὅπω; {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 82p.106B.}}-C.; compare ὅστις.
**B.V** _indefinite_, **anyhow,** τὸ οὐδ᾽ ὅ. the expression 'not **at all**', [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 183b (variant{οὐδ᾽ οὕτως}).}}]
**B** FINAL CONJUNCTION, **that, in order that,** the original notion of modality being merged in that of purpose or design, compare ἵνα, with which it is sometimes interchanged, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.23 and 24, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.14, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 119f:—in early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. only ὅπως ἄν is used, “IG” 12.39.19, +others; ὅπως without ἄν only once in 4th c.BC, [prev. work]22.226.42 (4th c.BC), after which it becomes gradually prevalent:}}
**B.1** with _subjunctive_,
**B.1.a** after primary tenses, or after _subjunctive_ or _imperative_, τὸν δὲ μνηστῆρες.. λοχῶσιν, ὅπως ἀπὸ φῦλον ὄληται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.181, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 873, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 238, “El.” 457, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.10.2, etc.}}
**B.1.b** after historical tenses (see. ἵνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.1b), when there is no}}_perfect_ form, or when the _aorist_ represents the _perfect_, ξυνελέγημεν ἐνθάδε, ὅ. προμελετήσωμεν we were convened, i. e. we have met in assembly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 117; παρήλθομεν.., ὅπως μὴ χεῖρον βουλεύσησθε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.73; also when the occurrence purposed is regarded from the point of view of the person purposing, ἦλθον πρεσβευσόμενοι, ὅπως μὴ σφίσι.. τὸ αὐτῶν [ναυτικὸν] ἐμπόδιον γένηται [prev. work] 31, compare 57, 65}}, etc.: sometimes the _optative_ and _subjunctive_ appear in consecutive clauses, φρυκτοὺς παρεσκευασμένους ἐς αὐτὸ τοῦτο, ὅπως ἀσαφῆ τὰ σημεῖα.. ᾖ καὶ μὴ βοηθοῖεν {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.22, compare 6.96, 7.17.}}
**B.2** with _optative_ after historical tenses, πὰρ δέ οἱ αὐτὸς ἔστη, ὅπως.. κῆρας ἀλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.548; more frequently in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, as 13.319, 14.312, 18.160, 22.472}}; so in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005, “OC” 1305, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 77e, etc.}}: after historical _present_, πέμπει τούσδ᾽ ὅπως κτείνοιεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 450; ἡγεμόνα πέμπει ὅπως ἄγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.19: after _optative_, ἔλθοι.. ὅ. γένοιτο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 297, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1221 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.3** with _indicative_,
**B.3.a** of historical tenses, where the principal clause expresses an action or obligation unfulfilled, εἴθ᾽ εἶχε φωνὴν ἔμφρον᾽ ἀγγέλου δίκην, ὅ. δίφροντις οὖσα μὴ 'κινυσσόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 196, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1134}}: rare in Prose, ἐδεξάμην ἃν.. φράσαι πρὸς ὑμᾶς.., ὅ... προῄδετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.21}}; τίς οὐκ ἂν.. ταῦτα ἐδήλωσεν, ὅ... ταῦτα ἠλέγχθ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.20; οὐκοῦν ἐχρῆν σε Πηγάσου ζεῦξαι πτερόν, ὅ. ἐφαίνου τοῖς θεοῖς τραγικώτερος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 135}}; τί.. οὐκ ἔρριψ᾽ ἐμαυτὴν.. ὅ. ἀπηλλάγη; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 749.}}
**B.3.b** of _future_, θέλγει, ὅ. Ἰθάκης ἐπιλήσεται (= φραζομένη ὅπως ἐ.) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.57, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.136; [χρὴ] ἀναβιβάζειν ἐπὶ τὸν τροχὸν τοὺς ἀπογραφέντας, ὅ. μὴ πρότερον νὺξ ἔσται 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.43; ἐμισθώσατο τοῦτον.., ὅ. συνερεῖ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.316}}: sometimes _future_ _indicative_ and _aorist_ _subjunctive_ are conjoined, σιγᾶθ᾽, ὅ. μὴ πεύσεταί τις, ὦ τέκνα, γλώσσης χάριν δὲ πάντ᾽ ἀπαγγείλῃ τάδε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 265.}}
**B.II** ὅπως with _subjunctive_ is sometimes used after Verbs of will and endeavour, instead of the _infinitive_, λίσσεσθαι.. ὅ. νημερτέα εἴπῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.19; αἰτεῖσθαι ὅ. μὴ καταψηφίσησθε 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.12; δεήσεται.., ὅ. δίκην μὴ δῷ [prev. work]23; ὅ. μὴ ἀποθάνῃ ἠντεβόλει 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.29}}; παρακελεύεσθε ὑμῖν αὐτοῖς ὅ... ἐξίητε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 127 (ἔξιτε Rehdantz): with ἄν, δεῖταί μου σφόδρα ὅπως ἂν οἰκουρῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1060, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.126, 3.44; διεκελεύετο ὅπως ἂν.. ἐγγράφωσί με 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.27}}; so δεῖ σ᾽ ὅ. δείξεις (for δεῖξαι), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 556, may be explained as ellipsis for δεῖ σ᾽ ὁρᾶν (σκοπεῖν) ὅπως,compare [prev. author] “Ph.” 55; δεῖ σ᾽ ὅπως.. μηδὲν διοίσεις.. 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 108.}}

3705:
** ὅρᾱμ-α**, ατος, τό,
**that which is seen, visible object, sight,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 428a16, “EN” 1173b18, +others}}; **sight, spectacle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.66}}; **vision** during sleep, **dream,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Gen.15.1, +others, “PGoodsp.Cair.” 3.5 (3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 78.37 (2nd c.BC), “SIG” 1128 (Delos, 2nd-1st c.BC), “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(1).249; ἐν τοῖς ὁράμασι τοῦ θεοῦ 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 42(6).8 codices (ἰάμασι conjecture Keil).}}
**II** **device, plan,** τὸ ὅ. Θάλεω (Camer. εὕρημα) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1259a31, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 55.1.}}

3706:
** ὅρ-ᾱσις**, εως, ἡ,
**seeing, the act of sight,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator 3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1174a14, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 123, etc.}}; distinguished as the ἐνέργεια or **act** from ὄψις (the sense or faculty), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 426a13, compare 428a7}}; but, **power of sight**, {{Refs|“SIG” 1141 (from Thrace).}}
**2** _plural_, **eyes,** τὰς ὁ. ἐκκόπτειν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.6, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.45, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.88d: _metaphorically_, as title of the daughter-goddess of the Sun, ὅρασιν αὑτοῦ (i.e. τοῦ Ἡλίου) “OGI” 56.56 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II** **a vision,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Joel.2.28, NT.Act.2.17 (_plural_).}}
**III** **appearance,** ὅμοιος ὁράσει λίθῳ ἰάσπιδι {{NT|NT.Rev.4.3; ὁ μεταμορφούμενος ἐν ταῖς ὁράσεσιν “PLeid.W.” 13.36.}}

3708:
** ὁράω**,
_contraction_ **ὁρῶ** even in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.234}}, _Epic dialect_ **ὁρόω** {{Refs|5.244}}, etc.; _Aeolic dialect_ **ὄρημι** (which see); _Ionic dialect_ **ὁρέω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, etc.}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ὁρῇς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.67, +others}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὁρῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ σαρκῶν” 17, “Vid.Ac.” I}}; _infinitive_ ὁρῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 11, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ σαρκῶν” 2}} (but _2nd pers._ _singular_ ὁρᾷς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 87}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὁρᾷ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.80, compare κατορᾷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ὁρῶμεν {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.40}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὁρῶσι (ἐπ-) {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.124}}_infinitive_ ὁρᾶν{{Refs|[prev. work] 33, 2.64): the forms ὁρῇς, ὁρῇ, ὁρῆν}} (except when found in _Doric dialect_, as {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.2, 15,47 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; _imperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἑώρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]28,70) seem to imply ὁρή-ω (compare ὄρημι), but ὁρᾷ, ὁρῶμεν, ὁρῶσι, etc. imply ὁρᾰ-ω: _Attic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἑώρων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 713, “Nu.” 354, (ἐ-) “SIG” 344.110 (Teos, 4th c.BC)}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὥρα [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, 3.72, _1st pers._ _plural_ ὡρῶμεν (variant{ὁρῶμεν}) [prev. author] 2.131}}],_2nd pers._ _plural_ ὡρᾶτε {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.8.β᾽}} _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὥρων {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.3, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὅρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.646, compare ὄρημι:}}_perfect_ ἑόρᾱκα, a form required by the metre in many passages, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 32, 33, “Av.” 1573, “Pl.” 98, 1045, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 181.3, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 272.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 166, “Pk.” 270, 3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 5.11, etc., whereas the metre never requires ἑώρακα}}; whence ἑόρακα, -άκη ought always to be restored in early _Attic dialect_ writers, though ἑώρακα was used in later Gr., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.55 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 685.74 (2nd c.BC), “UPZ” 119.43 (2nd c.BC),compare 9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 150}} (ἑώρακε[ν] is probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 5 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator): ἑωρ- in the}}_imperfect_ probably comes from ἠ- ϝορ- (with a long form of the augment, compare ἠειδ- (ᾐδ-) in _imperfect_ of οἶδα, while ἑορ- in the _perfect_ comes from ϝε-ϝορ-, see below: _Ionic dialect_ _perfect_ ὁρώρηκα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.77, +others, also ὥρηκα [prev. work] 40}}; _Doric dialect_ _perfect_ _participle_ ὡρακυῖα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.6 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; _1st pers._ _singular_ ὥρακα{{Refs|Baillet “Inscription destombeaux des rois” 1210}}: an _aorist 1_ ἐσορήσαις only _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 247.16:}} —_middle_ ὁράομαι, _contraction_ ὁρῶμαι even in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.99}}; _Epic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ὅρηαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.343 (see. ὄρημι):}}_imperfect_ ἑωρώμην, also ὡρώμην (προ-) {{NT|NT.Act.2.25}}, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὁρᾶτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.56}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἑώραμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.110, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.16:}}_aorist_ ἑωράθην only in late Prose, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.6}}; _infinitive_ ὁρᾱθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Motu Animalium” 699b19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 411b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Jud.Voc.” 6, etc.}}: _future_ ὁραθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 10.12: verb.}} _adjective_ ὁρᾱτός, ὁρᾱτέον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(which see).— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses _contraction_ forms, as ὁρῶ, ὁρᾷς, ὁρᾷ, ὅρα, ὁρᾶν, ὁρῶν, ὁρῶμαι, ὁρᾶται, ὁρᾶσθαι, ὁρώμενος, as well as lengthened _Epic dialect_ ὁρόω, ὁράᾳς, ὁράασθαι, ὁρόων}} _2nd pers._ _plural_ _optative_ ὁρόῳτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.347, etc.}}; besides these forms from ὁρα- (ὁρη-) we have
**II** from root ὀπ- (see. ὄψ) the only _future_ in use, ὄψομαι, always in _active_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.704}}, and _Attic dialect_, _Epic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ὄψεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.511: a rare}}_aorist 1_ ἐπ-όψατο in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 88.6 (for ἐπιώψατο, see at {ἐπιόψομαι})}}]; _subjunctive_ ὄψησθε [{{NT|NT.Luke.13.28 (where the variant{ὄψεσθε} may be right)}}]: _perfect_ ὄπωπα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.124, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.94, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 109, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.37, 63, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 1, “Carn.” 17; Trag. and Comedy texts, as 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 57, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 6, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1157, 1225}}, never in _Attic dialect_ Prose: _pluperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὀπώπει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.123, ὀπώπεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.ζ᾽}}; ὀπώπεσαν {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.125:}} —_passive_, _aorist 1_ ὤφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 709, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 970, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.73, etc.}}; _optative_ (_Ionic dialect_) ὀφθείησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.7}}; _participle_ ὀφθείς, _infinitive_ ὀφθῆναι, [{{Refs|[prev. author] 1.9, 10 (for ἐπι-οφθέντας, see at {ἐπιόψομαι})}}]: _future_ ὀφθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 452, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1155, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.10, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.34}}: _perfect_ ὦμμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator “fragment” 165, ὦψαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.263, ὦπται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 998, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.66}}; compare ὀπτέον.
**III** from ϝιδ- are formed _aorist_ _active_ εἶδον, _infinitive_ ἰδεῖν: _aorist_ _middle_ εἰδόμην, _infinitive_ ἰδέσθαι: _perfect_ with _present_ sense οἶδα **I know,** _infinitive_ εἰδέναι: verb. _adjective_ ἰστέος (for these tenses, see at {Εἴδω}). (ὁρ- probably from ϝορ-, as indicated by the _imperfect_ and _perfect_ forms; compare βῶροι (i.e. ϝῶροι), Engl. (**a)ware.**)
**A0**Senses:
**A0.I** _absolutely_, **see, look,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; εἴς τι or εἴς τινα to or at a thing or person, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.633, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.373, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 607; εἰς τὸν πράττοντα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1460a14}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.439, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 534, “fragment” 188}}; but ἔς τινα ὁρᾶν **to be of** so-and-so's **party,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.18}}; εἰς τὴν Ἀττάλου καθαίρεσιν εἶδεν **aimed** at.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus 6.12; for κατ᾽ αὐτοὺς αἰὲν ὅρα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.646, Τροίην κατὰ πᾶσαν ὁρᾶται 24.291, compare καθοράω 11}}; ὁρόων ἐπ᾽ ἀπείρονα πόντον **looking** over the sea, {{Refs|1.350}}; ὁρᾶν πρός τι **look towards,** ἀκρωτήριον τὸ πρὸς Μέγαρα ὁρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.93, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 7.496 (4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus, conjecture)}}; πρὸς πλοῦν ὁρᾷ **looks** to sail (i.e. is ready), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” [1624]; ὁ. ἐπὶ τὴν προδοσίαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 36.3; πρὸς σπονδάς [prev. author] 33.1.}}
**A0.I.2** **have sight,** opposed to μὴ ὁρᾶν, to be blind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 84}}; ὅσ᾽ ἂν λέγωμεν, πάνθ᾽ ὁρῶντα λέξομεν [though I am blind,] my words **shall have eyes,** i.e. shall be to the purpose, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 74}}; ἐν σκότῳ.. οὓς μὲν οὐκ ἔδει ὀψοίαθ᾽, i.e. should be blind, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 1274}}; ἀμβλύτερον ὁ., opposed to ὀξύτερον βλέπειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 596a}}; ἐπὶ σμικρὸν ὁ. **to be** short-**sighted,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 174e; ὁ. βραχύ τι [prev. author] “R.” 488b.}}
**A0.I.3** **see** to, **look** to, i.e. **take** or **give heed,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.239}}; ὁ. εἰς γλῶσσαν.. ἀνδρός **look** to, **pay heed** to, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.7, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 104 (Lyric poetry)}}: frequently in _imperative_, like{βλέπε}, followed by a dependent clause, ὅρα ὅπως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 300, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.27}}; ὅρα εἰ.. **see** whether.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 997, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 118, etc.; also ὅρα μὴ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 30, 519, etc.}}; ὅρα τί ποιεῖς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 589; πῶς.. ὑπερδικεῖς, ὅρα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 652.}}
**A0.I.4** ὁρᾷ; ὁρᾶτ; **see'st thou? d'ye see?** parenthetically, especially in explanations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 355, “Th.” 490, etc.; ὡς ὁρᾷς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1114, “Tr.” 365}}; also ὁρᾷ; at the beginning of a sentence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 628, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 87}}; ἀλλ᾽—ὁρᾷ; but, **do you see.. ?,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 336b; ὁρᾷς οὖν.. ὅτι [prev. author] “Grg.” 475e}}; οὐχ ὁρᾷ; ironically, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.232.}}
**A0.I.5** with _accusative_ _cognate_, like{βλέπω} 11, **look** so and so, δεινὸν ὁρῶν ὄσσοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 426; ὁρᾶν ἀλκάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.111; ἔαρ ὁρόωσα Νύχεια 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.45}}; also ἡδέως ὁρᾶν **look** pleasant, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1122: with _accusative_, κακῶς ὁρᾶν τινα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 7.42.}}
**A0.II** _transitive_, **see** an object, **behold, perceive, observe,** with _accusative_, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.:}}_perfect_ ὄπωπα exclusively in this sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.799, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.94, etc.}}; ὀφθαλμοῖσιν or ἐν ὀφθαλμοῖσιν ὁρᾶν **to see with** or **before** the eyes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.392, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.459, etc.}}; αἰεὶ τέρμ᾽ ὁρόων always **keeping** it **in sight,** keeping his eye on it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.323; φίλως χ᾽ ὁρόῳτε καὶ εἰ δέκα πύργοι Ἀχαιῶν.. μαχοίατο, i.e. δέκα πύργους, εἰ μαχοίατο, 4.347}}; ὁ. τινά **look to** (for aid), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.64: in significations 1 and 11 combined, οὐχ ὁρᾷς ὁρῶν τάδ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1623, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.89.}}
**A0.II.b** ζώει καὶ ὁρᾷ φάος ἠελίοιο, _poetry_ for ζῆν, like{βλέπειν}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.61, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.833, etc.; so φῶς ὁρᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 375, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1523, Alcaeus691}}:— in _middle_, φέγγος ὁρᾶσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Andr.” 113 (eleg.)}}; so ὁρᾶν alone, τὰ γὰρ φθιτῶν τοῖς ὁρῶσι κόσμος {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 78 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A0.II.c** followed by a clause, οὐχ ὁράᾳς οἷος..; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.108}}; ὁρᾷς ἡμᾶς, ὅσοι ἐσμέ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327c}}; οὐχ ὁρᾷς ὅτι ἥμαρτε; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 261, compare 325, 951; Διὸς.. οὐχ ὁρῶ μῆτιν ὅπᾳ φύγοιμ᾽ ἄν [prev. work] 906 (Lyric poetry); ἴδεσθέ μ᾽ οἷα.. πάσχω [prev. work] 92.}}
**A0.II.d** with _participle_, καπνὸν.. ὁρῶμεν ἀπὸ χθονὸς ἀΐσσοντα **we see** it rising, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.99}}; ὁρῶν ἐμαυτὸν ὧδε προυσελούμενον **seeing** myself thus insulted, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 438, compare 70, 384, +others}}; ὁρῶ σε κρύπτοντα.. **see** you hiding.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 342}}; so ὁρῶ μ᾽ ἔργον ἐξειργασμένην I **see that** I have done, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 706; compare below 4b}}; rarely in reference to the subject, ὁρῶ μὲν ἐξαμαρτάνων (= ὅτι ἐξαμαρτάνω) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 350}}:—so in _middle_, ἄνδρα διωκόμενον.. ὁρῶμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.169, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 896 (Lyric poetry)}}: also with _infinitive_, ἑώρων οὐκέτι οἷόν τε εἶναι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.60.}}
**A0.II.e** rarely with _genitive_, οὐδεὶς Σωκράτους οὐδὲν ἀσεβὲς.. οὔτε πράττοντος εἶδεν οὔτε λέγοντος ἤκουσεν (where the Constr. is suggested by the use of ἤκουσεν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.11; μέχρι βορῆος ἀπαστράψαντος ἴδηαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 430.}}
**A0.II.2** **see to,** ἴδε πῶμα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.443}}; **look out for, provide,** τινί τι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1165 (_anapaest meter_), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.2; πρόβατον εἰς ὁλοκάρπωσιν LXX.Gen.22.8.}}
**A0.II.3** the _infinitive_ is used after an _adjective_, δεινὸς ἰδεῖν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.6; εὐφεγγὴς ἰδεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 387, compare 398, “Ch.” 174, 176, +others (compare Εἴδω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.1 a)}}; ἐχθίστου.. ὁρᾶν most hateful **to behold,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 818; ὦ πάτερ δύσμοιρ᾽ ὁρᾶν [prev. author] “OC” 327; ὁρᾶν στυγνὸς ἦν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.9}}:—_middle_ or _passive_, αἰσχρὸς ὁρᾶσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 3.3}}: with an _adverb_, μὴ διχορρόπως ἰδεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 349}}: with a _substantive_, ἄνδρα τευχηστὴν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 644}}: with a Verb, πρέπουσι.. ἰδεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 720, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 792.}}
**A0.II.4** _middle_ is used by Poets like _active_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 179, “Ch.” 407 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 594 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 306, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 138, etc., see above 11.1 b, c: but in Prose}}_middle_ occurs only in compounds, as προ-ορῶμαι: for the _imperative_ ἰδοῦ, ἰδού, see at {ἰδοῦ}.
**A0.II.4.b** no _passive_ is used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; in _Attic dialect_ the _passive_ has the sense **to be seen,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 998, “Eu.” 411, etc.: with}}_participle_, ὤφθημεν ὄντες ἄθλιοι **was seen** in my wretchedness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 933; ὀφθήσεται διώκων}}**he will prove to be..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239c, compare “Smp.” 178e}}; τὰ ὁρώμενα **all that is seen, things visible,** like{τὰ ὁρατά}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prm.” 130a.}}
**A0.III** _metaphorically_, of mental sight, **discern, perceive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 945, etc.}}; so blind Oedipus says, φωνῇ γὰρ ὁρῶ, τὸ φατιζόμενον I **see** by sound, as the saying is, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “OC” 138 (_anapaest meter_); ἂν οἴνου.. ὀσμὴν ἴδωσιν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 222.4; compare above 1.4, δέρκομαι 1.2.}}
**A0.IV** _absolutely_, **see visions,** ὁ ἀληθινῶς ὁρῶν {{LXX|LXX.Num.24.3, 15}}:—_passive_, **appear in a vision,** ὤφθη ἄγγελος πρὸς τὴν γυναῖκα{{LXX|LXX.Judg.13.3.}}
**A0.V** **interview,** ἐμνήσθης μοι ἰδεῖν τὸν κεραμέα περὶ τῶν κεραμίων you told me to **see** the potter about the jars, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 264 (3rd c.BC).}}

3709:
** ὀργή**, ἡ,
**natural impulse** or **propensity** (see. ὀργάω II): hence, **temperament, disposition, mood,** κηφήνεσσι κοθούροις εἴκελος ὀργήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 304, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 98, 214, 964, etc.; ὀργὴν ἄλλοτ᾽ ἀλλοίην ἔχει 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.11}}; so μείλιχος, γλυκεῖα ὀργά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.43, “I.” 2.35; εὐανθεῖ ἐν ὀργᾷ παρμένων [prev. author] “P.” 1.89; ὀργῆς τραχύτης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 80}}; ὠμή, ἀτέραμνος ὀργή, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 187, “Pr.” 192, etc.}}; ὀργῆς νοσούσης εἰσὶν ἰατροὶ λόγοι **of 'a mind** diseased', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 380: so in _plural_, “hymnus ad Cererem” 205, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).34; ὀργαῖς ἀλωπέκων ἴκελοι [prev. author] “P.” 2.77; κνωδάλων ἔχοντες ὀργάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 763}}; ἀστυνόμοι ὀργαί social **dispositions,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 356 (Lyric poetry, compare σύντροφος 3); ὀργαὶ ἤπιοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 53}}: also in Prose, διεπειρᾶτο αὐτῶν τῆς τε ἀνδραγαθίης καὶ τῆς ὀργῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.128; οὐ τῇ αὐτῇ ὀ. ἀναπειθομένους τε πολεμεῖν καὶ ἐν τῷ ἔργῳ πράσσοντας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140; τῇ ὀ... χαλεπῇ ἐχρῆτο [prev. work] 130}}; ἐπιφέρειν ὀργάς τινι suit **one's moods** to another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.83, compare 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 230; ὁ πόλεμος πρὸς τὰ παρόντα τὰς ὀ. τῶν πολλῶν ὁμοιοῖ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82; τὴν τῶν πολλῶν.. συνιόντων ὀ... σοφίαν ἡγούμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 493d.}}
**II** **anger, wrath,** ὀργῇ χρῆσθαι to be in a **passion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.85, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1241; ὀργὴν ποιήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.25; ὀργὴν ποιεῖσθαι εἰ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.122; ὀργῇ χάριν δοῦναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 855}}; ὀργῇ εἶξαι, χαρίζεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 80, “fragment” 31; ὀργὴν ἔχειν τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 659 (but ὀ. ἔχει}} **involves** anger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.44); δι᾽ ὀργῆς ἔχειν τινά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.46}}; ἐν ὀργῇ ἔχειν, ποιεῖσθαί τινα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.65, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16; οὐ τίθεται ταῦτα παρ᾽ ὑμῖν εἰς.. ἣν προσῆκεν ὀ. [prev. author] 18.138; εἰς ὀργὴν πεσεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 696, etc.; ὀργῇ περιπεπτωκέναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.14}}; ἀνιέναι τῆς ὀργῆς, ὀργὴν χαλᾶν, remit **one's anger,** be pacified, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 700, “V.” 727; ὀ. κατέχειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 185; ὀργῆς κρατεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 574}}; ὀ. ἐμποιεῖν τινι make one angry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 793e}}; ὀργῆς τυγχάνειν to be visited with **anger,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.175, etc.}}; ὀργὴν ἄκρος quick **to anger,** passionate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73: in _plural_, ὀργὰς ἀφιέναι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 317; φαίνειν [prev. author] “Ch.” 326 (Lyric poetry), +others}}
**II.2** Adverbial usages, ὀργῇ **in anger. in a passion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61, 114, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 405, etc.; ὀργᾷ περιόργῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 216 (Lyric poetry); δι᾽ ὀργῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 807, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11; δι᾽ ὀργάν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 981 (Lyric poetry); ἐξ ὀργῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 766; κατ᾽ ὀργήν [prev. author] “Tr.” 933, etc.; μετ᾽ ὀργῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 34d; μετὰ τῆς ὀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.76; πρὸς ὀργήν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 369, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 844, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65}}; ὀργῆς χάριν, ὀ. ὕπο, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 688, “IA” 335.}}
**II.3** with _genitive_, Πανὸς ὀργαί **visitations of** Pan's **wrath,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 1172}}; but
**II.3.b** with _genitive_ objecti, ὀργή τινος **anger at** or **because of** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1309 (conjecture), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.20; ὀ. τῆς προδοσίας εἶχε τοὺς Ἀθηναίους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 9; ἀπύρων ἱερῶν ὀργάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 71 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.4** see at {ὀργάς} {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.—Not in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses θυμός}} instead; once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}; frequently in Eleg. and Lyric poetry and in _Ionic dialect_ and _Attic dialect_ Prose.

3710:
** ὀργ-ίζω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.2}}: _aorist_ ὤργισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus and 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (see. below): (ὀργή II)}}:— **make angry, provoke to anger, irritate,** τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 223, 404, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 267c}}; opposed to εὔνουν ποιῆσαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1415a35.}}
**II** more frequently in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 267d, etc.:}}_future_ _middle_ (in _passive_ sense) ὀργιοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.30, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 15.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.4, etc.; but ὀργισθήσομαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.20, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.111}}: _aorist_ ὠργίσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 346b, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὤργισμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1413, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 431, etc.}}: **grow angry, be wroth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 364, etc.: with}}_participle_, τίς γὰρ.. οὐκ ἂν ὀργίζοιτ᾽.. κλύων{{Refs|[prev. work] 339}}, etc.; τινι **with** a person or thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1646, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.128, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23c, +others; ὑπέρ τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.143, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.60; ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.30, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.2, etc.; ἐπί τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.183; διά τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.26}}: _absolutely_, in _participle_, ἄνθρωπος -όμενος **in a passion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.72}}; τὸ -όμενον τῆς γνώμης their **angry** feelings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.59. Cf.ὀργαίνω.}}

3712:
** ὄργυιᾰ**,
_Attic dialect_ **ὄργυᾰ**, ᾶς, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1672.9}}; _Ionic dialect_ **ὄργυιᾰ**, ῆς, ἡ (see. below): (ὀρέγω):— **the length of the outstretched arms,** about 6 **feet** or I **fathom,** ἕστηκε ξύλον.. ὅσον τ᾽ ὄργυι᾽ ὑπὲρ αἴης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.327; τοῦ μὲν ὅσον τ᾽ ὄργυιαν ἐγὼν ἀπέκοψα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.325, compare 10.167, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.19.}}
**2** more precisely, αἱ ἑκατὸν ὀργυιαὶ δίκαιαί εἰσι στάδιον ἑξάπλεθρον, ἑξαπέδου τῆς ὀργυιῆς μετρεομένης καὶ τετραπήχεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149, compare 4.41, 86, “PHal.” 1.98 (3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 669.39 (3rd c.AD).}}
**3** **rod for measuring land,** = {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|91/4 σπιθαμαὶ βασιλικαί, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Geometry texts” 4.11:—_poetry_ also **ὀρόγυια** (which see): in compounds -ωρυγ-, see at {δεκ-ώρυγος}. (Proparox. in _nominative_ and _accusative_ _singular_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus [same places]; oxytone or _perispomenon_ in other cases, cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.613, +others}}; in _Ionic dialect_ the _nominative_ and _accusative_ _singular_ end in -ᾰ, -ᾰν, as in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus [same places], the _genitive_ and _dative_ _sing._ in -ῆς, -ῇ (_accusative_ ὀργυιήν}} before consonant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 69, 196, is corrected to ὄργυιαν by Voss)}}; ὀργυιά, -άν in late Gr., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus [same place], etc.)}}

3713:
** ὀρέγω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.366, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1710 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ and later Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 497b27, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ὤρεγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.240, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.126}}: _future_ ὀρέξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.327, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 902}}: _aorist_ ὤρεξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.406, Trag. (5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 846, etc.), and sometimes in Prose, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.29}}:—_middle_ and _passive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.506, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, etc.}}: _future_ ὀρέξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 353, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 486a}} (ἐπ-): _aorist_ ὠρεξάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.99, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 16, etc.: rare in Prose, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.15}}; also ὠρέχθην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 16, “Ages.” 1.4, “Smp.” 8.35, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 7, “fragment” 187, as well as in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus (“Hel.” 1238) (not in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _perfect_ ὤρεγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὀστέων φύσιος” 18}}; _reduplicate_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὀρωρέχαται, _pluperfect_ -έχατο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.834, 11.26.}} —Cf. ὀρέγνυμι, ὀριγνάομαι:—**reach, stretch, stretch out,** χεῖρ᾽ ὀρέγων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.366; εἰς οὐρανόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.371, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.527; χεῖρας ἐμοὶ ὀρέγοντας, in entreaty, 12.257, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 36; μοι.. λεχέων ἐκ χεῖρας ὄρεξας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.743; πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.240, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 846, etc.}}; Ὅμηρον.., ἐφ᾽ ὃν πᾶσαι χεῖρ᾽ ὀρέγουσι πόλεις, to claim him, {{Refs|“APl.” 4.294.}}
**2** **reach out, hold out, hand, give,** κοτύλην καὶ πύρνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.312; δέπας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.102; ὁπποτέροισι πατὴρ Ζεὺς κῦδος ὀρέξῃ 5.33, compare 17.453, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 433; ἠέ τῳ εὖχος ὀρέξομεν, ἦέ τις ἡμῖν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.328, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1203 (Lyric poetry); ὀ. πλοῦτόν τινι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.110; τέλος ἔμπεδον [prev. author] “N.” 7.58; ὤρεξε τὴν κύλικα τῷ Σωκράτει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117b}}; later βοήθειαν ὀρέξαι τοῖς ἀδικουμένοις **extend** help, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“POxy.” 902.11 (5th c.AD).}}
**II** _middle_ and _passive_,
**II.1** _absolutely_, **stretch oneself out, stretch forth one's hand,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.53; ἀνδρὸς.. ποτὶ στόμα χεῖρ᾽ ὀρέγεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.506 (but χεῖρας ὀρεξαμένου}}**having lent a helping hand,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 448.4 (from Syria))}}; ὀρεξαμένη ἀπὸ δίφρου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 456}}; ὠρέξατο χερσὶ φίλῃσι, χειρὶ σκαιῇ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.99, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 178}}; ἔγχει ὀρεξάσθω **let him lunge with** the spear (from the chariot), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.307; πρόσθεν Ἄρης ὠρέξαθ᾽ ὑπὲρ ζυγὸν.. ἔγχεϊ χαλκείῳ 5.851}}; ποσσὶν ὀρωρέχαται πολεμίζειν, of horses, **they galloped** to the fight, {{Refs|16.834}}; ὀρέξατ᾽ ἰών **he stretched himself** as he went, i.e. **made a stride,** {{Refs|13.20}}; ὀρωρέχατο προτὶ δειρήν **were stretched out** towards the neck, {{Refs|11.26}}; of fish, **rise at the bait,** καί τις τῶν τραφερῶν ὠρέξατο [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.44; for 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1111, see at {ὄρεγμα} I.1.}}]
**II.2** with _genitive_, **reach at** or **to** a thing, **grasp at,** οὗ παιδὸς ὀρέξατο he **reached out to** his child, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.466, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.392}}; in a hostile sense, **aim at, assail, hit,** τοῦ δ᾽ ἀντίθεος Θρασυμήδης ἔφθη ὀρεξάμενος.. ὦμον **hit** him first **on** the shoulder, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.322; [prev. work] 314, a _genitive_ _person_ must be supplied, ἔφθη ὀρεξάμενος πρυμνὸν σκέλο; so in 23.805 ὁππότερός κε φθῇσιν ὀρεξάμενος χρόα καλόν}}; δηΐων ὀρέγοιτ᾽ ἐγγύθεν ἱστάμενος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.12}}; also of a suppliant, τί χρῆμα θηρῶσ᾽ ἱκέτις ὠρέχθης ἐμο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1238.}}
**II.2.b** _metaphorically_, **reach after, grasp at, yearn for,** γάμων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ion” 842; τῶν μεγίστων [prev. author] “fragment” 240; ἀπεόντων 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 202; ζωῆς [prev. author] 205}}: frequently in _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439b, 485d, etc.; ὀ. τοῦ πρῶτος ἕκαστος γίγνεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65}}: so with _infinitive_, πόλιν ὠρέξατ᾽ οἰκεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 16; ὀ. τοιοῦτος γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a; οὐδέποτε ὠρέχθην τοῖς πολλοῖς ἀρέσκειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 187}}: also, _absolutely_, **yearn, desire,** πάσῃσιν ὀρέξαιτο πραπίδεσσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.4; θυμὸς ὀρέξατο γηθοσύνῃσιν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.878; ὀρεγόμεθα κατὰ τὴν βούλευσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1113a12}}; compare ὀρεκτός, ὄρεξις.
**II.3** with _accusative_, σῖτόν τ᾽ ὄρεξαι **take** food, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 303 (variant{σίτων})}}]; αἰώρημα διὰ δέρης ὀρέξομαι I **will put** the noose on **my** neck, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 353 (Lyric poetry).}}

3714:
** ὀρεινός**, ή, όν,
(ὄρος) **mountainous, hilly,** χώρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.110, compare 2.34}}; opposed to πεδινός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.43}}; opposed to πεδιάς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 3.3.4; ὀρεινὴν οὖσαν [τὴν Ἀρκαδίαν] 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 351a3; ἡ ὀρεινή}}**hill-country,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 556a4, +others}}
**II** **of** or **from the mountains, dwelling on the mountains,** οἱ ὀ. Θρᾶκες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.96, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.11}}; of birds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 592b19}}; of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.8.3}}; τὸ ἄγριον καὶ τὸ ὀ. his wild and **mountain nature,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 394e: _metaphorically_, ὀ. ἱμάτιον, ={ἄκναπτον}, “Comica Adespota” 328.}}]
**III** in Egypt, **belonging to the edge of the desert,** especially as epithet of canals, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 61 (b). 160, +others (2nd c.BC).}}

3715:
** ὄρεξις**, εως, ἡ,
(ὀρέγω) general word for all kinds of **appetency, conation,** including ἐπιθυμία, θυμός, βούλησις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 414b2, compare 433a13, +others, “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.40, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 202, 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.16, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.78}} K.; opposed to φυγή, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 431a12}}; opposed to ἔκκλισις, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.4.1, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.7.}}
**1** with _genitive_ objecti, **longing** or **yearning after** a thing, **desire for** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 219, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 414b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1119b7, “de An.” 414b6, +others: more rarely, ὄ. ἐπί τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.48c; περί τι 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 72.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **propension, appetency,** ὄ. βουλευτική {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1113a11}}; ὄ. διανοητική{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1139b5; [ἐπιθυμίαι τινὲς] εὐδιάχυτον τὴν ὄ. ἔχουσιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 26.}}

3717:
** ὀρθός**, ή, όν,
**straight,**
**I** in height, **upright, standing,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, who commonly joins it with στῆναι, στῆ δ᾽ ὀρθός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.271, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.111, 9.22 (where it is used of a horse rearing)}}; ὀρθαὶ τρίχες ἔσταν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.359, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 540; ὀρθῶν ἑσταότων ἀγορή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.246; οἱ δ᾽ ἐν νηΐ μ᾽ ἔδησαν.. ὀρθὸν ἐν ἱστοπέδῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.179, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 239 (_anapaest meter_); κυρβασίας.. ὀρθὰς εἶχον πεπηγυίας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.64; ὀρθὸν αἴρεις κάρα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 496, etc.}}; ὀρθὸν οὖς ἵστησι pricks up his ear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 27, etc.}}; applied to the **erect** posture of man, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 653a31, +others}}; ὀ. θηρίον, of man, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 3}}; of buildings, **standing with their walls entire,** [τὸ Πάνακτον] ὀρθὸν παραδοῦναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.42; ὀρθαὶ κίονες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.267, compare “PLond.” 3.755v. 2 (4th c.AD)}}; of a **standing** crop, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1165.2 (2nd c.AD).}} _adverb_, ὀρθῶς ἑστῶτες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 689b19.}}
**I.b** Geometry texts, **at right angles to..,** εὐθεῖα πρὸς ἐπίπεδον ὀρθή ἐστιν ὅταν.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 11 “Def.” 3.}}
**I.c** Astrology texts, ὀρθὰ ζῴδια signs **which rise vertically,** opposed to πλάγια, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dorotheus Astrologus in “Cat. Codex Astr.” 5 (1). 240.}}
**II** in line, **straight** (opposed to σκολιός crooked and πλάγιος aslant), ἀντ᾽ ἠελίου τετραμμένος ὀρθός **straight, right** opposite the sun, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 727; ὀρθὸν εὐθύνοι βέλος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 200}}; ποιῶν ὀρθὰ πάντα πρὸς κανόνα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 7.3073.108 (Lebadea, 2nd c.BC)}}; ὀ. τρῶμα **longitudinal** to the muscle, opposed to ἐπικάρσιος, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.15; ὀρθὸς εἰς ὁδὸν πορεύεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1254; εἶμι.. ὀ. ὁδόν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 945; ὀ. κέλευθον ἰών 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.39}}; ὀρθὴν κελεύεις, i. e. ὀρθὴν ὁδόν με ἰέναι κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1}}; so ὀρθὴν ἄνω δίωκε (i.e. όδόν) [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 1223 (but ὀρθήν, ={εὐθύς}, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 257)}}]; δι᾽ ὀρθῆς τήνδε ναυκληρεῖς πόλιν (i.e. ὁδοῦ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 994; εἰς ὀρθὸν τρέχειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 61.5}}; εἰς ὀρθὸν ἀποδοῦναι **to face the front originally held,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.1; κατ᾽ ὀρθὸν εὐδρομεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 681}}; also ὀρθᾷ χερί **straightway,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).4}}; ὀρθῷ ποδί{{Refs|[prev. work] 13.72, “fragment” 167}}; but τιθέναι ὀρθὸν πόδα is probably **to put the foot out,** as in walking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 294 (see. κατηρεφής 1), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1166.}}
**II.2** βλέπειν ὀρθά, opposed to being blind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 419; ὀρθὸν ἀνέβλεψε}}**recovered his sight,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.966 (Rome, 2nd c.AD)}}; ἐξ ὀμμάτων ὀ.. κἀξ ὀρθῆς φρενός [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 528; ὀρθοῖς ὄμμασιν [prev. work] 1385; see at {ὄμμα} 1.}}]
**III** _metaphorically_,
**III.1** **right, safe, prosperous**:
**III.1.a** partly from _signification_{{Refs|1, τοὺς δὲ τομαῖς ἔστασεν ὀρθούς}}**set them up, restored,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.53}}; so ὀρθὸν ἀστάσας (= ἀναστήσας) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.52 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; ἐς ὀρθὸν ἱστάναι τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1230; ὀρθὰν φυλάσσειν Τένεδον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.5; so στάντες τ᾽ ἐς ὀρθὸν καὶ πεσόντες ὕστερον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 181b}}; ταύτης ἔπι (i.e. χθονός) πλέοντες ὀρθῆς (the state being represented as a ship) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 190; ἐν ὀρθῷ κεῖσθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.7.1.}}
**III.1.b** partly from _signification_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11, κατ᾽ ὀρθὸν ἐξελθεῖν, of prophecies, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 88, compare “OC” 1424}}; κατ᾽ ὀρθὸν οὐρίσαι to speed **in prosperous course,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 695 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.2** **right, true, correct,** ἄγγελος, ἀγγελία, νόος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, “P.” 4.279, 10.68; μάρτυρες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 318 (_anapaest meter_), etc.; γλῶσσα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 351; ὀρθᾷ φρενί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.24}}; ὄρθ᾽ ἀκούειν to be **rightly, truly** called, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 903 (Lyric poetry); κατὰ τὸ ὀ. δικάζειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96; ὀ. λόγῳ}}**strictly speaking, in very truth,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.17, 6.68, etc.}}: so in _adverb_, ὀρθῶς λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.51; ὀ. ἔλεξας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 341; ὀ. φράσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 526; εἴρηκας ὀ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1040; ὀ. φρονεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1000, 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1 (so εἰς ὀρθὸν φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 612); ὀ. γνῶναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.8}}; ὀ. ἔχει it is **right,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 9a; ὀ. ἐνδίκως τ᾽ ἐπώνυμον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 405, compare 829 (_anapaest meter_)}}: in answers, **rightly, exactly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 359e; ὀ. γε 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.18}}: _superlative_, ὀρθότατα καλεόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.59; so τὸ ὀρθὸν ἐξείρηκα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 374}}; φωνεῖν δίκης ἐς ὀρθόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 347; κατ᾽ ὀρθόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 44b.}}
**III.3** **true, real, genuine,** ὀ. πολιτεῖαι, opposed to παρεκβάσεις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1279a18, etc.}}; ὀ. μανία **real** madness, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.32, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.11.}} _adverb_ -θῶς **really, truly,** τοὺς ὀ. φιλομαθεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67b; ὁ ὀ. κυβερνήτης [prev. author] “R.” 341c; τὸν ὀ. συγγενῆ 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 102.}}
**III.4** **upright, just,** ἐμμένειν ὀ. νόμῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 350 (Lyric poetry); τὸ ὀ.}}**uprightness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 540d}}; ἐπιστήμη ἐνοῦσα καὶ ὀ. λόγος (see. λόγος IV. I) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 73a}}; ὁ ὀ. λόγος διὰ πάντων ἐρχόμενος (see. λόγος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|111.7) 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.4}}; ὀ. λόγοι **virtues** on the intellectual side, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 8}}. _adverb_ ὀρθῶς **rightly, justly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.56; ὀ. καὶ δικαίως 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.10, “IG” 22.228.14 (4th c.BC), “IPE” 12.32 B 73 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ὀ. καὶ νομίμως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.28.}}
**III.5** of persons, **'straight', straightforward,** σμικροὶ καὶ οὐκ ὀρθοὶ τὰς ψυχάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173a.}}
**III.6** **on tiptoe, full of expectation, excited,** ὀρθῆς τῆς πόλεως γενομένης διά τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.7; τὴν Ἑλλάδα ὀρθὴν οὖσαν ἐπί τινι [prev. author] 5.70; ὀ. ἦν ἡ πόλις ἐπὶ τοῖς συμβεβηκόσιν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 39, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 39; ὀ. καὶ μετέωροι ταῖς διανοίαις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.17.11; ὀ. καὶ περίφοβος ἦν ἡ πόλις [prev. author] 3.112.6; ὀ. διὰ τὸν φόβον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.84; ὀ. καὶ δραστήριος διὰ τὸ θαρρεῖν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 12.}}
**IV** ἡ ὀρθή,
**IV.1** (i.e. ὁδός) see above {{Refs|11.1.}}
**IV.2** ὀ. γωνία **right** angle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 55b}}; so ὀ. alone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1098a30, +others; compare ὄρθιος see 1: τέμνειν πρὸς ὀρθάς}} to cut **at right angles,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 3.3, +others}}; εἴ τις δείξειεν ὅτι αἱ ὀρθαὶ οὐ συμπίπτουσι.. that **right angles** do not meet (short for 'that two straight lines making, with a third, interior angles equal to two right angles, etc.'), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.74a13}}; τὸ δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς **the theorem that the angles of a triangle are together equal to two right angles,** {{Refs|[prev. work]85b5}}; ὀρθὸς κῶνος, κύλινδρος, a **right** cone, cylinder, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.26, 1.11.}}
**IV.3** (with or without πτῶσις) **nominative,** Latin _casus rectus,_ opposed to the oblique cases, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 636.3, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.28, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 39.10, +others, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.177.}}
**V** ὀρθά **active verbs,** opposed to ὕπτια (passive) and οὐδέτερα (neuter), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.59.}}
**VI** ὀ. τόνος **real** or **unmodified** (compare above [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|111.3) accent, opposed to ἐγκλινόμενος, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 36.10, +others; so ὀρθὴ τάσις [prev. work] 54.8, +others (The gloss of 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, βορθ-αγορίσκοι, ={ὀ}., and the dialect forms of Ὀρθεία (which see), suggest that the word originally had ϝ.)}}]

3721:
** ὄρθριος**, α, ον,
(ὄρθρος) **at daybreak, in the morning, early,** mostly with Verbs of motion, so as to agree with the person, ἀφίκετο.. ὄρθριος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 143; ὀρθρίη αὖθις ἔσειμι 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 863}}; ὄρθριος παρεῖναι, ἥκειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 283, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313b; ἤλουν ὄρθριαι τὰ σιτία 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 10.}}
**2** generally, **of the morning,** πόλτος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 23}}; διὰ τὸν ὄ. νόμον the **morning** song, as parody of ὄρθιον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 741}}; ὄρθριον ᾆσαι (i.e. ᾆσμα), of the cock, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 489; δεῖ ὄρθριον εἶναι τὸν σύλλογον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 961b}}; τὸ ὄ. as _adverb_, **in the morning, early,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 1; or ὄρθριον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 377, 526: irregular _comparative_ and _superlative_ ὀρθριαίτερος, -αίτατος, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Epim.” 166}}; also ὀρθρίτερον as _adverb_, **earlier**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 62.19 (2nd c.BC), “BGU” 1201.4 (1st c.AD).}}

3722:
** ὄρθρος**, ὁ,
**the time just before** or **about daybreak, dawn, cock-crow** (ἀπ᾽ ὄρθρου μέχρι περ ἂν ἥλιος ἀνάσχῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 951d), τάχα δ᾽ ὄ. ἐγίγνετο δημιοεργός “hymnus ad Mercurium” 98; ἐπειδὰν ὄ. ᾖ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 256, compare “Av.” 496, etc.; ὄρθρου}}**at dawn,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 577, 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 25, 4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 10; ὄρθρου γενομένου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.198; ἅμα ὄρθρῳ [prev. author] 7.188, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.112, etc.; ἐς ὄρθρον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.56, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.6; κατ᾽ ὄρθρον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 772; περὶ ὄρθρον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.101 (compare περίορθρος)}}; πρὸς ὄρθρον towards **dawn,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1089; πρὸς ὄρθρον γ᾽ ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ec.” 20; ὑπ᾽ ὄρθρον Batrachomyomachia 103; ὑπὸ τὸν ὄ. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 76.17}}; τὸν ὄ., _absolutely_, **in the morning,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.181}}; δι᾽ ὄρθρων each **morning early,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 909}}; ὄ. βαθύς dim **morning twilight,** ἀλλὰ νῦν ὄ. β. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 216, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43a, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.14; τῆς παρελθούσης νυκτὸς.., ἔτι βαθέος ὄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310a, cf. NT.Luke.24.1.}}
**II** **Ὄρθρος**, ὁ, a mythical dog, son of Typhaon and Echidna, that kept the herds of Geryoneus on the island Erytheia, and was there killed by Heracles, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 309, compare 293 (variant{Ὄρθος}).}}]

3723:
Included with: ** ὀρθός**, ή, όν,
**straight,**
**I** in height, **upright, standing,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, who commonly joins it with στῆναι, στῆ δ᾽ ὀρθός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.271, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.111, 9.22 (where it is used of a horse rearing)}}; ὀρθαὶ τρίχες ἔσταν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.359, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 540; ὀρθῶν ἑσταότων ἀγορή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.246; οἱ δ᾽ ἐν νηΐ μ᾽ ἔδησαν.. ὀρθὸν ἐν ἱστοπέδῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.179, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 239 (_anapaest meter_); κυρβασίας.. ὀρθὰς εἶχον πεπηγυίας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.64; ὀρθὸν αἴρεις κάρα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 496, etc.}}; ὀρθὸν οὖς ἵστησι pricks up his ear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 27, etc.}}; applied to the **erect** posture of man, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 653a31, +others}}; ὀ. θηρίον, of man, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 3}}; of buildings, **standing with their walls entire,** [τὸ Πάνακτον] ὀρθὸν παραδοῦναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.42; ὀρθαὶ κίονες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.267, compare “PLond.” 3.755v. 2 (4th c.AD)}}; of a **standing** crop, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1165.2 (2nd c.AD).}} _adverb_, ὀρθῶς ἑστῶτες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 689b19.}}
**I.b** Geometry texts, **at right angles to..,** εὐθεῖα πρὸς ἐπίπεδον ὀρθή ἐστιν ὅταν.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 11 “Def.” 3.}}
**I.c** Astrology texts, ὀρθὰ ζῴδια signs **which rise vertically,** opposed to πλάγια, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dorotheus Astrologus in “Cat. Codex Astr.” 5 (1). 240.}}
**II** in line, **straight** (opposed to σκολιός crooked and πλάγιος aslant), ἀντ᾽ ἠελίου τετραμμένος ὀρθός **straight, right** opposite the sun, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 727; ὀρθὸν εὐθύνοι βέλος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 200}}; ποιῶν ὀρθὰ πάντα πρὸς κανόνα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 7.3073.108 (Lebadea, 2nd c.BC)}}; ὀ. τρῶμα **longitudinal** to the muscle, opposed to ἐπικάρσιος, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.15; ὀρθὸς εἰς ὁδὸν πορεύεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1254; εἶμι.. ὀ. ὁδόν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 945; ὀ. κέλευθον ἰών 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.39}}; ὀρθὴν κελεύεις, i. e. ὀρθὴν ὁδόν με ἰέναι κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1}}; so ὀρθὴν ἄνω δίωκε (i.e. όδόν) [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 1223 (but ὀρθήν, ={εὐθύς}, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 257)}}]; δι᾽ ὀρθῆς τήνδε ναυκληρεῖς πόλιν (i.e. ὁδοῦ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 994; εἰς ὀρθὸν τρέχειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 61.5}}; εἰς ὀρθὸν ἀποδοῦναι **to face the front originally held,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.1; κατ᾽ ὀρθὸν εὐδρομεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 681}}; also ὀρθᾷ χερί **straightway,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).4}}; ὀρθῷ ποδί{{Refs|[prev. work] 13.72, “fragment” 167}}; but τιθέναι ὀρθὸν πόδα is probably **to put the foot out,** as in walking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 294 (see. κατηρεφής 1), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1166.}}
**II.2** βλέπειν ὀρθά, opposed to being blind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 419; ὀρθὸν ἀνέβλεψε}}**recovered his sight,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.966 (Rome, 2nd c.AD)}}; ἐξ ὀμμάτων ὀ.. κἀξ ὀρθῆς φρενός [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 528; ὀρθοῖς ὄμμασιν [prev. work] 1385; see at {ὄμμα} 1.}}]
**III** _metaphorically_,
**III.1** **right, safe, prosperous**:
**III.1.a** partly from _signification_{{Refs|1, τοὺς δὲ τομαῖς ἔστασεν ὀρθούς}}**set them up, restored,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.53}}; so ὀρθὸν ἀστάσας (= ἀναστήσας) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.52 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; ἐς ὀρθὸν ἱστάναι τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1230; ὀρθὰν φυλάσσειν Τένεδον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.5; so στάντες τ᾽ ἐς ὀρθὸν καὶ πεσόντες ὕστερον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 181b}}; ταύτης ἔπι (i.e. χθονός) πλέοντες ὀρθῆς (the state being represented as a ship) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 190; ἐν ὀρθῷ κεῖσθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.7.1.}}
**III.1.b** partly from _signification_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11, κατ᾽ ὀρθὸν ἐξελθεῖν, of prophecies, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 88, compare “OC” 1424}}; κατ᾽ ὀρθὸν οὐρίσαι to speed **in prosperous course,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 695 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.2** **right, true, correct,** ἄγγελος, ἀγγελία, νόος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, “P.” 4.279, 10.68; μάρτυρες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 318 (_anapaest meter_), etc.; γλῶσσα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 351; ὀρθᾷ φρενί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.24}}; ὄρθ᾽ ἀκούειν to be **rightly, truly** called, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 903 (Lyric poetry); κατὰ τὸ ὀ. δικάζειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96; ὀ. λόγῳ}}**strictly speaking, in very truth,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.17, 6.68, etc.}}: so in _adverb_, ὀρθῶς λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.51; ὀ. ἔλεξας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 341; ὀ. φράσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 526; εἴρηκας ὀ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1040; ὀ. φρονεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1000, 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1 (so εἰς ὀρθὸν φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 612); ὀ. γνῶναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.8}}; ὀ. ἔχει it is **right,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 9a; ὀ. ἐνδίκως τ᾽ ἐπώνυμον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 405, compare 829 (_anapaest meter_)}}: in answers, **rightly, exactly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 359e; ὀ. γε 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.18}}: _superlative_, ὀρθότατα καλεόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.59; so τὸ ὀρθὸν ἐξείρηκα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 374}}; φωνεῖν δίκης ἐς ὀρθόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 347; κατ᾽ ὀρθόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 44b.}}
**III.3** **true, real, genuine,** ὀ. πολιτεῖαι, opposed to παρεκβάσεις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1279a18, etc.}}; ὀ. μανία **real** madness, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.32, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.11.}} _adverb_ -θῶς **really, truly,** τοὺς ὀ. φιλομαθεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67b; ὁ ὀ. κυβερνήτης [prev. author] “R.” 341c; τὸν ὀ. συγγενῆ 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 102.}}
**III.4** **upright, just,** ἐμμένειν ὀ. νόμῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 350 (Lyric poetry); τὸ ὀ.}}**uprightness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 540d}}; ἐπιστήμη ἐνοῦσα καὶ ὀ. λόγος (see. λόγος IV. I) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 73a}}; ὁ ὀ. λόγος διὰ πάντων ἐρχόμενος (see. λόγος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|111.7) 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.4}}; ὀ. λόγοι **virtues** on the intellectual side, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 8}}. _adverb_ ὀρθῶς **rightly, justly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.56; ὀ. καὶ δικαίως 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.10, “IG” 22.228.14 (4th c.BC), “IPE” 12.32 B 73 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ὀ. καὶ νομίμως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.28.}}
**III.5** of persons, **'straight', straightforward,** σμικροὶ καὶ οὐκ ὀρθοὶ τὰς ψυχάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173a.}}
**III.6** **on tiptoe, full of expectation, excited,** ὀρθῆς τῆς πόλεως γενομένης διά τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.7; τὴν Ἑλλάδα ὀρθὴν οὖσαν ἐπί τινι [prev. author] 5.70; ὀ. ἦν ἡ πόλις ἐπὶ τοῖς συμβεβηκόσιν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 39, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 39; ὀ. καὶ μετέωροι ταῖς διανοίαις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.17.11; ὀ. καὶ περίφοβος ἦν ἡ πόλις [prev. author] 3.112.6; ὀ. διὰ τὸν φόβον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.84; ὀ. καὶ δραστήριος διὰ τὸ θαρρεῖν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 12.}}
**IV** ἡ ὀρθή,
**IV.1** (i.e. ὁδός) see above {{Refs|11.1.}}
**IV.2** ὀ. γωνία **right** angle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 55b}}; so ὀ. alone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1098a30, +others; compare ὄρθιος see 1: τέμνειν πρὸς ὀρθάς}} to cut **at right angles,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 3.3, +others}}; εἴ τις δείξειεν ὅτι αἱ ὀρθαὶ οὐ συμπίπτουσι.. that **right angles** do not meet (short for 'that two straight lines making, with a third, interior angles equal to two right angles, etc.'), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.74a13}}; τὸ δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς **the theorem that the angles of a triangle are together equal to two right angles,** {{Refs|[prev. work]85b5}}; ὀρθὸς κῶνος, κύλινδρος, a **right** cone, cylinder, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.26, 1.11.}}
**IV.3** (with or without πτῶσις) **nominative,** Latin _casus rectus,_ opposed to the oblique cases, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 636.3, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.28, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 39.10, +others, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.177.}}
**V** ὀρθά **active verbs,** opposed to ὕπτια (passive) and οὐδέτερα (neuter), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.59.}}
**VI** ὀ. τόνος **real** or **unmodified** (compare above [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|111.3) accent, opposed to ἐγκλινόμενος, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 36.10, +others; so ὀρθὴ τάσις [prev. work] 54.8, +others (The gloss of 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, βορθ-αγορίσκοι, ={ὀ}., and the dialect forms of Ὀρθεία (which see), suggest that the word originally had ϝ.)}}]

3724:
** ὁρίζω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **οὐρ-** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below)}}: _future_ ὁριῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 5b5, (δι-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.174}}: _aorist_ ὥρισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 452, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864e}}; _Ionic dialect_ οὔρισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142}}: _perfect_ ὥρικα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.24, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 382a19}}:—_middle_, _future_ **-ιοῦμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 190e, “Lg.” 737d}}: _aorist_ ὡρισάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 148a, 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.18, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ ὁρισθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158d}}: _aorist_ ὡρίσθην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 171a}}: _perfect_ ὥρισμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182a, etc.}}; but in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 801, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 31.5}}: (ὅρος):—**divide** or **separate from, as a border** or **boundary,** c._accusative_ et _dative_, ὁ Νεῖλος ὁ τὴν Ἀσίην οὐρίζων τῇ Λιβύῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.16: with _accusative_ et _genitive_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 636}}:—_passive_, θύρᾳ βαλανωτῇ ὡρισμένην ἀπὸ τῆς ἀνδρωνίτιδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.5}}; or
**b** with two _accusatives_ joined by καί, **separate,** [λίμνη] οὐρίζει τήν τε Σκυθικὴν καὶ τὴν Νευρίδα γῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.51, compare 56, 7.123, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 501b16, “OGI” 335.112 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1289, etc.; ἐὰν.. κύκλος.. ὁρίζῃ τό τε ἀφανὲς καὶ τὸ φανερὸν ἡμισφαίριον 4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus “Sph.” 4: hence ὁρίζων κύκλος [prev. author] 1.1}}; see at {ὁρίζων}.
**c** **delimit,** χρὴ τὸν νόμον ὁρίζειν πειρᾶσθαι κατὰ μέρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 944a.}}
**2** **bound,** τὴν ἀρχὴν ὥριζεν αὐτῷ ἡ Ἐρυθρὰ θάλαττα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.21; τὰ δὲ πρὸς Τριβαλλοὺς.. Τρῆρες ὥριζον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.96}}; of a line (or surface) as **limiting** a surface (or solid), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1017b17}}:—_passive_, Εὔβοια.. ὅροις ὑγροῖσιν ὡρισμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 295: _metaphorically_, ὡρίσθω μέχρι τοῦδε}} so far **let it go and no further,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71.}}
**3** **pass between** or **through,** διδύμους πέτρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 433 (Lyric poetry).}}
**4** **part,** **divide,** χειμὼν ἄλλοσ᾽ ἄλλον ὥρισεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 128}}; ὁ. τινὰ ἀπὸ.. **banish** one **from..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 941 (Lyric poetry):}} —_passive_, ματρὸς ἐκ χερῶν ὁ. **depart** from.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 1459 (Lyric poetry)}}, but very uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 202; compare ἐξορίζω (A) 11, 111.}}
**II** **mark out by boundaries, mark out,** βωμὸν ἱδρύσατο καὶ τέμενος περὶ αὐτὸν οὔρισε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142, compare 6.108, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 754, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1670, “IG” 12.76.54, 42(1).76.19 (_passive_, Epid., 2nd c.BC), etc.}}; see infr. IV. 1: _metaphorically_, ὁ. τι ἔς τι **limit** one thing according to another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82.}}
**II.2** **trace out as a boundary,** πόρον (of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|10 tracing out the Bosporus), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 546 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** **ordain, determine, lay down,** αἶσα τόνδε σοὐρίζει (i.e. σοι ὁρίζει) μόρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 927 (σοι πορίζει M 1, σ᾽ ὁ. M 2); ἡμῖν ὥρισεν σωτηρίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 979; ἐς τήνδε παῖδα ψῆφον ὥρισαν φόνου [prev. author] “Hec.” 259; ἡ Δίκη.. ἐν ἀνθρώποισιν ὥρισεν νόμους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 452; [τὸν χρόνον] ὁ νόμος ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864e; ἀριθμὸς ὁ ὁρίζων τὸ πολὺ καὶ τὸ ὀλίγον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.36; τὸ δοῦλον γένος πρὸς τὴν ἐλάσσω μοῖραν ὥρισεν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 218; ὁρίσατέ μοι μέχρι πόσων ἐτῶν δεῖ νομίζειν νέους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.35}}: with _infinitive_, ἄνακτες ὥρισαν.. θανεῖν ἐμὴν δέσποιναν οὐ ψήφῳ μιᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1222, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 24}}; ὁ. τινὰ θεόν **determine** one **to be** a god, **deify,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 12.158.7 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); ὁ. θάνατον εἶναι τὴν ζημίαν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 65, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.61 (_middle_)}}; θάνατον ὡρικέναι τὴν ζημίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.24}}:—_passive_, ὧραι ἑκάστοις εἰσὶν ὡρισμέναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 542a19, etc.}}; ἐπί τισι ὡρισμένοις on certain **definite terms** (compare ῥητός), {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1285b22}}; ἀρχαὶ ἀριθμῷ ὡρισμέναι **limited, definite,** opposed to ἄπειροι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1002b18; τόποι ὡ. [prev. author] “Cael.” 273 a14; τὸ ὡρισμένον [prev. author] “Mete.” 369b29.}}
**III.2** **define** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 171a (_passive_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.6.4, +others: more frequently in}}_middle_ than _active_,{{Refs|see infr. IV. 3.}}
**IV** _middle_, **mark out for oneself,** τίνα ὅρον ὁρίζῃ what **criterion** do you **assign,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 470b}}; στήλας ὁ. **set up** stones **as boundary marks,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.5.13; ὁ. χθόνα}}**take possession of, take to oneself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 256; γαῖα.. ἣν Πέλοψ ὁρίζεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 696; ὁ. ἑαυτῷ μέρος τῆς οὐσίας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.6}}: with _infinitive_ added, ἱερὸν ὡρίσαντ᾽ ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 969; ὁρίζεσθαι βωμούς}}**set up,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 237 (just like{ὁρίζειν} [prev. work] 754)}}]; see at {ὕπαστρος}.
**IV.2** **determine for oneself, get** or **have** a thing **determined,** ἃ ὡρίσω σὺ δίκαια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.241, compare variant in 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.19: with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, αὐτὸν πολεμεῖν ὁρίζομαι I **lay it down** that.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.19}}; τί ποτ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ὡρίσαντο καὶ τίνος γένους εἶναι τὸ φυτό; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.18.}}
**IV.3** **define** a thing, τὴν ἡδονὴν ἀγαθὸν ὁ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 505c, compare “Sph.” 246b; ὁ. τὰς ἀρετὰς ἀπαθείας τινάς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1104b24, +others; ἡδονῇ τε καὶ ἀγαθῷ ὁ. τὸ καλόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 475a; τὸ ζῆν ὁ. δυνάμει αἰσθήσεως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1170a16, +others}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ὁ. δικαίους εἶναι τοὺς εἰδότας κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.6.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 190e, etc.}}:— _passive_, **to be defined,** [ἡ αἰδὼς] ὁρίζεται φόβος τις ἀδοξίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1128b11}}; οἷς αἱ φιλίαι ὁρίζονται{{Refs|[prev. work] 1166a2; τὸ ὁριζόμενον [prev. author] “Top.” 141b24, +others}}
**V** _intransitive_, **border upon,** πλὴν ὅσον αὐτῆς πρὸς τὴν Ἀσίην οὐρίζει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42.}}
**VI** as _Attic dialect_ law-term, δισχιλίων ὡρισμένος τὴν οἰκίαν **having** the house **marked with** ὅροι (compare ὅρος II) to secure a claim on it for {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2, 000 drachmas, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 31.5; so χωρίον ὡρισμένον 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.9.}}

3725:
** ὅριον**, τό,
={ὅρος}, _diminutive_ only in form, **boundary, limit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 3, “Schwyzer” 664.4 (Orchom. 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 4th c.BC), “epigram Gr.” 978.12 (from Philae), “POxy.” 2134.18 (2nd c.AD)}}: mostly in _plural_, **boundaries, bounds, frontier,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 375, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.230, “PCair.Zen.” 251.3 (3rd c.BC), “Supp.epigram” 3.378 B 11 (Delph., 2nd-1st c.BC)}}; ἐπὶ τοῖς ὁ. on **the frontier,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.45}}; ὅρια κελεύθου **limits** of a road, i.e. the road itself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 721 (uncertain); μὴ κινείτω γῆς ὅ. μηδείς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 842e}}; **territories,** {{LXX|LXX.Exo.10.4, +others}}
**2** Astrology texts, a subdivision of a zodiacal sign, appropriated to a planet, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 43, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.37, “PMag.Lond.” 46.48, “PTeb.” 277.15, 4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.1, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.166, 4.265.}}
**II** _plural_, **rules,** e.g. for the use of wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὑγρῶν χρήσιος” 5.}}
**III** ὅριον· τείχισμα, φραγμόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; so ὅρια καὶ σταυρώματα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.74 (restored from Scholia).}}

3727:
** ὅρκος**, ὁ,
**the object by which one swears,** as the Styx among the gods, Στυγὸς ὕδωρ, ὅς τε μέγιστος ὅ. δεινότατός τε πέλει μακάρεσσι θεοῖσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.38, compare 2.755, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 400, 784, 805, “hymnus ad Cererem” 259, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 983b31}}; or as Zeus among mortals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.167}}; so of things, ὅρκον δ᾽ ἐνοσφίσθης μέγαν, ἅλας τε καὶ τράπεζαν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 96; οἷς ἦν μέγιστος ὅ... κύων, ἔπειτα χήν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 231, compare “Placita Philosophorum” 1.3.8: hence}}
**2** **oath,** mostly with epithet μέγας, καρτερός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below)}}, etc.; θεῶν ὅ. **an oath by** the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.377; μακάρων ὅ. 10.299, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 647, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 657; ὅ. ἐκ θεῶν μέγας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1284; ὅ. κατὰ τῶν.. ὀφθαλμῶν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.153}}; ὅ. πλατύς **a** firm-based **oath,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 30.3}}; ὅρκον ὀμόσαι swear **an oath,** ὄμοσέν τε τελεύτησέν τε τὸν ὅ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.378, etc.; ὅ. ἀπώμνυ [prev. work] 377, compare 10.381; ἐπὶ δ᾽ ὅρκον ὀμεῖται 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 194; κατομόσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 790}}; ὅ. ἐπιορκῆσαι take a false **oath,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.115, etc.}}; ὅρκου προστεθέντος when **an oath** is added, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 472, compare “El.” 47}}; δαίμονι τῷ Πλεισθενιδῶν ὅρκους θεμένη having made a **sworn compact** with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1570 (_anapaest meter_); ὅ. ἀλλήλοις ποιοῦνται οἱ μὲν ἔφοροι ὑπὲρ τῆς πόλεως, βασιλεὺς δ᾽ ὑπὲρ ἑαυτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 15.7; ὅρκους συνῆψαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1241, etc.}}; of the person demanding the oath, ὅ. ἑλέσθαι τινός or τινί take **it** of him, i.e. make him swear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.746, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.119}}; ὅρκους ἐπελάσαι and προσάγειν τινί lay **oath** upon a man, put him on **his oath,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.146, 6.62, 74}}; τὸν ὅ... ἐπάγειν.. Ὀποντίοις readminister **the oath,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(1).334.12 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}; ὅρκους δοὺς καὶ δεξάμενος after tendering **his oath** to them and accepting theirs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.23, compare “IG” 12.52.18, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 429, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 589, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.3 and 4; so ὅρκον διδόναι καὶ λαμβάνειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1377a7, 8}}; ἀποδοῦναι take **it** oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.318, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.74}}; ἀπολαμβάνειν administer or tender **it,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.9, 18.25}}; ὅρκους καὶ πίστιν ἀλλήλοις δότε **swear to** one another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1185, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.107}}; ὅρκοις καταλαβὼν τὰ τέλη having bound the authorities **by oaths,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86; ὅρκοις κατειλημμένους [prev. author] 1.9}}; ὅρκῳ ἐμμένειν abide by it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 754; ὅ. τηρεῖν 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 239; παραβαίνειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 286.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 332, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.318; ἐκβάντι τῶν ὅ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 183b; ἐκλιπεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1194; συγχέαι [prev. author] “Hipp.” 1063; ἐμπεδοῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.10: after ὅρκος}} _aorist_, _present_, or _future_ _infinitive_ may refer to _future_ time, ὤμοσα καρτερὸν ὅ., μὴ.. ἀναφῆναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.253}}; ἐμεῦ δ᾽ ἕλετο μέγαν ὅ., μὴ πρὶν σοὶ ἐρέειν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 746; ὅρκους ἔδοσαν καὶ ἔλαβον, ἀποδοῦναι.., Ἀθηναίους δὲ μὴ πολεμεῖν.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.3.9: with Preps., οὐκ αὔτως.., ἀλλὰ σὺν ὅρκῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.151; σὺν θεῶν ὅρκῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.12}}; εἶπαι ἐπ᾽ ὅρκου say on **oath,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.11; κατὰ τοὺς ὅ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.54; opposed to παρ᾽ ὅρκον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.83; παρὰ τοὺς ὅ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.41: proverbial, ὅρκους ἐγὼ γυναικὸς εἰς ὕδωρ γράφω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 811; parodied by 5th c.BC: Philonides Comicus 7 ὅρκους δὲ μοιχῶν εἰς τέφραν.. γράφω,compare 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 25.}}
**II** Ὅρκος, personified, son of Eris, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 804}}; a divinity who punishes the false and perjured,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 219, “Th.” 231, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86.γ}}; Διὸς Ὅ., as servant of Zeus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1767 (_anapaest meter_). (Cogn. with ἕρκος.)}}

3729:
** ὁρμάω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 875b}}: _aorist_ ὥρμησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.338, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 534c}}; _Laconian dialect_ _imperative_ ὅρμᾱον, i.e. ὅρμαὁν, ={ὅρμησον}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1247}}: _perfect_ ὥρμηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 265a}}:—_middle_ and _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.5, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 339, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17, etc.}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ὡρμᾶτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.142}}: _future_ ὁρμήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.9, ὁρμηθήσομαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.85}}: _aorist_ ὡρμησάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.595, variant in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 127}} (ἐφ-), never in Prose, except ἐξ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.20 codices: more frequently in _passive_ form ὡρμήθην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.12, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.98, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὥρμημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 70, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 340, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33, etc.: _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_}}_perfect_ and _pluperfect_ ὁρμέαται and -έατο (with variants ὡρμ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.121, 8.35; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} codices usually have the augment, but {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus read ὁρμήθησαν in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.359}}: (ὁρμή):
**A** _active_,
**I** causal, **set in motion, urge on, cheer on,** τινὰ εἰς πόλεμον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.338, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.127; τινὰ ποτὶ κλέος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).21; τὸ στράτευμα ὁ. ἐπὶ τὰς Ἀθήνας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 174 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 352 (_anapaest meter_); ἡ φύσις ὁρμήσει τινὰ ἐπὶ πλεονεξίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 875b, compare “Ion” 534c; [τὰ] ὁρμῶντα [σώματα] 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.8.7; μέριμναν ὁρμήσασ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἔργον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1064 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ. τινὰ ἐκ χερός **tear** from one's arms, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 143 (_anapaest meter_):}} —_passive_, ὁρμηθεὶς θεοῦ ἄρχετο **inspired** by the god he began, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.499; πρὸς θεῶν ὡρμημένος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 70; ὑπὸ ἔρωτος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 181d}}; ἵπποι.. ὁρμηθέντες ὑπὸ πληγῇσιν ἱμάσθλης **urged on** by.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.82.}}
**I.2** with a thing as the object, **stir up,** πόλεμον {{Refs|18.376}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, τὰς διόδους τῶν πτερῶν.. ὥρμησε πτεροφυεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255d}}:—_passive_, ὡρμάθη πλαγά **was sped,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 196 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** more frequently _intransitive_, **start,**
**II.1** with _infinitive_, ἴρηξ ὃς ὁρμήσῃ διώκειν ὄρνεον ἄλλο **starts** in chase of.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.64}}; ὁσσάκι δ᾽ ὁρμήσειε πυλάων.. ἀντίον ἀΐξασθαι whenever he **started** to rush for the gates,{{Refs|22.194; ὁσσάκι δ᾽ ὁρμήσειε.. στῆναι ἐναντίβιον 21.265}}; ἐξελαύνειν ὁρμῆσαι τὸν στρατόν **began** to lead out.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.76, compare 7.150; νίκην ὁρμῶντ᾽ ἀλαλάξαι}}**eager to..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 133 (Lyric poetry); ὥρμα ἀντιλαμβάνεσθαι τοῦ λόγου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336b.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_, **rush headlong at** one, Τρώων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.335: more frequently with Preps., ὁ. ἐπί τινα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 403, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, etc.; πύργωμα Καδμείων ἔπι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1220; εἴς τινας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.17; καθ᾽ αὑτούς [prev. author] “An.” 5.7.25}}; also ὁ. ἐς μάχην **hasten** to battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 394; εἰς ἀγῶνα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 259 (Lyric poetry); εἰς τὸ διώκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.25; ἐπὶ ἁρπαγάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 391d; ἐπὶ τοὺς Ἀθηναίους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.34}}; ὥρμασε (_Doric dialect_) ἐπὶ τὰ βασίλεια τῶν Σκυθᾶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 709.19 (Chersonesus, 2nd c.BC)}}: without any sense of hostility, **rush,** τᾶσδ᾽ ἀπὸ πέτρας πηδήσασα πυρὸς ἔσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1015 (Lyric poetry); ἐς πατρὸς δόμους [prev. author] “Med.” 1178}}; **set out,** ἀπὸ [τῆς Οἰνόης] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.19; ἐς φυγήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.179, etc.; εἰς τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκεῖνα τῆς γῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 112b; ἐπ᾽ ἄλλον λόγον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.5; ἐπὶ τὸ σκοπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.7.9}}; ἐπὶ τραγῳδίαν ὥρμηκε **has turned** to tragedy, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 135.14}}; δηλώσεις.. τὴν φύσιν ἐπὶ τί μάλισθ᾽ ὥρμηκε, i. e. what your natural **bent** is,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 8; φυσικῶς ἐπὶ τὴν ὀργὴν ὁρμᾶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” 93 W.; πρὸς τὰς πράξεις [prev. author] “Mus.” p.71 K.; ἐπὶ φιλοσοφίαν [prev. author] “Acad.Ind.” p.64 M.; πρὸς τὰς ὀχείας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 546a15: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ὁδόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.8; στρατείαν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.6.20.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **start, begin,** ὥσπερ ὡρμήσαμεν, ἴωμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314b, compare “R.” 425c}}; αἱ μάλιστα ὁρμήσασαι [νῆες] the ships **that were** hottest **in pursuit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.34.}}
**B** _middle_ and _passive_, like the _intransitive_ _active_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus II:}}
**B.1** with _infinitive_, μὴ φεύγειν ὁρμήσωνται **that they put** not **themselves in motion, set** not **themselves** to flee, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.511; so διώκειν ὁρμήθησαν 10.359, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.282}}; ὡρμήθη κόρυθα κρατὸς ἀφαρπάξαι **he rushed** to snatch.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.188, compare 182}}; ἦτορ ὡρμᾶτο πτολεμίζειν ἠδὲ μάχεσθαι **was eager** to.., {{Refs|21.572}}; μᾶλλον ὅρμητο στρατεύεσθαι **was eager** to march, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.1, compare 19, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45}}; ὅδε ὁ λόγος ὅρμηται λέγεσθαι this account **has begun** to be given, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 6.86.δ᾽ (λέγεσθαι is restored for λέγεται in 3.56)}}; but λόγον, τὸν ὅρμητο λέγειν which **he purposed** to make, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.50.}}
**B.2** the object **for** or **after** which one goes is sometimes in _genitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.488, 21.595}}: a case with a _preposition_, ὡρμήθησαν ἐπ᾽ ἀνδράσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.214; ἐπί τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 47, etc.; εἴς τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.9}}; μετά τινα **after** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.605; so ὁ. ἐπὶ τὸ ἱρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.35; ἐς πύλας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 31; πρὸς δόμους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1152; ἐπ᾽ ἀλήθειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 228c; ἐς φυγήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.14; πρὸς τίσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1328; πρὸς τὸ κρατεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 581a; [ἡ ποίησις] πρὸς ἡδονὴν ὥρμηται [prev. author] “Grg.” 502c}}; οἱ περὶ λόγον ἢ παιδείαν ὁρμώμενοι persons **keen** about.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 199.5: rarely with _accusative_ loci, νερτέρας πλάκας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1576 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.2.b** the starting-point is expressed by ἐκ, ὡρμᾶτ᾽ ἐκ θαλάμοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.142, compare 9.178}}, etc.; or ἀπό, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 156, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101d, etc.; ἀπὸ φιλοσοφίας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.357 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; or by a form in -θεν, σέθεν.. ὕμνος ὁρμᾶται θέμεν αἶνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.5: in historical Prose, ὁρμᾶσθαι ἐκ..}} **start from, begin from,** especially of the place where one carries on any regular operations, ἐνθεῦτεν ὁρμώμενοι **living there and going out from thence** to do their daily work, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17}}; of fishers, ἐκ πλοίων ὁρμώμενοι {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.98}}; of a general, **making that place his head-quarters** or **base of operations,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.133, compare 5.125, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.64, 2.69, +others}}; ἀπ᾽ ἐλασσόνων ὁρμώμενος **setting out, beginning** with smaller means, {{Refs|[prev. work] 65, compare 1.144}}; of rivers, ἐκ τῆς Ἴδης ὁ. **rising..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 682b.}}
**B.3** _absolutely_, **rush, dart, attack,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.12, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.126, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1068 (Lyric poetry)}}; also with ἔγχεϊ, ξιφέεσσι, etc., added, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.855, 17.530, 13.496, +others}}
**B.3.b** generally, **hasten, be eager,** ὁρμώμενον δὲ μηδαμῶς μ᾽ ἀντισπάσῃς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 339, compare 395; ἀλλ᾽ ἥδε.. ὁρμᾶται}}**comes forth,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 151 (_anapaest meter_); τὸ φέγγος ὁρμάσθω πυρός [prev. author] “Eu.” 1029}}; ὕβρις ἀτάρβητα ὁρμᾶται insolence **goes** fearless **forth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 197 (Lyric poetry).}}

3730:
** ὁρμ-ή**, ἡ,
**rapid motion forwards, onrush, onset, assault,** μόγις δέ μευ ἔκφυγεν ὁρμήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.355}}; ἐκ τοῦ αὐτοῦ χωρίου ἡ ὁ. ἔσται the **attack, invasion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11; ἡ ἐπὶ βασιλέα ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.10}}; also of an **impulse** received from another, ἐμέ τ᾽ εἰσορόων καὶ ἐμὴν ποτιδέγμενος ὁ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.123, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.403.}}
**2** more frequently of things, πυρὸς ὁ. the **rage** of fire, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.157}}; ὑπὸ κύματος ὁρμῆς by the **shock** of a wave, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.320; ἔγχεος ὁ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 365}}; but ἐς ὁρμὴν ἔγχεος ἐλθεῖν within my spear's **cast,** within **reach** of my spear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.118}}; ὁ. γονάτων **spring** of knee, i.e. power to spring or leap, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.20}}; ποδὸς ὁ. **speed** of foot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 112 (Lyric poetry)}}: _plural_, of the tides, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 3.}}
**II** **impulse** to do a thing, **effort,** μίνυνθα δέ οἱ γένεθ᾽ ὁρμή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.466; μελέη δέ μοι ἔσσεται ὁ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.416; φιλότητος.. ἄμβροτος ὁ. 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 35.13; πίστιος ὁ. [prev. author] 114.3; ἐπεὶ δὲ δαιμονίη τις γίνεται ὁ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18; μαινομένᾳ σὺν ὁρμᾷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 135 (Lyric poetry), compare “Tr.” 720}}; τίς προσήγαγε χρεί; τίς ὁ.; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 237}}; οὕτω καθ᾽ ὁρμὴν δρῶσιν, i. e. with so much zeal,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 566; εἰ.. ἄγοι αὐτὸν ὁ. θειοτέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279a: joined with ἐπιθυμία, [prev. author] “Phlb.” 35d, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}; μιᾷ ὁ. with one **impulse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.9; ἀπὸ μιᾶς ὁ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71; ὑπὸ μιᾷ τῇ ὁ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 2}}: with _genitive_ objecti, **eager desire of** or **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.43, etc.}}: so with a _preposition_, ἡ ὁρμή, ἣν ὁρμᾷς ἐπὶ τοὺς λόγους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 135d, compare 130b; ἔχειν ὁρμὴν πρός τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1185a31, +others}}; ὁ. ἐπέπεσέ τισι, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4}}; ὁ. παραστῆσαί τισι εἴς τι or with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.48.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 33}}; ὁ. σχεῖν, with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Publ.” 19.}}
**II.2** in Stoic philosophy, **appetition,** including reasoned choice and irrational impulse, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.40, +others}}
**II.3** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus name for 2, Anatolius cited in “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 8.}}
**III** **setting oneself in motion, start** on a march, etc., ἐν ὁρμῇ εἶναι to be on **the point of starting,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.3, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1393a3}}; ἐπὶ παντὸς ὁρμῇ.. πράγματος at the **start** of every undertaking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27c}}; ἡ ὁ. [τούτων τῶν ἀνέμων] the **point at which** these winds **start,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 364b5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 511b (_plural_)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _s´rati_ 'flow'.)

3731:
** ὅρμ-ημα**, ατος, τό,
**sudden rush, swoop, onset,** ἀετοῦ {{LXX|LXX.Deu.28.49}}; of attacking troops,{{LXX|LXX.1Mac.4.8, +others}}; of the fall of a stone, {{NT|NT.Rev.18.21}}: _plural_, **rapid movement,** ὁρμήμασι νηός, ={νηῒ ὁρμωμένη}, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 13.21.}}
**2** ={ὁρμή}, **impulse, incitement, motive,** μηδ᾽.. ἡμῶν τι συνεργὸν μηδ᾽ ὅ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 98 G., compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.452c}}; τὸ ὅ. μου my **indignation,** {{LXX|LXX.Hos.5.10}}; θαλάσσης -ήματα, of the tides, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Paraphrasis Ptolemaei Tetrabiblou” 4.}}
**II** the earliest exception is Ἑλένης ὁρμήματά τε στοναχάς τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.356, 590, where Ἑλένης is taken by 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus cited in Scholia A}} as the objective _genitive_, the **cares** (as if from ὁρμαίνω) and groans [of the Greeks] **about** Helen, i. e. caused by her; by the χωρίζοντες {{Refs|([prev. passage]) as the subjective _genitive_, the}}**searchings of heart** and groans **of** Helen; the former view is more probably, but ὁ. may be from ὁρμάομαι and mean the **rushes, struggles** of war.

3732:
** ὄρνεον**, τό,
={ὄρνις}, **bird,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.64, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 108, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 291, 305, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 274c, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 756a16, +others}}
**II** τὰ ὄ. the **bird-market,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 13.}}

3733:
** ὄρνις**, ὁ,
also ἡ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.323, 14.290, +others, frequently in _Attic dialect_, compare 111}}; _genitive_ ὄρνῑθο; _accusative_ _singular_ ὄρνῑθα and ὄρνιν, neither in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus: _plural_, _nominative_ and _accusative_ ὄρνῑθες, -θας, but in _accusative_ also ὄρνεις or ὄρνῑς (5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 966, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1059, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 717, 1250, 1610, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.245, etc.)}}:—also **ὄρνιξ**, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 375.1 (3rd c.BC), variant in NT.Luke.13.34}}, called _Ionic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ by {{Refs 7th c.BC+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. (but ὄρνις _nominative_ in 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 26.4)}}; _accusative_ ὄρνῑχα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.88; _genitive_ ὄρνῑχος [prev. author] “I.” 6(5).53: _nominative_ _plural_ ὄρνῑχες 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.22, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.47; _genitive_ _plural_ ὀρνίχων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 67, “Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1925(5).33 (Cyrene, 4th c.BC)}}; _dative_ ὄρνιξι, ὀρνίχεσσι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.112, 4.190 (ὄρνιξι also in “PLond.” 1.131r. 125, +others (1st c.AD))}}: on the gender and _declension_, see {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.373f (Cf. ὄρν-εον}} Gothic _ara,_ _genitive_ **arins** 'eagle', etc.) [In the _trisyllable_ cases ῑ always: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ὄρνῑς in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.323, 12.218, but ὄρνῐς [prev. work] 24.219}}; and later _Epic dialect_ use both ὄρνῑς and ὄρνῐς: in Trag. both quantities are found, ὄρνῐς in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 304.3 (-ῐν), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1021, “El.” 149 (Lyric poetry), “fragment” 654, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 72, and so 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 79.10; but ὄρνῑς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1365, and always in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus (“Av.” 103, +others), for inib. 168, the words τίς ὄρνῐς οὗτος}}; are borrowed from Sophocles; ὄρνῑς is said to be _Attic dialect_, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 632.8.]}}
**I** **bird,** including birds of prey and domestic fowls, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; applied to ostriches, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.7}}: frequently added to the specific names, ὄρνισιν ἐοικότες αἰγυπιοῖσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.59; λάρῳ ὄρνιθι ἐοικώς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.51}}; ὄ. ἀηδών, πέρδιξ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 629, “fragment” 323}}; ὄ. ἁλκυών, ὄ. κύκνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1089 (Lyric poetry), “Hel.” 19.}}
**II** like{οἰωνός}, **bird of omen,** from the flight or cries of which the augur divined, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 828}}; δεξιός, ἀριστερὸς ὄρνις, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.821, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.242, +others; χρηστηρίους ὄρνιθας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 26, compare “Ag.” 112, 157 (both Lyric poetry); ὄ. αἴσιος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 52, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 19, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.314; ὀρνίθων οἰωνίσματα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 839.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **omen taken from the flight** or **cries of birds,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.277, +others}}: generally, **omen, presage,** without direct reference to birds,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|24.219, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.19; ὄρνιθα δ᾽ οὐ ποιῶ σε τῆς ἐμῆς ὁδοῦ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 95, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 988, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 63, “Av.” 719 ff.}}; see at {ὅδιος}.
**III** in _Attic dialect_ ὄρνις, ὁ, is mostly, **cock,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 18; κοκκυβόας ὄ. [prev. author] “fragment” 791, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 815}}; ὄρνις, ἡ, **hen,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 167, 168, “PCair.Zen.” 266 (3rd c.BC, _plural_)}}; ἀλέκτορα καὶ ὄρνιθα τελέαν cock and **hen**, {{Refs|“TAM” 2(1).245.8 (from Lycia)}}; in full, ὄ. ἐνοίκιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 866; θήλεια ὄ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 477}}; πότερον ὄ. ἢ ταὧ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 102 (with play on this signification and signf. I)}}; ὁ ὄρνιξ ὁ σιτευτός fatted **fowl,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 375.1; ὀρνίθων φοινικολόφων 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.72, compare 24.64, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.49; ὄ. οἰκίης 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 17.1}}; also, **goose,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 123.1.}}
**IV** in _plural_ sometimes, **bird-market,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.245; compare ὄρνεον 11.}}
**V** Μοισᾶν ὄρνιχες **song-birds,** i.e. **poets,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.47.}}
**VI** Provs.: διώκει παῖς ποτανὸν ὄρνιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 394 (Lyric poetry); ὄ. ὥς τις ἐκ χερῶν ἄφαντος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 828}}; ὀρνίθων γάλα 'pigeon's milk', i.e. any marvellous dainty or good fortune, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 508, “Av.” 1673, 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 936; but ὄρνιθος γάλα}}**white of egg,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 22}}; also a plant, see at {ὀρνιθόγαλον}.
**VII** a constellation, later Cygnus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.16, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 275, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 26.}}

3734:
** ὁρο-θεσία**, ἡ,
**fixing of boundaries**: in _plural_, **limitations, boundaries**:— {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 42.8 and 12 (2nd c.BC), NT.Act.17.26 (_plural_), “BGU” 889.17 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **ὁρο-θέσια**, τά, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.349, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, etc.: gloss on{οὖροι}, “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum Hdt.” cited in Stein “Herodotus” 2p.468: the _sing._ ὁροθέσιον}}] **(boundary)** occurs in Petr.Patr.p.433 {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

3735:
** ὄρος**, εος, τό:
_genitive_ _plural_ ὀρέων (the form proper to _Ionic dialect_ and other dialects) is required by the metre in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 719 (iambic) and frequently in Lyric poetry verse, as 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1106}}, and is alone used in {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX, LXX.Gen.8.5, +others, compare “IG” 7.2225.18 (2nd c.BC), NT.Rev.6.15}}; but ὀρῶν (the _Attic dialect_ form) is required in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 719, 811, “fragment” 342, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 791, and occurs in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 111c, “SIG” 888.120 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD), etc.: the _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry forms οὔρεος, οὔρεϊ, οὔρεα, οὔρεσι probably have οὐρ- for the sake of meter for ὀρ- (so ὤρεα, ὤρεος}} for the sake of meter| in _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.77, 115, 4.35, “Hymnus ad Isim” 162): the _Ionic dialect_ form is ὀρ-, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.21, +others, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 2.5}}, see at {ὄρειος} (οὔρεσιν is an Epicism in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 14.1, compare οὔρη}} at end of a _hexameter_, {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).445.6 (from Thasos))}}; ὄρος is found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.43, 2.8, 12, 7.176}} and should perhaps be _restored_ elsewhere:—**mountain, hill,** ὄρεος κορυφῇσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.10, +others; οὔρεος ἐν βήσσῃσιν 11.87; ἐν ὄρεσσι 1.235, +others; ἐν οὔρεσιν 24.614, +others; τρέμε δ᾽ οὔρεα μακρά 13.18, etc.; Γαῖα.. γείνατο δ᾽ Οὔρεα μακρά, θεῶν χαρίεντας ἐναύλους 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 129.}}
**2** **canton, parish,** ὄ. Ἀργειατᾶν, ὄ. Ὀγχνιάἱον, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Mnemos.” 42.332 (Argos, 4th c.BC).}}
**3** in Egypt, **desert,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PTeb. 383.61 (1st c.AD)}}; as place of burial, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 274.27 (1st c.AD), “PRyl.” 153.5 (2nd c.AD), “PGrenf.” 2.77.22 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**4** part of the foot, ={τὸ ἄνω πρὸ τῶν δακτύλων}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.197.}}
**5** ={ἀμίς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 82.}}

3736:
** ὀρύσσω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.305}}, _Attic dialect_ **ὀρύττω** (late _present_ _imperative_ ὄρυγε {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).519 (from Seriphos))}}: _future_ ὀρύξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.341:}}_aorist_ ὤρυξα, _Epic dialect_ ὄρυξα as always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.25, +others:}}_perfect_ ὀρώρῠχα (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.19}}: _pluperfect_ ὠρωρύχειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.107}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ὠρυξάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1032, etc.:}} —_passive_, _future_ ὀρυχθήσομαι (κατ-) [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.10, also ὀρῠχήσομαι (κατ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 394 (Elmsl.) and ὀρωρύξομαι 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {ὤρυσσον} (probably)}}]: _aorist_ ὠρύχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὀρώρυγμαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.158, etc.}}; in compounds, ὤρυγμαι (κατ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.12 codices, ὑποκατ- 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 3, δι- 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 53, etc.)}}: _pluperfect_ ὀρωρύγμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118c, also ὠρωρύγμην (δι-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.14.}} —An _aorist 2_ _active_ ὤρῠγον occurs in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.25}}: _passive_ ὠρύγην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 72.7,673.6 (Egypt, 1st c.AD), (δι-) 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.7, “Geoponica” 4.3.2}}, (κατ-) _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.11}}:—**dig,** ὀρύξομεν ἐγγύθι τάφρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.341; βόθρον ὄρυξα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.25; ἔλυτρον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186; ὀρύγματα [prev. author] 4.200; ταῖς ὁπλαῖς εὐνάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 605; ὑπόνομον ἐκ τῆς πόλεως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76: _absolutely_, ὤρυσσον ὑπὸ μαστίγων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.22; ἐὰν ὀρύξῃ τις παρὰ τὴν θάλασσαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 933b33}}:—_middle_, δεξαμενὰς ὀρύξασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.9:}} —_passive_, ὀρώρυκται (i.e. ἡ διῶρυξ) {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.158}}; τὸ ὀρυχθέν, ={τὸ ὄρυγμα}, the **trench,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.186.}}
**II** **dig up,** [μῶλυ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.305; κυκλάμινον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.123:}}_middle_, λίθους ὠρύξατο **had** stones **dug** or **quarried,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186:}} —_passive_, ὁ ὀρυσσόμενος χοῦς the soil **that was dug up,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 185; ὑπὸ μεταλλείας ὀρύττεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 114e.}}
**III** **dig through,** i. e. **make a canal through** (like διορύσσειν), τὸν ἰσθμὸν ὀ. Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.174; τὸ χωρίον ὀρώρυκτο [prev. author] 1.186}}; of moles, **burrow,** either _absolutely_, as {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 606a2}}; or γῆν ὀ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mir.” 842b4.}}
**IV** **bury,** ἔγχος.. γαίας ὀρύξας ἔνθα μήτις ὄψεται (where γαίας depends on ἔνθα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 659, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.2.}}
**V** of a wrestler, **dig into, gouge** a tender part, τὠφθαλμώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 442, compare “Pax” 899, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.25}}; **gouge out,** ὀφθαλμὸν ὤρυττέν τις ὥσπερ ἰχθύος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 119.}}

3737:
** ὀρφᾰνός**, ή, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 149 (_anapaest meter_)}}:— **orphan, without parents, fatherless,** αἱ δ᾽ ἐλίποντο ὀρφαναί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.68; ὀ. τέκνα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 330; παῖδά τ᾽ ὀ. λιπεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 653}}: as _substantive_, **orphan,** ἐπίκληροι καὶ ὀ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.12; ὀρφανοῖς καὶ ὀρφαναῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 926c; ὀρφανῶν κάκωσις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ath.” 56.6, compare “IG” 12.6.124: also in _neuter_, εἰς ὀρφανὰ καὶ ἔρημα ὑβρίζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 927c}}; of animals, ὄρνις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1361}}; ὀ. οἶκος, δόμος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 943, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 657}}: _metaphorically_, **neglected,** ἡ δέλτος ὀρφανὴ κεῖται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.15}}; ὀρφανὰ κεῖται σκῦλα epigram cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.13.3.}}
**II** with _genitive_, **bereaved** or **bereft of,**
**II.1** of children, ὀ. πατρός **reft** of father, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 914, 1010; τοῦ πατρὸς ὀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.70; γονέων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.293d, etc.}}
**II.2** of parents, πότμον ὀρφανὸν γενεᾶς childless, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.61}}; ὀ. παιδός, τέκνων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 149 (_anapaest meter_), “fragment” 332.6; νεοσσῶν ὀρφανὸν λέχος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 425.}}
**II.3** generally, ὀ. ἑτάρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 730d; νύμφας ἔθηκεν ὀρφανὰς ξυναόρων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1136; ἐπιστήμης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.147a; κρατός Sositheus Tragicus 2.20}}; ὀρφανοὶ ὕβριος **free from** insolence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).8(26); ψόφον.. Κασταλίας ὀρφανὸν ἀνδρῶν χορεύσιος [prev. author] “Pae.” 6.9}}; ὀ. μαχᾶν, ={ἀπόμαχος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 154}}; ὀρφανὴ βίου, i.e. **poor,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.39; ὀ. ἀγκίστρου κάλαμος “Anthologia Graeca” 12.42 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus): Comedy texts _metaphorically_, ὀ. ταρίχιον}} salt-fish **without sauce,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 22 (uncertain reading). (A shorter form ὀρφο- appears in ὀρφο-βότης (which see), ὀρφόω}} cf. Latin _orbus,_ Gothic _arbi_ 'inheritance'.)

3738:
** ὀρχέομαι**,
_imperfect_ ὠρχούμην: _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _present_ ὀρχεῦνται, _imperfect_ ὠρχεῦντο (see. below): _future_ ὀρχήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1178, etc.}}: _aorist_ ὠρχησάμην {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 69, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.129}}; _infinitive_ ὀρχήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below)}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ὠρχήθην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 87}}:—**dance,** ἠΐθεοι καὶ παρθένοι.. ὠρχεῦντ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.594; Ἅλιον καὶ Λαοδάμαντα κέλευσε μουνὰξ ὀρχήσασθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.371, compare 14.465; πόσσ᾽ ἁπαλοῖσιν ὀρχεῦνται 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 4}}; ὀ. πρὸς ὅπλα, of the Pyrrhic dance, Demetr.Sceps. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.155b; ἐν ῥυθμῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.10}}; ὀρχεῖσθαι ταῖς χερσί (compare χειρονομέω) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 113.1: with _accusative_ loci, δώσω τοι Τεγέην ποσσίκροτον ὀρχήσασθαι}} **to dance in** or **on,** Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.66, cf. Euph. [prev. cited] (_passive_): also with _accusative_ _cognate_, Λακωνικὰ σχημάτια ὀρχεῖσθαι}} **dance** Laconian steps, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.129; ὀ. τὸ Περσικόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.12; ὀ. πρὸς τὸν αὐλὸν σχήματα [prev. author] “Smp.” 7.5; ὀ. τὸν ὅρμον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” II}} {ὀρχηδόν}, etc.:—_passive_, τῶν ὕμνων οἳ μὲν ὠρχοῦντο οἳ δὲ οὐκ ὠρχοῦντο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.631d.}}
**2** **represent by dancing** or **pantomime,** ὀρχεῖσθαι τὴν τοῦ Κρόνου τεκνοφαγίαν, ὀ. τὸν Αἴαντα, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 80, 83, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.248 (1st c.AD: Boethus Epigrammaticus), 11.254 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **leap, bound,** ὀρχεῖται δὲ καρδία φόβῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 166, compare 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 59}}; Θεσσαλίη ὠρχήσατο Thessaly **shook, trembled,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 139.}}
**III** _active_ **ὀρχέω**, **make to dance** (see. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 407a)}}, is used by Ion Trag.{{Refs|50, ἐκ τῶν ἀέλπτων μᾶλλον ὤρχησεν φρένας}} **made** my heart **leap** (so codices {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus, ὤρχησαι Nauck)}}; but ὀρκῆσι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1179 is a barbarism for ὀρχῆται.}}

3739:
** ὅς**, ἥ, ὅ,
_genitive_ οὗ, ἧς, οὗ, etc.; _dative_ _plural_ οἷς, αἷς, οἷς, etc.: _Epic dialect_ forms, _genitive_ ὅου (probably replacing Οο) in the phrases ὅου κλέος οὔ ποτ᾽ ὀλεῖται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.325, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 156; ὅου κράτος ἐστὶ μέγιστον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.70 (elsewhere οὗ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.325, +others, never οἷο)}}; _feminine_ ἕης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.208 (perhaps imitation of ὅου}}; elsewhere only ἧς {{Refs|5.265, +others)}}; _dative_ _plural_ οἷς, οἷσι, ᾗς, ᾗσι (never αἷς or αἷσι in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus):}} — _pronoun_ used,
**A ** as _demonstrative_ by the side of οὗτος, ὅδε, and the _Article_ ὁ, ἡ, τό: in post-Homeric Gr. this use survived only in a few special phrases.
**B ** as a Relat. by the side of the _Article_ ὅ, ἥ, τό (see. ὁ, ἡ, τό, c):—this _demonstrative_ and Relat. _pronoun_ must not be confounded with the Possess. ὅς, ἥ, ὅν. (With Gr. Relat. ὅς, ἥ, ὅ cf. Sanskrit Relat. **yas, yā, yad,** Lithuanian _jis, ji (he, she),_ Oslav. **i, ja, je (he, she, it**).)
**A** DEMONSTR. PRON., ={οὗτος},{ὅδε}, **this, that**; also, **he, she, it**:
**A.I** Homeric usage: this form only occurs in the _nominative_ _masculine_ and _neuter_ ὅς, ὅ, and perhaps _nominative_ _feminine_ ἥ and _nominative_ _plural_ οἵ, the other cases being supplied by ὁ, ἡ, τό (ὅ, ἡ, τό); most codices have ἥ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.551, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.255, +others, and this (as also οἵ) can be referred equally to either (on the accent see ὁ, ἡ, τό): with γάρ or καί, ὃς γὰρ δεύτατος ἦλθεν 1.286; ἀλλὰ καὶ ὃς δείδοικε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.198; ὃ γὰρ γέρας ἐστὶ θανόντων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.190, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.9, compare 12.344: frequently used emphatically in apodosi, mostly with οὐδέ or μηδέ}} before it, μηδ᾽ ὅν τινα γαστέρι μήτηρ κοῦρον ἐόντα φέροι, μηδ᾽ ὃς φύγοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.59, compare 7.160, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.653: after a}}_participle_, εἰς ἕτερον γάρ τίς τε ἰδών.., ὃς σπεύδει (for ὅστις ἂν ἴδῃ, ὃς σπεύδει) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 22.}}
**A.II** in later Gr. this usage remained in a few forms:
**A.II.1** at the beginning of a clause, καὶ ὅς and **he,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 118, “Prt.” 310d}}; καὶ ἥ and **she,** καὶ οἵ and **they,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.56, 87, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 201e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.4.}}
**A.II.2** ὃς καὶ ὅς **such and such a person,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.68:}} —here also the _Article_ supplied the _oblative_ cases.
**A.II.3** ἦ δ᾽ ὅς, ἦ δ᾽ ἥ, said **he,** said **she,** see at {ἠμί}.
**A.II.4** in oppositions, where it sometimes answers to the _Article_, Λέριοι κακοί· οὐχ ὁ μέν, ὃς δ᾽ οὔ.. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 1; ὃς μὲν.., ὃ δὲ.. 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.76; ὃ μὲν.., ὃς δὲ.., ὃ δὲ.., ὃς δὲ.. 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.81; so τῷ μὲν.., ᾧ δὲ.., ᾧ δὲ.. “Anthologia Graeca” 6.187 (1st c.AD: Alpheus Epigrammaticus)}}; ὃ μὲν.., ὃ δὲ.., ὃ δὲ.. (_neuter_) {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Matt.13.8; ἂ μὲν.., ἃ δὲ.. 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 102, 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.110; ὧν μὲν.., ὧν δὲ.. 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 99; πόλεις ἃς μὲν.., ἃς δὲ.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.71 (as see l.)}}: so in _Doric dialect_ _dative_ _feminine_ as _adverb_, ᾇ μὲν.., ᾇ δὲ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.81; ἐφ᾽ ὧν μὲν.., ἐφ᾽ ὧν δὲ.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1109a1}}: very frequently in late Prose, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.25.1, etc.}}: also answering to other Prons., ἑτέρων.., ὧν δὲ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 31.6; ἐφ᾽ ᾧ μὲν.., ἐπὶ θατέρῳ δὲ.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 564a21, etc.}}
**B** RELAT. PRON., **who, which.**—By the side of the simple Relat., ὅς, ἥ, ὅ (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus also ὁ, ἡ, τό), we find in common use the _compound_ forms ὅστε, ὅστις and ὅτις, ὅσπερ and ὅπερ, ὅς γε (which see).}}
**A0**USAGE of the Relat. _pronoun_ (the following remarks apply to ὅς γε, ὅσπερ, ὅστε, ὅστις, as well as to ὅς, and to ὁ, ἡ, τό as _relative_):
**A0.I** in respect of CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}} —Prop. it agrees in gender and number with the Noun or _pronoun_ in the antecedent clause.—But this rule admits of many exceptions:
**A0.I.1** the Relat. mayagree with the gender implied, not expressed, in the antecedent, φίλον θάλος, ὃν τέκον αὐτή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.87; τέκνων, οὓς ἤγαγε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 12}}: so after collective Nouns, the Relat. is frequently put in _plural_ in the gender implied in the Noun, λαόν.., οὕς.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.369}}; στρατιάν.. τοιαύτην.., οἵ τινες.., τὸ ναυτικόν, οἵ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91, 3.4; πλήθει, οἵπερ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260a}}; especially after the names of countries or cities, Τηλέπυλον Λαιστρυγονίην ἀφίκανεν, οἳ.. (i. e. to Telepylos **of the Laestrygonians, who.. **) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.319; τὰς Ἀθήνας, οἵ γε.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8. β; Μέγαρα.., οὓς.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.94: it also may agree with the Noun or _pronoun_ implied in an}} _adjective_, Θηβαίας ἐπισκοποῦντ᾽ ἀγυιάς, τάν.. the streets **of Thebes, which..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1137 (Lyric poetry)}}; τοὺς Ἡρακλείους παῖδας, ὃς.. the children **of Heracles, who..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 157; τῆς ἐμῆς ἐπεισόδου, ὅν..}}**of me whom..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 731}}; τὸν ἥμισύν ἐστ᾽ ἀτελὴς τοῦ χρόνου· εἶθ᾽ ἧς πᾶσι μέτεστι.., where ἧς agrees with ἀτελείας implied in ἀτελής, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.8.}}
**A0.I.2** when the antec. Noun in _singular_ implies a class, the Relat. is sometimes in _plural_, ἦ μάλα τις θεὸς ἔνδον, οἳ.. ἔχουσιν (for τις θεῶν, οἵ..) in some places, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 638}}; οὗ μέν.., οὗ δέ.. **in some places.., in others..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1345b34: with _genitive_, οὐκ εἶδεν οὗ γῆς εἰσέδυ}} **in what part** of the earth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” [1583]; ἐννοεῖς οὗ ἐστὶ.. τοῦ ἀναμιμνήσκεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 84a; συνιδὼν οὗ κακῶν ἦν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 17.}}
**A0.I.2** in pregnant phrases, μικρὸν προϊόντες..,οὗ ἡ μάχη ἐγένετο (for ἐκεῖσε οὗ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.6; so οὗπερ προσβεβοηθήκει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.86, compare 1.134}}; ἀπιὼν ἐκ τῆς πόλεως, οὗ κατέφυγε (for οἷ κατέφυγε καὶ οὗ ἦν) {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.14 (uncertain reading); ἐπειδὰν ἱζήσωμεν οὗ ἄγεις 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Her.Prooem.” 13: in later Gr. οὗ was used simply for οἷ, οὗπερ ἂν ἔλθῃ Tim069,cf. NT.Luke.10.1, etc.}}: but in early writers this is _falsa lectio_, as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.74, etc.}}
**A0.II** _dative_ _feminine_ ᾗ, _Doric dialect_ ᾇ, of Place, **where,** or Manner, **as,** see at {ᾗ}.
**A0.III** old _locative_ οἷ, as _adverb_, see at {οἷ}.
**A0.III.2** old _ablative_ (?) ὧ, in _Doric dialect_ (compare ϝοίκω), τηνῶθε καθεῖλον, ὧ (**whence**) μ᾽ ἐκέλευ καθελεῖν τυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.11; ἐν τᾷ πόλι, ὧ κ᾽ ᾖ, καρῡξαι ἐν τἀγορᾷ “IG” 9(1).334.21 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC).}}
**A0.IV** _accusative_ _singular_ _neuter_ ὅ, very frequently ={ὅτι}, **that, how that,** λεύσσετε γὰρ τό γε πάντες ὅ μοι γέρας ἔρχεται ἄλλῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.120, +others}}; and so also, **because,** ταρβήσας ὅ οἱ ἄγχι πάγη βέλος {{Refs|20.283, +others}}
**A0.IV.2** in _Attic dialect_ ὅ, **for which reason,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 13, “Ph.” 155, 263, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 338: also _accusative_ _neuter_ _plural_ ἅ in this sense, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 137 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.122.}}
**A0.IV.3** **whereas,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40, 3.12, NT.Rom.6.10, NT.Gal.2.20.}}
**A0.V** ἀφ᾽ οὗπερ **from the time that..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 177.}}
**A0.VI** ἐφ᾽ ᾧ, see at {ἐπί} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.3.}}

3741:
** ὅσι-ος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 831d, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.71}}:— **hallowed,** i. e. **sanctioned** or **allowed by the law of God** or **of nature,** δίκη {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 132; θοῖναι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 529 (Lyric poetry); λουτρά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1405 (_anapaest meter_); καθαρμοί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 77 (Lyric poetry); μέλος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 898}}; οὐχ ὅ. **unhallowed,** ὕβρις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 374 (Lyric poetry); ἔρως [prev. author] “Hipp.” 764 (Lyric poetry); θυσίαι [prev. author] “IT” 465 (_anapaest meter_).}} —The sense of ὅσιος often depends on its relation on the one hand to δίκαιος (sanctioned by **human** law), on the other to ἱερός (**sacred** to the gods):
**1** opposed to δίκαιος, **sanctioned by divine law, hallowed, holy** (μόριον τοῦ δικαίου τὸ ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 12d), δικαιότερον καὶ ὁσιώτερον καὶ πρὸς θεῶν καὶ πρὸς ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.25; τὰ πρὸς τοὺς ἀνθρώπους δίκαια καὶ τὰ πρὸς τοὺς θεοὺς ὅ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.10.8: hence, in a common antithesis, τὰ δίκαια καὶ ὅ.}} **things of** human and **divine ordinance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 301d, etc., compare “Euthphr.” 6e; also ὅ. καὶ νόμιμα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 676 (Lyric poetry); οὐ.. νόμιμον οὐδ᾽ ὅ. ἂν εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 861d}}; θεοὺς ὅσιόν τι δρᾶν discharge **a duty men owe the gods,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 40, compare “Hipp.” 1081}}; τὸ ὅσιον, ={εὐσέβεια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 5d}}: in an imprecatory formula, ἀποδοῦσι μὲν αὐτοῖς ὅσια ᾖ, μὴ ἀποδοῦσι δὲ ἀνόσια {{Refs|“SIG” 1199 (from Cnidus), compare ἀνοσία 11; so ὅ. καὶ ἐλεύθερα [prev. work]1180.6 ([prev. passage]).}}
**2** opposed to ἱερός, **permitted** or **not forbidden by divine law, profane,** ἱερὰ καὶ ὅ. things sacred and **profane,** ἐς ὀλιγωρίαν ἐτράποντο καὶ ἱερῶν καὶ ὁ. ὁμοίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.52, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344a, “Lg.” 857b, etc.}}; κοσμεῖν τὴν πόλιν καὶ τοῖς ἱεροῖς καὶ τοῖς ὁ. with sacred and **profane** buildings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.66; τῶν ἱερῶν μὲν χρημάτων τοὺς θεούς, τῶν ὁ. δὲ τὴν πόλιν ἀποστερεῖ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.9; ἀργυρίου ὁσίου “IG” 12.186.13; ὁ ταμίας τῶν ὁ. προσόδων “OGI” 229.58 (Smyrna, 3rd c.BC)}}; ὁ ταμίας τῶν ὁ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 1.366.58 (Samos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ὅ. χωρίον a **lawful** place (for giving birth to a child), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 743}}; ὅσιόν ἐστι followed by _infinitive_, **it is lawful, not forbidden by any law,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1045, etc.; οὐκ ὅσιόν ἐστι}}**nefas est,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.81}}; οὐκ ὅσιον ποιεῦμαι I deem it **impious,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.170, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 5.3}}; οὐδὲ ὅσια (i.e. ἐστι).. μιαίνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 416e; οὐ γάρ σοι θέμις οὐδ᾽ ὅσιον.. ἱστάναι κτερίσματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 432; ὅσια ποιέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86}}.α; λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.79; φωνεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 662; φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1203 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** of persons, **pious, devout, religious,** ἄνδρες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 27 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 850 (Lyric poetry), etc.; Παλλάδος ὁ. πόλις [prev. author] “El.” 1320 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὅ. θιασῶται, μύσται, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 327, 336 (both Lyric poetry); ἐμαυτὸν ὅ. καὶ δίκαιον παρέχειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.2; ὅσιοι πρὸς οὐ δικαίους ἱστάμεθα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.104}}; opposed to ἀνόσιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 547}}; opposed to ἐπίορκος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.25}}; ὅσιος εἴς τινα, περὶ ξένους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 719, “Cyc.” 125; πρὸς τοὺς τοκέας 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista 6.}}
**II.2** **sinless, pure,** ἐξ ὁ. στομάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 4.2}}; ὅ. ἔστω καὶ εὐαγής Legal Solonis cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.96: with _genitive_, ἱερῶν πατρῴων ὅσιος}} in regard to the sacred rites of his forefathers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1015; ὅσιος ἀπ᾽ εὐνᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 150 (Lyric poetry)}}; also ὅσιαι χέρες **pure, clean** hands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 378 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 470.}}
**II.3** rarely of the gods, **holy,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 77.2; θεοῖς ὁ. καὶ δικαίοις “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3830 (from Cotyaeum), compare 3594 (Alexandria Troas).}}
**II.4** title of five special priests at Delphi, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.292d, 365a.}}
**II.5** οἱ ὅσιοι 'the **saints',** {{LXX|LXX.Psa.29(30).4, +others}}
**III** _adverb_ ὁσίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.12, etc.; ὁ. οὔχ, ὑπ᾽ ἀνάγκας δέ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 63 (Lyric poetry); οὐχ ὁ. [prev. author] “Hipp.” 1287 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5 (variant); καλῶς καὶ ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113d; δικαίως καὶ ὁ. [prev. author] “R.” 331a; ὁ. καὶ κατὰ νόμον [prev. author] “Lg.” 799b}}; ὁ. ἂν ὑμῖν ἔχοι τοῦτον θύειν.. it would be **right** for you that he should.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.26: with}}_participle_, ὁ. ἂν ἔχοι αὐτῷ μὴ δεχομένῳ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 4.7.2}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1194, etc.}}: _superlative_, ὡς -ώτατα διαβιῶναι τὸν βίον [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 81b, etc. (Not in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who has only _substantive_ ὁσίη, see at {ὁσία}.)}}]

3743:
Included with: ** ὅσι-ος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 831d, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.71}}:— **hallowed,** i. e. **sanctioned** or **allowed by the law of God** or **of nature,** δίκη {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 132; θοῖναι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 529 (Lyric poetry); λουτρά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1405 (_anapaest meter_); καθαρμοί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 77 (Lyric poetry); μέλος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 898}}; οὐχ ὅ. **unhallowed,** ὕβρις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 374 (Lyric poetry); ἔρως [prev. author] “Hipp.” 764 (Lyric poetry); θυσίαι [prev. author] “IT” 465 (_anapaest meter_).}} —The sense of ὅσιος often depends on its relation on the one hand to δίκαιος (sanctioned by **human** law), on the other to ἱερός (**sacred** to the gods):
**1** opposed to δίκαιος, **sanctioned by divine law, hallowed, holy** (μόριον τοῦ δικαίου τὸ ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 12d), δικαιότερον καὶ ὁσιώτερον καὶ πρὸς θεῶν καὶ πρὸς ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.25; τὰ πρὸς τοὺς ἀνθρώπους δίκαια καὶ τὰ πρὸς τοὺς θεοὺς ὅ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.10.8: hence, in a common antithesis, τὰ δίκαια καὶ ὅ.}} **things of** human and **divine ordinance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 301d, etc., compare “Euthphr.” 6e; also ὅ. καὶ νόμιμα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 676 (Lyric poetry); οὐ.. νόμιμον οὐδ᾽ ὅ. ἂν εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 861d}}; θεοὺς ὅσιόν τι δρᾶν discharge **a duty men owe the gods,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 40, compare “Hipp.” 1081}}; τὸ ὅσιον, ={εὐσέβεια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 5d}}: in an imprecatory formula, ἀποδοῦσι μὲν αὐτοῖς ὅσια ᾖ, μὴ ἀποδοῦσι δὲ ἀνόσια {{Refs|“SIG” 1199 (from Cnidus), compare ἀνοσία 11; so ὅ. καὶ ἐλεύθερα [prev. work]1180.6 ([prev. passage]).}}
**2** opposed to ἱερός, **permitted** or **not forbidden by divine law, profane,** ἱερὰ καὶ ὅ. things sacred and **profane,** ἐς ὀλιγωρίαν ἐτράποντο καὶ ἱερῶν καὶ ὁ. ὁμοίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.52, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344a, “Lg.” 857b, etc.}}; κοσμεῖν τὴν πόλιν καὶ τοῖς ἱεροῖς καὶ τοῖς ὁ. with sacred and **profane** buildings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.66; τῶν ἱερῶν μὲν χρημάτων τοὺς θεούς, τῶν ὁ. δὲ τὴν πόλιν ἀποστερεῖ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.9; ἀργυρίου ὁσίου “IG” 12.186.13; ὁ ταμίας τῶν ὁ. προσόδων “OGI” 229.58 (Smyrna, 3rd c.BC)}}; ὁ ταμίας τῶν ὁ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 1.366.58 (Samos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ὅ. χωρίον a **lawful** place (for giving birth to a child), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 743}}; ὅσιόν ἐστι followed by _infinitive_, **it is lawful, not forbidden by any law,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1045, etc.; οὐκ ὅσιόν ἐστι}}**nefas est,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.81}}; οὐκ ὅσιον ποιεῦμαι I deem it **impious,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.170, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 5.3}}; οὐδὲ ὅσια (i.e. ἐστι).. μιαίνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 416e; οὐ γάρ σοι θέμις οὐδ᾽ ὅσιον.. ἱστάναι κτερίσματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 432; ὅσια ποιέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86}}.α; λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.79; φωνεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 662; φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1203 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** of persons, **pious, devout, religious,** ἄνδρες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 27 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 850 (Lyric poetry), etc.; Παλλάδος ὁ. πόλις [prev. author] “El.” 1320 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὅ. θιασῶται, μύσται, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 327, 336 (both Lyric poetry); ἐμαυτὸν ὅ. καὶ δίκαιον παρέχειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.2; ὅσιοι πρὸς οὐ δικαίους ἱστάμεθα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.104}}; opposed to ἀνόσιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 547}}; opposed to ἐπίορκος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.25}}; ὅσιος εἴς τινα, περὶ ξένους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 719, “Cyc.” 125; πρὸς τοὺς τοκέας 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista 6.}}
**II.2** **sinless, pure,** ἐξ ὁ. στομάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 4.2}}; ὅ. ἔστω καὶ εὐαγής Legal Solonis cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.96: with _genitive_, ἱερῶν πατρῴων ὅσιος}} in regard to the sacred rites of his forefathers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1015; ὅσιος ἀπ᾽ εὐνᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 150 (Lyric poetry)}}; also ὅσιαι χέρες **pure, clean** hands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 378 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 470.}}
**II.3** rarely of the gods, **holy,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 77.2; θεοῖς ὁ. καὶ δικαίοις “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3830 (from Cotyaeum), compare 3594 (Alexandria Troas).}}
**II.4** title of five special priests at Delphi, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.292d, 365a.}}
**II.5** οἱ ὅσιοι 'the **saints',** {{LXX|LXX.Psa.29(30).4, +others}}
**III** _adverb_ ὁσίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.12, etc.; ὁ. οὔχ, ὑπ᾽ ἀνάγκας δέ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 63 (Lyric poetry); οὐχ ὁ. [prev. author] “Hipp.” 1287 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5 (variant); καλῶς καὶ ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113d; δικαίως καὶ ὁ. [prev. author] “R.” 331a; ὁ. καὶ κατὰ νόμον [prev. author] “Lg.” 799b}}; ὁ. ἂν ὑμῖν ἔχοι τοῦτον θύειν.. it would be **right** for you that he should.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.26: with}}_participle_, ὁ. ἂν ἔχοι αὐτῷ μὴ δεχομένῳ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 4.7.2}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1194, etc.}}: _superlative_, ὡς -ώτατα διαβιῶναι τὸν βίον [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 81b, etc. (Not in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who has only _substantive_ ὁσίη, see at {ὁσία}.)}}]

3744:
** ὀσμ-ή**, ἡ,
_Attic dialect_ form of the older **ὀδμή** (see. near the end), **smell, odour,** frequently of **foul smells,** δεινὴ δὲ θεείου γίγνεται ὀδμή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.415}}; πικρὸν ἀποπνείουσαι ἁλὸς.. ὀδμήν, of seals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.406; ὀδμὰ.. κατὰ χῶρον κίδναται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 129.6; ὀσμὴ βροτείων αἱμάτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 253}}; ὀσμὴν ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ (i.e. τοῦ νεκροῦ).. πεφευγότες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 412; κακὴ ὀσμή [prev. author] “Ph.” 891: _plural_, βυρσῶν ὀσμὰς δεινάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 753; ὀσμαὶ οὐκ ἀνεκτοί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.87, etc.}}; but also of **fragrant odours,** ὡς καλὴν ὀσμὴν ἔχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 153, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1124; οἴνου ὀ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 222.4, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 98.4}}: hence, **scent, perfume,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.4, 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 2.38}}; on ὀ. as affecting the sense,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 440b28f, “de An.” 421a7f, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.1.1f}}
**II** **the sense of smell,** ={ὄσφρησις}, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 11, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} —The older form ὀδμή (compare ὄδωδα, ὄζ-ω, **od-or**) is alone used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, and 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}}; it occurs also in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 115 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus}} [same place], and in later Prose, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.14.1, +others, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 63 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, Diocl.fragment 129, 1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 34.38, etc.}}; but ὀσμή is _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 71}}; also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs|Hippon.in “PSI” 9.1089.11.}}

3745:
** ὅσος**,
_Epic dialect_ **ὅσσος**, η, ον, both forms in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}; ὅσσος also in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 864 (Lyric poetry)}}; and in many dialects, e. g. Lesb., {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15}} ii 18 (ὄσσος), argument, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 4.748.5 (Troezen, 4th c.BC), Thess., [prev. work] 9(2).517.19, +others}}; Central Cretan dialect **ὄζος** {{Refs|“GDI” 5090 (from Lyttos), +others}}, and **ὄττος** {{Refs|[prev. work]5000 (from Gortyn): Relat. and indirect _interrogative_}} _adjective_:—of Size, **as great as, how great**; of Quantity, **as much as, how much**; of Space, **as far as, how far**; of Time, **as long as, how long**; of Number, **as many as, how many**; of Sound, **as loud as, how loud**: _correlative_ with τόσος (τόσσος), τοσόσδε, τοσοῦτος, in sense **as,** τόσσον χρόνον ὅσσον ἄνωγας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.670, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.169; τόσονδ᾽, ὅσον.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 286}}; τοσοῦτον ὄχλον καὶ παρασκευήν, ὅσην.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.35: sometimes with πᾶς or ἅπας}} as antecedent, χῶρον ἅπαντα ὅσσον.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.190; ἐκ πασέων, ὅσσαι.. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.723; πάντα μάλ᾽ ὅσσα.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.115; τοὺς πάντας.., ὅσοι.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 976, etc.; also ὅσων.. ψαύοιμι, πάντων τῶνδ᾽ ἀεὶ μετειχέτην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1464: with ἴσος}} just so much **as,** ἐμοὶ δ᾽ ἴσον τῇς χώρας μέτα, ὅσονπερ ὑμῖν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 174, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.44: frequently without antecedent, τῆς ἦ τοι φωνὴ μὲν ὅση σκύλακος νεογιλῆς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.86, compare 10.113, etc.}}; ἀσπίδες ὅσσαι ἄρισται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.371, compare 75, 18.512}}; agreeing with an antecedent implied in an _adjective_, γυναικείας ἀρετῆς, ὅσαι.. the virtue **of all the women, who..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, compare ὅς 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.1: the}} _substantive_ frequently precedes, where we put it in the Relat. clause, οὐδέ τι οἶδε πένθεος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(about the woe), ὅσσον ὄρωρε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.658}}; ὁρᾷς.. τὴν θεῶν ἰσχύν, ὅση [ἐστί]; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 118}}; ὦ Ζεῦ.., τὸ χρῆμα τῶν κόπων ὅσον! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1278}}; τὸ χρῆμα τῶν νυκτῶν ὅσον· ἀπέραντον! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 2: and sometimes it is attracted to the case of the antecedent, εὐτραφέστατον πωμάτων ὅσων ἵησιν (for ὅσα) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 309 (Lyric poetry); joined with οἷος, ὅσσος ἔην οἷός τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.630; so ὅσσοι τε καὶ οἵτινες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.236: repeated in the same clause, τὸ δὲ ὅσον μέτρον ὅσοις [μειγνύμενον]}} the quantities of the first ingredient and the others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 68b; γαίης ὅσσης ὅσσον ἔχει μόριον “Anthologia Graeca” 7.740 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus): perhaps sometimes followed by a participle for a finite Verb, ὅσοι συμπαρεπόμενοι (assuming variant) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.12, compare “HG” 6.1.10.}}
**2** with a _partitive_ _genitive_ in the principal clause, Τρώων θάνον ὅσσοι ἄριστοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.13; ἄριστοι ἵππων, ὅσσοι ἔασιν 5.267; Περσῶν ὅσοιπερ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 441}}; οὔ τις.. ὀνόσσεται, ὅσσοι Ἀχαιοί of all the Achaeans, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.55}}; [τῶν στρατειῶν] ὅσαι τε καὶ μὴ ἐπικίνδυνοι which are and which are not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467d}}; on τῶν ὅσοι, see ὁ, ἡ, τό {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.}}
**3** of Time, ὅσαι ἡμέραι, ὅσα ἔτη, etc., see at {ὁσημέραι}.
**4** with τις, in indirect questions, ἰδώμεθα.. ὅσσος τις χρυσὸς.. ἔνεστιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.45; ὅσον τι δένδρον.. γίνεται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193}}; ὅσον τι ἐστί{{Refs|[prev. work] 185; ὅσοι τινὲς ἐόντες.. [prev. author] 7.102, etc.}}
**5** with _accusative_ of extent, λίμνη.. μέγαθος, ὅσηπερ ἡ ἐν Δήλῳ in size **as large as** that in Delos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.170, compare 175, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 423b.}}
**6** with _adjectives_ expressing Quantity, etc., both words being put in the same case, [πίθηκοι] ἄφθονοι ὅσοι.. γίνονται, i. e. in amazing numbers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.194}}; ὄχλος ὑπερφυὴς ὅσος prodigiously large, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 750; χρήματα θαυμαστὰ ὅσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 282c, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Halc.” 5, etc.}}; ἀπλάτων ὅσων, ἀμύθητα ὅσα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.3,91 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 9; ὀλίγους ὅσους τῶν κοφίνων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 1; ἐτόλμησαν ἐπιφύεσθαι ἡμεῖν πλεῖστοι ὅσοι “SIG” 888.65 (Thrace, 3rd c.AD)}}; μετὰ ἱδρῶτος θαυμαστοῦ ὅσου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 350d; διὰ μυρίων ὅσων 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 1.1}}: frequently in adverbial construction, θαυμαστὸν ὅσον ἐπιδιδόντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 150d; θ. ὅσον διαφέρει [prev. author] “La.” 184c; ἀμηχάνῳ δὴ ὅσῳ πλείονι [prev. author] “R.” 588a; τυτθὸν ὅσσον ἄπωθεν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.45; βαιὸν ὅσον παραβάς “Anthologia Graeca” 12.227 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**7** with _superlative_, ὅσας ἂν πλείστας δύνωνται καταστρέφεσθαι τῶν πολίων the most they possibly could.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.44, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21}}; also ὅσον τάχος **as** quickly **as possible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 727 (more frequently ὅσον τάχιστα, see infr. IV. 4)}}; ὅσον σθένος with all possible strength, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.42, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.589.}}
**8** with _infinitive_, **so much as is enough for..,** ὅσον ἀποζῆν **enough to** live off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2; ἐλείπετο τῆς νυκτὸς ὅσον.. διελθεῖν τὸ πεδίον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.5}}; εὐδαιμονίας τοσοῦτον, ὅσον δοκεῖν so much **as is enough for** appearance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1191 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 416e, etc.}}
**II** for ὅτι τοσοῦτος (see. οἷος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.2, 3, ὅς 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus IV. 3), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.75, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 74, etc.}}
**III** followed by Particles:
**III.1** ὅσος ἄν (κεν) **how great (many) soever,** with _subjunctive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.66, etc.}}
**III.2** ὅσος δή **of such and such a size** or **number** (but in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus merely strengthened for ὅσος, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.487, +others), κήρυγμα ἐποιήσατο.., ζημίην τοῦτον ὀφείλειν, ὅσην δὴ εἴπας}} naming **such and such an amount,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52}}; ἐπέταξε τοῖσι.. ἔθνεσι γυναῖκας.. κατιστάναι, ὅσας δὴ ἐπιτάσσων ordering **such and such a number,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 159}}; παρεσκευάζοντο ἐπὶ μισθῷ ὅσῳ δή for payment **of a certain amount,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.160; σιτία παρακαταλιπόντες ὅσων δὴ μηνῶν [prev. author] 4.151; so ὅσος δή κοτε [prev. author] 1.157}}; ὁσοσδηποτοῦν, in _plural_, **any number whatsoever,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 9.9, +others, 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 34; ὁσοσδηοῦν}}**however large,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.119a; ὅσος δή τις 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.45, 4.60.}}
**III.3** ὁσοσοῦν, _Ionic dialect_ -ῶν, **ever so small,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199}}: in _plural_, **however many,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265a41; see infr. IV. 6.}}
**III.4** ὅσοσπερ, **precisely as great as,** τοῦ μὲν χειμῶνός ἐστι [ὁ Ἴστρος] ὅσοσπέρ ἐστι **of its normal size,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.50, compare 2.170}}, etc.: in _plural_, **as many as,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 475, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 423, 441; ἔθνεα πάντα ὅσαπερ ἦγε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.87; ἅπαντα.., ὅσαπέρ γ᾽ ἔφασκον, κἄτι πολλῷ πλείονα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 806: but ὅσοσπερ can frequently hardly be distinguished from ὅσος, see above 1.2, 5, infr. IV. 1, 3, and 7}}; and this is still more the case with _Epic dialect_ ὅσος τε (compare ὅστε), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.113, +others}}
**IV** Adverbial usages of ὅσον and ὅσα:
**IV.1** **so far as, so much as,** οὐ μέντοι ἐγὼ τόσον αἴτιός εἰμι, ὅσσον οἱ ἄλλοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.371: with}}_infinitive_, ὅσον αὔξειν ἢ καθαιρεῖν **so far as** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1376a34: in parenthesis, with}}_infinitive_, ὅσον γέ μ᾽ εἰδέναι **as far as** I know, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1252, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 145a, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.59}}; so μακραίων γ᾽, ὅσ᾽ ἀπεικάσαι conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 152 (Lyric poetry); ὅσον ἐς Ἑλλάδα γλῶσσαν ἀπὸ Λατίνης μεταβαλεῖν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.11}}: but more frequently with _indicative_, ὅσσον ἔγωγε γιγνώσκω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.222, compare 20.360; so ὅσονπερ ἂν σθένω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 946; ὅσα γε.. ἦν εἰκάσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.46; ὅσον δυνατόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 196d, etc.}}; ὅσον καθ᾽ ἕν᾽ ἄνδρα **so far as** was in one man's power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.153; ὅσον τὸ σὸν μέρος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1509: with _genitive_, ὅσον γε δυνάμεως παρ᾽ ἐμοί ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 422c, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1239; also ὅσα ἐγὼ μέμνημαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.21; οἱ πατέρες, ὅσα ἄνθρωποι, οὐκ ἀμαθεῖς ἔσονται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467c}}; ὅσα γε τἀνθρώπεια **humanly** speaking, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cri.” 47a.}}
**IV.1.b** **how far, how much,** ἴστε γὰρ ὅσσον ἐμοὶ ἀρετῇ περιβάλλετον ἵπποι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.276; μαθήσεται ὅσον τό τ᾽ ἄρχειν καὶ τὸ δουλεύειν δίχα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 927}}: with _adjectives_, **how,** ὅσον or ὅσσον.. μέγ᾽ ὄνειαρ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 41, 346; ὅσ᾽ ἤπειρος πολλὰ τρέφει [prev. author] “Th.” 582.}}
**IV.2** **only so far as, only just,** ὅσον ἐς Σκαιάς τε πύλας καὶ φηγὸν ἵκανεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.354; ὅσον ἐκ Φοινίκης ἐς Κρήτην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.45; φιλοσοφίας, ὅσον παιδείας χάριν, μετέχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 485a, compare “R.” 403d; οὐδὲν ἡδέως ποιεῖ γὰρ οὗτος, ἀλλ᾽ ὅσον νόμου χάριν 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.14, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1076a27, +others}}; ὅσον καὶ ἀπὸ βοῆς ἕνεκα ὠργίζετο, opposed to τῷ ἀληθεῖ ἐχαλέπαινον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92: so, more fully, ὅσον μοῦνον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.20, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.105, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607a, etc.; or μόνον ὅσον [prev. author] “Lg.” 778c; ἐγὼ μέν μιν οὐκ εἶδον εἰ μὴ ὅσον γραφῇ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.20; σιτάρια μικρὰ προσφέρων οἴνου θ᾽ ὅσον ὀσμήν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 98.3}}; τί οὐκ ἀπεκοιμήθημεν ὅ. ὅ. στίλη; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 213; ἢ ὅσον ὅσσον στιγμή “Anthologia Graeca” 7.472 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus), compare 5.254 (Paul. Sil.)}}; ἐπαναγαγεῖν ὅ. ὅ. [{{NT|NT.Luke.5.3 (codex D, variant{ὀλίγον})}}]; ὅσον· ὀλίγον, ὅσον ὅσον δέ, ὀλίγον ὀλίγον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; παρ᾽ ὅσον ἧττον **a little** less, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 631.17 (= παρ᾽ ὀλίγον ἧττον, Scholia)}}; οὐδ᾽ ὅσον **not even,** οὐδ᾽ ὅ. ἀττάραγόν τυ δεδοίκαμες {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 47.9}}: _absolutely_, **not the least mite,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ap.” 37, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.181, 190; οὐδέ περ ὅσσον [prev. author] 3.519; οὐδ᾽ ὅσον ὅσσον 4th-3rd c.BC: Philetas 7}}; cf. IV. 5.
**IV.3** of size or distance, ὅσον τε **about, nearly,** ὅσον τ᾽ ὄργυιαν, ὅσον τε πυγούσιον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.325, 10.517; ὅσον τ᾽ ἐπὶ ἥμισυ 13.114, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.351; ὅσον τε δέκα στάδια 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.57; ξύλα ὅσον τε διπήχεα [prev. author] 2.96, compare 78; so ὅσονπερ τρία στάδια [prev. author] 9.51}}; in _Attic dialect_ ὅσον alone, ὅσον δύο πλέθρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.38; ὅσον δύ᾽ ἢ τρία στάδια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229c; ὅσον παρασάγγην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.28}}; so of other measurements, ὅσον τριχοίνικον ἄρτον {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.3.23.}}
**IV.4** with _adjectives_ of Quality or Degree, mostly with _comparative_, αἴθ᾽, ὅσον ἥσσων εἰμί, τόσον σέο φέρτερος εἴην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.722, compare 1.186}}; ὅσσον βασιλεύτερός εἰμι **so far as, inasmuch as** I am a greater king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|9.160: and with _superlative_, γνώσετ᾽.., ὅσον εἰμὶ θεῶν κάρτιστος 8.17, compare 1.516, etc.: with Advs., ὅσον τάχιστα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 772, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1103, “El.” 1433; ὅσον μάλιστα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 524; ὅσα ἐδύνατο μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185.}}
**IV.5** with _negatives_, ὅσον οὐ or ὁσονού **just not, all but**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(cf. IV. 2), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36, 5.59, etc.}}; ὅσον οὐκ ἤδη **almost** immediately, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 141 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.96; later ὅσον ἤδη 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.4.4, 8.34.8; ὅσον οὔπω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1076, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.125, 6.34: ὅσον οὐδέπω}} with _future_, **presently, in a minute,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.8, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.31, +others}}
**IV.5.b** οὐχ ὅσον οὐκ ἠμύναντο, ἀλλ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἐσώθησαν **not only not.., but not even,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.62.}}
**IV.5.c** ὅσον μή **so far as not, save** or **except so far as,** καλός τε κἀγαθὸς τὴν φύσιν, ὅσον μὴ ὑβριστής (sic to be read) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 273a}}; ὅσον γ᾽ ἂν αὐτὸς μὴ ποτιψαύων **so far as I can** without touching.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1214; ὅσον μὴ χερσὶ καίνων [prev. author] “OT” 347; ὅσα μή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.111, 4.16}}: sometimes with a finite Verb, πείθεσθαι.., ὅσον ἂν μὴ ἀνάγκη ᾖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 83a; compare ὅ τι 11.}}
**IV.6** ὁσονοῦν, _Ionic dialect_ ὁσονῶν, **ever so little,** εἰ τοίνυν ἐχιόνιζε καὶ ὁσονῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22; so ἐφ᾽ ὁσονοῦν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.7.5, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.14 P.}}
**IV.7** ὅσα and ὅσαπερ, just like{ὡς, ὥσπερ}, **as,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.12, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.24, etc.}}
**V** ὅσῳ, ὅσῳπερ, **by how much,** frequently with _comparative_, ὅσῳ πλέον ἥμισυ παντός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 40; ὅσῳ κρείττω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 488.3; ὅσῳ ἂν πλεονάκις εἰσίῃς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.14}}: with _superlative_, διέδεξε, ὅσῳ ἐστὶ τοῦτο ἄριστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 59, 1050.}}
**V.2** ὅσῳ with _comparative_ when followed by another _comparative_ with τοσούτῳ, **the more.., so much the more**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.80; ὅ. μᾶλλον πιστεύω, τοσούτῳ μᾶλλον ἀπορῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 368b: with τοσούτῳ omitted, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1419, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 792}}: sometimes a _superlative_ replaces the _comparative_, ὅσῳ μάλιστα ἐλεύθεροι.., τοσούτῳ καὶ θρασύτατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.84, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.39}}; ὅσῳ alone, ἑωυτοὺς δὲ γενέσθαι τοσούτῳ.., ἀμείνονας, ὅσῳ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.137, compare 5.49, 8.13; νιν τῶνδε πλεῖστον ᾤκτισα.., ὅσῳπερ καὶ φρονεῖν οἶδεν μόνη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 313, compare “OC” 743.}}
**VI** ἐς ὅσον, ἐφ᾽ ὅσον, καθ᾽ ὅσον are frequently used much like{ὅσον, εἰς ὅσον σθένω} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1403 (troch.); ἐφ᾽ ὅσον ἐδύνατο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.4; εἰς ὅσον δύνανται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607a; καθ᾽ ὅσον δυνατόν [prev. author] “Ti.” 51b}}; ἐφ᾽ ὅσον ἐστὶν δυνατός **as far as** he can, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 22.903.11 (2nd c.BC)}}; later of Time, ἐς ὅσον δύναμίς μοι ὑπῆρχεν **as long as..** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 899.8 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; ἐφ᾽ ὅσον περιῆσαν **as long as** they lived, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 31i23 (2nd c.BC).}}
**VI.2** ἐν ὅσῳ **while,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 943 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87.}}
**VII** no _adverb_ ὅσως occurs.

3746:
** ὅσπερ**, ἥπερ, ὅπερ
(_Epic dialect_ ὅπερ as _masculine_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.114, 21.107)}}; _genitive_ οὗπερ, _Epic dialect_ οἷόπερ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1325}}; in _Ionic dialect_ writers and in Poets the _oblative_ cases are sometimes borrowed from the _Article_, τόπερ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.1098; τοῦπερ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 779; τῇπερ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.603, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30; τοίπερ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.130; τάπερ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 418 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.16; τῶνπερ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.638, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 974}}; on concord and _construction_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare ὅς 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.1,2,4, 11.1a}},3, IV. 4:—**the very man who, the very thing which**; frequently indistinguishable from simple ὅς,{{Refs|compare ὅσος 111.4: with words intervening between ὅς}} and περ, ὅς ῥ᾽ ἔβαλέν περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.524, compare 13.101, etc.}}
**2** used after _adjectives_ of resemblance, ὡυτὸς ὅσπερ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.42; ἐκ τῶν ἴσων.., ὧνπερ αὐτὸς ἐξέφυ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1499}}; ὅμοια ἔπραττον ἅπερ ἄν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.34}}; ὅρκια τάπερ τε.. **the same as**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74.}}
**II** special uses of cases,
**II.1** ὅπερ, **wherefore,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.18 codices (assuming variant)}}; **although,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 103.7.}}
**II.2** ἅπερ, **as, like,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 381 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 131, 660, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 168 (_anapaest meter_, as variant), “OT” 175 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.18, etc.}}; compare καθάπερ.
**II.3** οὗπερ, see at {ὅς} A b. {{Refs|1.}}
**II.4** ᾗπερ, _Doric dialect_ ᾇπερ, **which way, where, whither,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.41, 12.33, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.10, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ τῇπερ, ἔθαψαν.. τῇπερ ἔπεσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30}}; also, **as,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.286, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 440 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 364; ᾗπερ δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.310; ᾗπερ καί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.2.}}
**II.5** in the Logic of Aristotle, ὅπερ ἐστί, or ὅπερ alone, has two senses:
**II.5.a** non-technical, and unemphatic, **what** (a thing) **is,** ἑκάστη οὐσία τοῦθ᾽ ὅ. ἐστίν, οὐ λέγεται μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον each substance is called **what** it **is** without the difference of more or less, {{Refs|“Cat.” 3b36}}; τὸ διπλάσιον τοῦθ᾽ ὅ. ἐστίν, ἑτέρου λέγεται the double is called **what** it **is** (_i.e. _ the double) of something, i.e. is relative, {{Refs|[prev. work]6a39.}}
**II.5.b** expressing identity, οὔτε ἡ χιὼν ὅ. λευκόν snow is not **what** white **is,** i.e. is not identical with white, {{Refs|“Top.” 120b23}}; ὁ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος οὐκ ἔστιν ὅ. χρῶμα {{Refs|[prev. work]116a27: hence, to indicate the}}**precise** or **essential** nature of a thing, οὐ γὰρ ἂν φαίη ὅ. κακόν τι εἶναι τὴν ἡδονήν he would not say that pleasure is **essentially** something bad, {{Refs|“EN” 1153b6}}; τὰ μὲν οὐσίαν σημαίνοντα ὅ. ἐκεῖνό ἢ ὅ. ἐκεῖνό τι σημαίνει expressions which show the essence show **precisely what** the thing in question **is** or **precisely** of what kind it **is** (i.e. indicate either its species or its genus), {{Refs|“APo.” 83a24}}; ὅ. [τόδε] τί ἐστι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι the essence of a thing **is precisely** a 'this', i.e. a fully specified particular, {{Refs|“Metaphorical” 1030a3}}; ἡ μὲν [ἐπιστήμη] ὅ. ἀνθρώπου ἐστίν knowledge (that man is an animal) is apprehension that 'animal' is **an element in the essential nature** of man, {{Refs|“APo.” 89a35.}}

3747:
** ὀστέον**, τό,
_Attic dialect_ _contraction_ **ὀστοῦν**, _poetry_ **ὀστεῦν** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.480 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ perhaps **ὄστιον** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Oxy.” 2081}} (**d)fragment **5: _plural_ ὀστέα, _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ ὀστᾶ, late _Epic dialect_ ὀστά [ᾰ] {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.268, epigram in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.63, “epigram Gr.” 517.7 (from Edessa)}}; _Doric dialect_ ὀστία {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.61}}; but Trag. and Comedy texts use _genitive_ _plural_ ὀστέων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 367 (codices 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 769, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1226}}, and it is so written in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1177 where metre requires ὀστῶν}}: and the un _contraction_ forms generally occur in later Prose, as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus (see. below); _nominative_ ὀστέον “PLit.Lond.” 167.17 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; _dative_ _plural_ ὀστέοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 39}}; _Epic dialect_ _genitive_ _plural_ ὀστεόφιν (see. below):—**bone**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.460, +others) and 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus (“VC” 1, +others); 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} only in _plural_, {{Refs|“Th.” 540, +others}}; λεύκ᾽ ὀστέα **the bleached bones of the dead**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.161, etc.; σάρκας τε καὶ ὀστέα 9.293}}; πολὺς δ᾽ ἀμφ᾽ ὀστεόφιν θίς a huge heap **of bones** around,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|12.45; ῥινὸν ἀπ᾽ ὀστεόφιν ἐρύσαι 14.134; γυμνοῦσι τὰ ὀστέα τῶν κρεῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.61}}; ὀστέων στέγαστρον, of the skin, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 367; ἀρχὴ τῶν ὀστῶν ἡ καλουμένη ῥάχις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 54b11}}; especially of the cranium, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 2, +others, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.185.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, γῆς ὀστέοισιν ἐγχριμφθεὶς πόδα, i.e. **rocks**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Choerilus Tragicus 2 (ὀστοῖσιν Nauck).}}
**III** **stone** of fruit, ὀστῶν περσεΐνων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 176.168 (3rd c.BC),compare 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.66, “Geoponica” 10.13.3, +others, Scholia 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 99. [Accent ὀστέον Hdn.Gr. 2.943, but ὄστεον Anonymus cited in Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.793}}.] (Cf. Sanskrit _´sthi_, _genitive_ **asthn´s** 'bone', etc.)

3748:
** ὅστις**, ἥτις, ὅ τι
(sometimes written ὅ, τι to distinct it from ὅτι, **that**): {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has also the _masculine_ collateral form ὅτις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.47, +others (also in 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 2.9}} and _Ionic dialect_ and {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus Prose, “Jahresh.” 12.136 (from Erythrae), “IG” 12(5).22 (from Ios), 5(2).343.34 (Orchom. 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus))}} and the _neuter_ ὅττι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.402, +others, compare ὄττι 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 45.}}—In some forms only the second part is inflected, _i.e. _ _genitive_ ὅτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23, +others}}, _Epic dialect_ ὅττεο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.124, later _Ionic dialect_ ὅτεο “Jahresh.”}}[same place], _contraction_ ὅττευ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.121, ὅτευ [prev. work] 421, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7; Lesb. ὄττω 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.3; _dative_ ὅτῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36, +others}}; perhaps also in _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 2.5, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 99, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 14}}; _Epic dialect_ ὅτεῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.114}}, and as _disyllable_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.428, 15.664; so 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, +others, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 100, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 15, “SIG” 194.21 (Amphipolis, 4th c.BC); 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ὀσέοι “IG” 5(2).262.14 (Mantinea, 5th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ _accusative_ ὅτινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.204, 15.395; Delph. _genitive_ ὅτινος “IG” 22.1126.37 (4th c.BC), also “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.167 (from Cyrene)}}; Delph. _dative_ ὅτινι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1126.25; Cretan dialect _dative_ _sing._ ὄτιμι “Leg.Gort.” 7.51, 8.7, +others: _plural_, _nominative_ _masculine_ argument ὄττινες “Mnemos.” 44.65 (3rd c.BC)}}; _neuter_ ὅτινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.450; _genitive_ ὅτεων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.39, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65}}, _Attic dialect_ ὅτων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 414, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.24 (conjecture), “Oec.” 3.2 (conjecture) (also in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 238, 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 12, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 21); _dative_ ὁτέοισι (ν) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.491, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.82}}, _Attic dialect_ ὅτοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1335, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 758, ὅτοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1119; _accusative_ ὅτινας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.492}}, _Aeolic dialect_ ὄττινας {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 12: in a few forms only the first part is inflected, Cretan dialect _genitive_ _sing._ ὦτι probably in “Leg.Gort.” 1.5, 2.50, 11.50, +others, “GDI” 4993 ii 10: _neuter_ _plural_ ἄτι “Leg.Gort.” 2.47, +others}}: of the forms with double inflexion {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has only ὅν τινα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.188, +others, ἥν τινα 3.286, +others, οἵ τινες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.94, +others, οὕς τινας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.240, +others, ἅς τινας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.573}}; ᾧτινι first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 31, ἧστινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1358, ᾗ τινι δή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87, οἷστισι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1279: _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. have ἧστινος ᾗτινι along with _masculine_ and _neuter_ ὅτου ὅτῳ, and this rule holds with few exceptions in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ Prose before 4th c.BC}}; ᾡτινιοῦν occurs in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.37, etc.: ὅτῳ}} rarely as _feminine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1071.}}—For the _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ form **ἅσσα**, _Attic dialect_ ἅττα, see at {ἅσσα}.—On the concord and construction{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare ὅς 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.1,3, 11.3}}, 111.2a,b:—Radic. sense, **any one who, anything which, whosoever, whichsoever**; ὣς ἀπόλοιτο καὶ ἄλλος, ὅτις τοιαῦτά γε ῥέζοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.47; ἀθανάτων ὅς τίς σε φυλάσσει 15.35, etc.}}: frequently without express antecedent, χαίρει δέ μιν ὅς τις ἐθείρῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.347; ἆσσον ἴτω ὅς τις δέπας οἴσεται 23.667}}: hence frequently in maxims or sentiments, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅ. πάντ᾽ ἀνὴρ εὐδαιμονεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1217; μακάριος ὅ. οὐσίαν καὶ νοῦν ἔχει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 114}}; οὗτος βέλτιστος ἂν εἴη, ὅ... [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.4, etc.: frequently in such phrases as ὅστις εἶ, ὅστις ἐστί, see at {ὅς} 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.2}}]; ἔστιν ὅ., frequently with a _negative_, οὐ γὰρ ἔην ὅς τίς σφιν.. ἡγήσαιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.687; οὐκ ἔστιν ὅτῳ μείζονα μοῖραν νείμαιμ᾽ ἢ σοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989, 1070 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}; εἰσὶν οἵτινες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 354.5}}; οὐδὲν ὅ τι οὐ.. **everything**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.87:—in these phrases the case of ὅστις commonly depends on that of οὐδεί; but sometimes the reverse, see at {οὐδείς} I.2: also joined with _superlative_, τρόπῳ ὅτῳ ἂν δύνωνται ἰσχυροτάτῳ Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.23; ὅντινα ἀφανέστατον δύναιντο τρόπον 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.1.5: in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ sometimes strengthened by an antecedent πᾶς}}], but only in _singular_, ἅπας δὲ τραχὺς ὅ. ἂν νέον κρατῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 35, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.90 (πάντες ὅσοι being commonly used in _plural_, not πάντες οἵτινες}}; but πᾶσιν.. ὅστις ἐρωτᾷ {{Refs|“IG” 12.410).}}
**II** referring to a definite object, properly only when a general notion is implied, Πολυκράτεα.., δι᾽ ὅντινα κακῶς ἤκουσε, not **the man through whom**, but **one through whom**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.120}}; τελευταῖόν σε προσβλέψαιμι νῦν, ὅστις πέφασμαι φύς τ᾽ ἀφ᾽ ὧν οὐ χρῆν may I see thee now for the last time, I **who am one** born from sinful parentage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1184, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 38, “Ag.” 1065}}; but in quite definite sense, βωμόν, ὅστις νῦν ἔξω τῆς πόλεώς ἐστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.3: sometimes even with οὗτος or ὅδε as antecedent, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.167, 2.99, 6.47, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 943, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.87.}}
**II.2** ἐφ᾽ ὅτῳ, ={ἐφ᾽ ᾧτε}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.4; so ἐφ᾽ ὅτῳ τε “Delph.” 3(2).236 (2nd c.BC).}}
**III** in indirect questions, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc., εἴπ᾽ ἄγε μοι καὶ τόνδε.., ὅς τις ὅδ᾽ ἐστί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.192, compare 167, etc.}}; ἔσπετε νῦν μοι, Μοῦσαι, ὅς τις δὴ κτλ. **who it was that**..,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.509; ξεῖνος ὅδ᾽, οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὅς τις 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.28: in dialogue, when the person questioned repeats the question asked by τίς, as οὗτος τί ποιεῖ;—ὅ τι ποι; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 198}}; ἀλλὰ τίς γὰρ ε;—ὅστις**;** πολίτης χρηστός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 595, compare “Pl.” 462, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2c, etc.}}
**III.2** rare and late in direct questions, ὅ τι ἐστὶ τὸ ἐμποδίζον **;** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 140.12}}; ἀνθ᾽ ὅτου.. **;** = **why?** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 82p.109B.}}-C.; compare ὅπως.
**IV** limited or made more indefinite by the addition of Particles:
**IV.1** ὅστις γε **being one who** (compare ὅσγε), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1335, “OC” 810, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1184.}}
**IV.2** ὅστις δή (see. δή IV. {{Refs|1), frequently used without any distinct relative force, θεῶν ὅτεῳ δή}}**to some one or other** of the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86; ὅτευ δὴ χρήματος δεησόμενον [prev. author] 3.121; ᾗ τινι δὴ γνώμῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87, etc.; also ὅ τι δήκοτε πρήξοντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.134; ὅστις δήποτ᾽ ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 273c; ὡς ἀπετύγχαν᾽ ὁτουδήποτε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.167; ὁτῳδήτινι τρόπῳ “PFay.” 21.11 (2nd c.AD)}}; so
**IV.2.b** ὁστισοῦν, ὁτιοῦν **anybody (anything) whatsoever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198b, etc.; μετὰ ὁτουοῦν τρόπου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.27; ὁτῳοῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175a}}; εἷς ὁστισοῦν **any** one **person**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286a31: frequently with _negative_, μηδ᾽ ἂν ὁστισοῦν τυγχάνῃ ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 5e, compare “Phd.” 78d, etc.}}; οὐδ᾽ ὁτιοῦν not **the least mite**, nothing **whatsoever**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 344, “Pl.” 385; μηδοτιοῦν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 64}}: rarely, = **whoever (whatever**), as subject of a verb, ὁτιοῦν ἔτυχε τῶν ἐπὶ μέρους (variant{ὅτι ἄν}) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 391a22.}}
**IV.2.c** ὁστισδηποτοῦν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.8, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.164.}}
**IV.2.d** so also ὅστις alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 282d, etc.: with _negative_, μηδὲ οἵτινες}} none **at all**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.9; οὐδ᾽ ἧστινος ἂν ἀσχολίας τὸ πρᾶγμα προσεδεῖτο 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.14.6.}}
**IV.3** ὅστις ποτε **whoever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 160 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65.}}
**IV.4** ὅστις περ (compare ὅσπερ), mostly in _neuter_, ὅ τι πέρ ἐστ᾽ ὄφελος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 53, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 492e: in _masculine_, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.225.}}
**IV.5** ὅστις τε, where τε is otiose as in ὅστε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.43, +others}}
**V** _neuter_ ὅ τι used _absolutely_ as a _conjunction_, see at {ὅ τι}.
**VI** ἐξ ὅτου **from which time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 345, “Tr.” 326, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 528, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.16, etc.; ἐξ ὅτου περ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 596}}; ἀπ᾽ ὅτευ **since**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7, compare “SIG” 45.18 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}; so ἕως ὅτου **until..,** {{NT|NT.Luke.13.8.}}
**VI.2** **from what cause**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 671, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 639.}}

3749:
** ὀστρᾰκ-ῐνος**, η, ον,
**earthen, made of clay**, of vessels, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 34, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 114, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.645 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus), 11.74 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus), NT.2Tim.2.20, NT.2Cor.4.7, “PLond.” 3.1177.95 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **like earthenware**, ὀστράκινατὸ δέρμα, ={ὀστρακόδερμα}, variant in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 6.}}

3750:
** ὄσφρ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**the sense of smell**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 111b (as variant), “Tht.” 156b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 421b23; τὸ τῆς ὀ. αἰσθητήριον [prev. author] “PA” 658b28; ῥινῶν ὀσφρήσιες 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.66.}}
**2** **the organ of smell**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 425a5, “Pr.” 907b28, NT.1Cor.12.17, “PRyl.” 63.5 (3rd c.AD)}}; αἱ ὀ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.12.2.}}

3751:
** ὀσφῦς**, ἡ,
_genitive_ ὀσφύος: _accusative_ ὀσφῦν, also ὀσφύα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.213 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus)}}: —**lion** or **loins, lower part of the back**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 80, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aët.” 20, “Aph.” 3.23, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493a22, +others}}; opposed to κῶλα, ὦμοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 497, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40}}; **loin** of a victim, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 57.9 (Milet., 5th c.BC), 1037.2 (4th-3rd c.BC), 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 23.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1053, “Lys.” 964, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 319.12}}; of wasps, ἔχουσι κέντρον ἐκ τῆς ὀ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 225, compare 740 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.12}}; of a fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 132.6 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**2** Hellenist., _metaphorically_, ὁ καρπὸς τῆς ὀ., of a son, {{LXX+NT|NT.Act.2.30, cf. LXX.Gen.35.11, +others}}
**3** περιζώννυσθαι or ἀναζώννυσθαι τὴν ὀσφύν gird up one's **loins**,{{LXX|LXX.Jer.1.17}}; βυσσίνῳ{{LXX+NT|LXX.Dan.10.5, cf. NT.Luke.12.35. [ῡ in _nominative_ and _accusative_ _sing._, which are accented ὀσφῦς, ὀσφῦν by Hdn.Gr. 2.937}}; ὀσφύς, -ύν frequently in codices, and this may be admitted for late writers: compare ὀφρῦς.]

3752:
** ὅταν**,
for ὅτ᾽ ἄν (ὅτε ἄν) as sometimes in codices: _adverb_ of Time,
**A** **whenever**, with a conditional force, so as nearly to ={ἐάν} (see. εἰ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus II)}}, referring to an _indefinite_ future {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (ὅτε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus I. IC), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.519, etc.}}; also of events like ly to recur,{{Refs|2.397, “IG” 12.97.9, etc.}}: also in _Epic dialect_ ὅτε κεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.567, 6.225: strengthened, ὅ. περ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 301, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 565a, 565d}}: repeated for _rhetorical_ effect, ὅ. ὡς ὑβρίζων, ὅ. ὡς ἐχθρὸς ὑπάρχων, ὅ. κονδύλοις, ὅ. ἐπὶ κόρρης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.72.}}
**A.b** later causal, **since**, ὅταν.. ᾖ **since** it is, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395a19, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 7.105, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Gaurum - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 11.2}}; in earlier examples the application to the particular case is less directly expressed, καὶ τοῦτο τυφλὸν ὅταν ἐγὼ βλέπω βραχύ this too (_i.e. _ my staff) is blind **when** I am (= **when** its owner is) short-sighted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 744, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 137 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 241a, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.9.}}
**A.2** never with _indicative_ in early authors, except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.410, ὡς δ᾽ ὅταν.. σκαίρουσι (assuming variant); in 24.88, ὅτε κεν.. ζώννυνταί τε νέοι καὶ ἐπεντύνονται ἄεθλα}}; and in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.41, ὡς δ᾽ ὅτ᾽ ἂν ἔν τε κύνεσσι καὶ ἀνδράσι.. στοέφεται (ἔναντα κύνεσσι conjecture Monro)}}: but frequently in LXX with _imperfect_ _indicative_, as ὅταν εἰσήρχετο {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Gen.38.9, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.32.5, NT.Mark.3.11}}: also with _aorist_ _indicative_, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.16.3, NT.Rev.8.1}}: with _future_ _indicative_, ὅταν ἕξουσι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 187.12}}; ὅταν ὄψεσθε (variant{ὄψησθε}) {{NT|NT.Luke.13.28}}: with _present_ _indicative_, ὅταν δείκνυται {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.3.27 (assuming variant): generally, ὅταν supersedes ὅτε in Hellenistic Greek.}}
**A.3** never with _optative_ in early authors, except in indirect speech, where in orat. recta the _subjunctive_ with ὅταν would have stood, as perhaps {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 450 may be explained (ὅτ᾽ ἐκνεῶν Elmsl.)}}; ὅτε κεν followed by ἵκοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.525.}}
**A.II** Special usages:
**A.II.1** to introduce a simile,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.5, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.394.}}
**A.II.2** πρίν γ᾽ ὅ., ={πρίν γε ἢ ὅ}. (see. ὅτε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.2), 2.374.}}
**A.II.3** εἰς ὅτε κεν until **such time as**.., {{Refs|[prev. work]99, 19.144.}}
**A.II.4** ὅ. τάχιστα, Latin _cum primum_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1205, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.33; ὅ. πρῶτον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 211b. [ὅτᾱν only in later Poetry, “Lyric poetry Alex. Adesp.” 37.17.]}}

3753:
** ὅτε**,
also {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria, “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.1}} H., _Doric dialect_ **ὅκα**, _Aeolic dialect_ **ὄτα** (which see), Relat. _adverb_, formed from the Relat. stem ὁ- and τε (see. τε B), answering to _demonstrative_ τότε and _interrogative_ πότ; properly of Time, but sometimes passing into a causal sense (compare ὁπότε).
**A** of Time, **when, at the time when**,
**A.I** Constr.:
**A.I.1** with _indicative_ to denote single events or actions in past time, with _imperfect_ or _aorist_, **when**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.397, 432, etc.: rarely with}}_pluperfect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.392: the Verb is sometimes to be repeated from the apodosis, Καλλίξενος δὲ κατελθών, ὅ. καὶ οἱ ἐκ Πειραιῶς (i.e. κατῆλθον) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.35: frequently in elliptically phrases, πῇ ἔβαν εὐχωλαί, ὅ. δὴ φάμεν εἶναι ἄριστοι}}; whither are gone the boasts, [**which we made] when** we said.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.229: so after Verbs of perception and the like, ἦ οὐ μέμνῃ, ὅ. τ᾽ ἐκρέμω..}}; rememberest thou not [**the time] when**.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.18, compare 21.396, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.115, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 354, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.21, etc.}}; ἄκουσα εὐχομένης ὅτ᾽ ἔφησθα.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.397, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 782c; οὐδ᾽ ἔλαθ᾽ Αἴαντα Ζεύς, ὅ. δὴ Τρώεσσι δίδου.. νίκην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.627.}}
**A.I.1.b** with _present_, of a thing always happening or now going on,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.471; νῦν, ὅ... σοι ὀξέως ὑπακούω 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.6; ᾔδεα μὲν γὰρ ὅ... Δαναοῖσιν ἄμυνεν, οἶδα δὲ νῦν ὅ. τοὺς.. κυδάνει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.71.}}
**A.I.1.c** rarely with _future_, of a definite future, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.272.}}
**A.I.2** with _optative_, to denote repeated events or actions in past time, ἔνθα πάρος κοιμᾶθ᾽, ὅ. μιν γλυκὺς ὕπνος ἱκάνοι **whenever, as often as**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.610, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.87, etc.; ὅ. δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.216.}}
**A.I.2.b** sometimes of future events which are represented as uncertain, in clauses dependent on a Verb in the _optative_ or _subjunctive_, οὐκ ἄν τοι χραίσμῃ κίθαρις.., ὅτ᾽ ἐν κονίῃσι μιγείης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3.55, compare 18.465, 21.429, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 726.}}
**A.I.2.c** ὅ. μή, in early authors always with _optative_, for εἰ μή, **unless, except, save when**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.319, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.197, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277a24: used by 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus with}}_subjunctive_, {{Refs|1.245, 4.409.}}
**A.I.3** with _subjunctive_, only in _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.259, 19.337, 21.323, etc., probably in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 766 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.II** Special usages:
**A.II.1** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus to introduce a simile, ὡς δ᾽ ὅτε}} **as when**, mostly with _subjunctive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.147, 4.130, 141, 6.506, +others: sometimes with _indicative_, 16.364, 21.12}}: the Verb must frequently be supplied from the context, as in {{Refs|2.394, 4.462.}}
**A.II.2** in the _Epic dialect_ phrase πρίν γ᾽ ὅτε δή.., ἤ is omitted before ὅτε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|9.488, 12.437, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.322.}}
**A.III** ὅτε with other Particles,
**A.III.1** ὅτ᾽ ἄν, ὅτε κεν, see at {ὅταν}.
**A.III.2** ὅτε δή and ὅτε δή ῥα, stronger than ὅτε, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, ὅτε δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.65, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 280, +others; ὅτε δή ῥα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.446, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 58, +others}}; see infr. IV. I; so ὅτ᾽ ἄρ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.540.}}
**A.III.3** ὅτε τε (where τε is otiose, see τε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus I) 2.471, 10.83, etc.}}
**A.III.4** ὅτε περ **even when**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.802, 14.319, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.99, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8, etc.; ὅτε πέρ τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.259, 10.7.}}
**A.IV** the proper _correlative_ _adverb_ is τότε, as ὅ. δὴ.., τότε δὴ{{Refs|.. 10.365; ὅ. δή ῥ᾽.., δὴ τότε 23.721}}; ὅ. δὴ.., καὶ τότε δὴ{{Refs|.. 22.208; ὅ. δή ῥα.., καὶ τότ᾽ ἄρ᾽ 24.31: for τότε we sometimes have ἔπειτα, 3.221; αὐτίκα δ᾽, 4.210; δὲ.., 5.438}}; also νῦν.., ὅ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 710 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; μεθύστερον, ὅ... {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 711}}; ἤματι τῷ, ὅ... {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.743, etc.}}; so in _Attic dialect_, ἦν ποτε χρόνος, ὅ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320c, compare “Phd.” 75a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.160.}}
**A.IV.2** elliptical in the phrase ἔστιν ὅ. or ἔσθ᾽ ὅ., **there are times when, sometimes, now and then**, ἔστι ὅ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.120}}; ἔστιν ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62a}}; ἔσθ᾽ ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56 (see. below c).}}
**B** ὅτε sometimes has a causal sense, **when, seeing that**, mostly with _present_ _indicative_, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.433 (variant{ὅ τε}) ὅ. δή 20.29}}]; and in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ Prose, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1095, “OT” 918, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 206b, “R.” 581e, “Prt.” 356c, “Sph.” 254b, etc.; so ὅ. γε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.ά}}: with _perfect_ used as _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 428, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 34.}}
**B.2** sometimes where ὥστε would be more usual, οὕτω.. πόρρω κλέος ἥκει, ὅ. καὶ βασιλεὺς ἠρώτησεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ach.” 647.}}
**C** **ὁτέ** Indef. _adverb_, **sometimes, now and then**, used like{ποτέ} at the beginning of each of two corresponding clauses, **now.., now.., sometimes.., sometimes**.. (not in early Prose, ὁτὲ μὲν.., ὁτὲ δὲ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1290a4, +others), ὁτὲ μὲν.., ἄλλοτε.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.49s q.; ὁτὲ μὲν.., ἄλλοτε δ᾽ αὖ.. 18.599f; ὁτὲ μέν τε.., ἄλλοτε δὲ.. 11.64}}; ὁτὲ μὲν.., ὁτὲ δ᾽ αὖτε.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1270}}; ὁτὲ μέν τε.., ὅτ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.1300}}; ὁτὲ μὲν.., ποτὲ δὲ.. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.20.8}}; ὁτὲ μὲν.., ὁτὲ δὲ.., καὶ ἄλλοτε.. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.106}}; ὁτὲ μὲν.., πάλιν δὲ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1100a28}}; ἐνίοτε μὲν.., ὁτὲ δὲ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 360b3}}; ὁτὲ μὲν.., ἢ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Po.” 1448a21 (assuming variant): also reversely, ἄλλοτε μὲν.., ὁτὲ δὲ.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.568}}; also ὁτὲ δέ in the second clause, without any correlative in the first,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.178; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus joins ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε.., ὅτ᾽ ἄλλοτ᾽ ἄλλον “Aj.” 56}}; ὁτὲ δέ alone, at the beginning of a clause, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.8 and 20, 9.8 and 20.}}

3754:
** ὅτῐ**,
_Epic dialect_ **ὅττῐ** (both in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _conjunction_, to introduce an objective clause, **that**, after Verbs of seeing or knowing, thinking or saying; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently strengthened ὅτι ῥα, and ὅτι δή:}} —Usage:
**I** when ὅτι introduces a statement of fact:
**I.a** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always with _indicative_, the tense following the same rules as in English, ἤγγειλ᾽ ὅττι ῥά οἱ πόσις ἔκτοθι μίμνε πυλάων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.439.}}
**I.b** in _Attic dialect_, ὅτι takes _indicative_ after primary tenses, _indicative_ or _optative_ after secondary tenses, e.g. ἐνδείκνυμαι ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι σοφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23b; ᾔσθετο ὅτι τὸ Μένωνος στράτευμα ἤδη ἐν Κιλικίᾳ ἦν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.21, compare 2.2.15, +others; ἔλεγον ὅτι οὐκ ὀρθῶς αἱ σπονδαὶ γένοιντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.61, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59e, etc.; ἠπείλησ᾽ ὅτι.. βαδιοίμην 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 88: the _indicative_ is frequently retained in the same tense which the speaker used or would have used, ἠγγέλθη.. ὅτι Μέγαρα ἀφέστηκε}} news came **that** Megara had (literal has) revolted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.114; ἀποκρινάμενοι ὅτι πέμψουσι [prev. work] 90}}: sometimes _optative_ and _indicative_ are found in the same sentence, ἔλεγον, ὅτι Κῦρος μὲν τέθνηκεν, Ἀριαῖος δὲ πεφευγὼς.. εἴη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.3; Περικλῆς.. προηγόρευε.. ὅτι Ἀρχίδαμος μέν οἱ ξένος εἴη.., τοὺς δ᾽ ἀγροὺς τοὺς ἑαυτοῦ καὶ οἰκίας.., ἀφίησιν αὐτὰ δημόσια εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc.: also ὅτι}}.. and the _accusative_ with _infinitive_ are found together, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.13.}}
**I.2** when ὅτι introduces a conditional sentence, the Constr. after ὅτι is the same as in independent conditional sentences, εἴ τις ἔροιτο, καθ᾽ ὁποίους νόμους δεῖ πολιτεύεσθαι, δῆλον ὅτι ἀποκρίναισθ᾽ ἄν.. it is manifest **that** you would answer.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.12, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.12.}}
**II** ὅτι is frequently inserted _pleonastic_ in introducing a quotation (where we use no _conjunction_ and put inverted commas), λόγον τόνδε ἐκφαίνει ὁ Πρωτεύς, λέγων ὅτι ἐγὼ εἰ μὴ περὶ πολλοῦ ἡγεύμην.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.115}}; καὶ ἐγὼ εἶπον, ὅ. ἡ αὐτή μοι ἀρχή ἐστι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 318a, compare 356a, 361a, etc.}}; even where the quotation consists of one word,{{Refs|[prev. work] 330c, “Men.” 74b, 74c.}}
**II.2** ὅ. is also used _pleonastic_ with the _infinitive_ and _accusative_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ὡς 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus I.I), εἶπον ὅτι πρῶτον ἐμὲ χρῆναι πειραθῆναι κατ᾽ ἐμαυτόν (which is in fact a mixture of two constructions) [prev. author] “Lg.” 892d, compare “Phd.” 63c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.2, etc.}}; but ὅτι has frequently been wrongly inserted by the copyists, as if εἶπεν or λέγουσιν must be followed by it, as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.37 (om. Papyrus), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.1, etc.}}
**III** ὅτι in _Attic dialect_ frequently represents a whole sentence, especially in affirmative answers, οὐκοῦν.. τὸ ἀδικεῖν κάκιον ἂν εἴη τοῦ ἀδικεῖσθαι. Answ. δῆλον δὴ ὅτι (i.e. ὅτι κάκιον ἂν εἴη, or ὅτι ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 475c}}; compare οἶδ᾽ ὅτι, ἴσθ᾽ ὅτι, οἶσθ᾽ ὅτι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 276, 758, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 486a, etc.: hence arose the practice of using δηλονότι (which see) as}} _adverb_
**III.2** what we make the subject of the Verb which follows ὅτι frequently stands in the preceding clause, Αυκάονας δὲ καὶ αὐτοὶ εἴδομεν, ὅτι.. καρποῦνται (for εἴδομεν, ὅτι Λυκάονες καρποῦνται) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.23, compare 3.2.29, etc.}}
**IV** ὅτι sometimes = **with regard to the fact that**, ὅτι.. οὔ φησι.. ὄνομα εἶναι, ὑποπτεύω αὐτὸν σκώπτειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 384c, compare “Prt.” 330e, etc.}}
**V** οὐχ ὅ..., ἀλλὰ or ἀλλὰ καὶ.., οὐχ ὅ. ὁ Κρίτων ἐν ἡσυχίᾳ ἦν, ἀλλὰ οἱ φίλοι αὐτοῦ **not only** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Crito Medicus}}.., but his friends, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.8; more fully, οὐ μόνον ὅ. ἄνδρες, ἀλλὰ καὶ αἱ γυναῖκες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 179b: so followed by ἀλλ᾽ οὐδὲ.., ταύτῃ ἀδύνατα ἐξισοῦσθαι οὐχ ὅ. τὰ ἐν τῇ Εὐρώπῃ, ἀλλ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἐν τῇ Ἀσίᾳ}} **not only** the powers in Europe, but.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97: οὐχ ὅ}}., not followed by a second clause, means **although**, οὐχ ὅ. παίζει καί φησι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 336d, compare “Grg.” 450e, “Tht.” 157b; compare ὅπως 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus II. 2.}}
**V.2** for ὅτι μή, see at {ὅ τι} 11.
**B** as a causal Particle, **for that, because**, generally after Verbs of feeling, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.56, 14.407, +others; οὐδὲν ἐκπλαγείς, ὅτι.. εἶδες 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.31a}}: but without such a Verb, ὃν περὶ πάσης τῖεν ὁμηλικίης, ὅτι οἱ φρεσὶν ἄρτια ᾔδη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.326, compare 9.76, +others; μάλιστα δ᾽ αὐτοὺς ἐπεκαλέσαντο ὅτι τειχομαχεῖν ἐδόκουν δυνατοὶ εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.102, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.75, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.231; so ὅτιπερ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.14.}}
**B.b** followed by τί, ὅτι τ; **why?** (literal **because why?**) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.214}}; ὅτι τί δ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 136, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 22}}; ὅτι δὴ τί μάλιστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 343a}}; ὅτι δὴ τί γ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 161c}}; compare ὁτιή.
**B.2** **seeing that**, in giving the reason for saying what is said, γλαυκὴ δέ σε τίκτε θάλασσα.. ὅτι τοι νόος ἐστὶν ἀπηνής **as is proved by the fact that**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.35, compare 21.488, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.36. [The last syllable is never elided in _Attic dialect_, probably to avoid confusion with ὅτε: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus ὅτ᾽}} (ὅ τ᾽) probably always represents ὅτε (ὅ τε): there are no examples of ὅττ᾽: hiatus after ὅτι is permitted in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 611, “Ach.” 516.]}}

3755:
Included with: ** ὅστις**, ἥτις, ὅ τι
(sometimes written ὅ, τι to distinct it from ὅτι, **that**): {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has also the _masculine_ collateral form ὅτις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.47, +others (also in 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 2.9}} and _Ionic dialect_ and {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus Prose, “Jahresh.” 12.136 (from Erythrae), “IG” 12(5).22 (from Ios), 5(2).343.34 (Orchom. 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus))}} and the _neuter_ ὅττι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.402, +others, compare ὄττι 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 45.}}—In some forms only the second part is inflected, _i.e. _ _genitive_ ὅτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23, +others}}, _Epic dialect_ ὅττεο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.124, later _Ionic dialect_ ὅτεο “Jahresh.”}}[same place], _contraction_ ὅττευ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.121, ὅτευ [prev. work] 421, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7; Lesb. ὄττω 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.3; _dative_ ὅτῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36, +others}}; perhaps also in _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 2.5, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 99, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 14}}; _Epic dialect_ ὅτεῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.114}}, and as _disyllable_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.428, 15.664; so 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, +others, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 100, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 15, “SIG” 194.21 (Amphipolis, 4th c.BC); 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ὀσέοι “IG” 5(2).262.14 (Mantinea, 5th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ _accusative_ ὅτινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.204, 15.395; Delph. _genitive_ ὅτινος “IG” 22.1126.37 (4th c.BC), also “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.167 (from Cyrene)}}; Delph. _dative_ ὅτινι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1126.25; Cretan dialect _dative_ _sing._ ὄτιμι “Leg.Gort.” 7.51, 8.7, +others: _plural_, _nominative_ _masculine_ argument ὄττινες “Mnemos.” 44.65 (3rd c.BC)}}; _neuter_ ὅτινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.450; _genitive_ ὅτεων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.39, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65}}, _Attic dialect_ ὅτων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 414, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.24 (conjecture), “Oec.” 3.2 (conjecture) (also in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 238, 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 12, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 21); _dative_ ὁτέοισι (ν) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.491, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.82}}, _Attic dialect_ ὅτοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1335, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 758, ὅτοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1119; _accusative_ ὅτινας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.492}}, _Aeolic dialect_ ὄττινας {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 12: in a few forms only the first part is inflected, Cretan dialect _genitive_ _sing._ ὦτι probably in “Leg.Gort.” 1.5, 2.50, 11.50, +others, “GDI” 4993 ii 10: _neuter_ _plural_ ἄτι “Leg.Gort.” 2.47, +others}}: of the forms with double inflexion {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has only ὅν τινα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.188, +others, ἥν τινα 3.286, +others, οἵ τινες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.94, +others, οὕς τινας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.240, +others, ἅς τινας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.573}}; ᾧτινι first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 31, ἧστινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1358, ᾗ τινι δή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87, οἷστισι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1279: _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. have ἧστινος ᾗτινι along with _masculine_ and _neuter_ ὅτου ὅτῳ, and this rule holds with few exceptions in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ Prose before 4th c.BC}}; ᾡτινιοῦν occurs in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.37, etc.: ὅτῳ}} rarely as _feminine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1071.}}—For the _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ form **ἅσσα**, _Attic dialect_ ἅττα, see at {ἅσσα}.—On the concord and construction{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare ὅς 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.1,3, 11.3}}, 111.2a,b:—Radic. sense, **any one who, anything which, whosoever, whichsoever**; ὣς ἀπόλοιτο καὶ ἄλλος, ὅτις τοιαῦτά γε ῥέζοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.47; ἀθανάτων ὅς τίς σε φυλάσσει 15.35, etc.}}: frequently without express antecedent, χαίρει δέ μιν ὅς τις ἐθείρῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.347; ἆσσον ἴτω ὅς τις δέπας οἴσεται 23.667}}: hence frequently in maxims or sentiments, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅ. πάντ᾽ ἀνὴρ εὐδαιμονεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1217; μακάριος ὅ. οὐσίαν καὶ νοῦν ἔχει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 114}}; οὗτος βέλτιστος ἂν εἴη, ὅ... [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.4, etc.: frequently in such phrases as ὅστις εἶ, ὅστις ἐστί, see at {ὅς} 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.2}}]; ἔστιν ὅ., frequently with a _negative_, οὐ γὰρ ἔην ὅς τίς σφιν.. ἡγήσαιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.687; οὐκ ἔστιν ὅτῳ μείζονα μοῖραν νείμαιμ᾽ ἢ σοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989, 1070 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}; εἰσὶν οἵτινες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 354.5}}; οὐδὲν ὅ τι οὐ.. **everything**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.87:—in these phrases the case of ὅστις commonly depends on that of οὐδεί; but sometimes the reverse, see at {οὐδείς} I.2: also joined with _superlative_, τρόπῳ ὅτῳ ἂν δύνωνται ἰσχυροτάτῳ Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.23; ὅντινα ἀφανέστατον δύναιντο τρόπον 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.1.5: in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ sometimes strengthened by an antecedent πᾶς}}], but only in _singular_, ἅπας δὲ τραχὺς ὅ. ἂν νέον κρατῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 35, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.90 (πάντες ὅσοι being commonly used in _plural_, not πάντες οἵτινες}}; but πᾶσιν.. ὅστις ἐρωτᾷ {{Refs|“IG” 12.410).}}
**II** referring to a definite object, properly only when a general notion is implied, Πολυκράτεα.., δι᾽ ὅντινα κακῶς ἤκουσε, not **the man through whom**, but **one through whom**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.120}}; τελευταῖόν σε προσβλέψαιμι νῦν, ὅστις πέφασμαι φύς τ᾽ ἀφ᾽ ὧν οὐ χρῆν may I see thee now for the last time, I **who am one** born from sinful parentage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1184, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 38, “Ag.” 1065}}; but in quite definite sense, βωμόν, ὅστις νῦν ἔξω τῆς πόλεώς ἐστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.3: sometimes even with οὗτος or ὅδε as antecedent, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.167, 2.99, 6.47, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 943, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.87.}}
**II.2** ἐφ᾽ ὅτῳ, ={ἐφ᾽ ᾧτε}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.4; so ἐφ᾽ ὅτῳ τε “Delph.” 3(2).236 (2nd c.BC).}}
**III** in indirect questions, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc., εἴπ᾽ ἄγε μοι καὶ τόνδε.., ὅς τις ὅδ᾽ ἐστί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.192, compare 167, etc.}}; ἔσπετε νῦν μοι, Μοῦσαι, ὅς τις δὴ κτλ. **who it was that**..,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.509; ξεῖνος ὅδ᾽, οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὅς τις 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.28: in dialogue, when the person questioned repeats the question asked by τίς, as οὗτος τί ποιεῖ;—ὅ τι ποι; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 198}}; ἀλλὰ τίς γὰρ ε;—ὅστις**;** πολίτης χρηστός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 595, compare “Pl.” 462, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2c, etc.}}
**III.2** rare and late in direct questions, ὅ τι ἐστὶ τὸ ἐμποδίζον **;** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 140.12}}; ἀνθ᾽ ὅτου.. **;** = **why?** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 82p.109B.}}-C.; compare ὅπως.
**IV** limited or made more indefinite by the addition of Particles:
**IV.1** ὅστις γε **being one who** (compare ὅσγε), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1335, “OC” 810, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1184.}}
**IV.2** ὅστις δή (see. δή IV. {{Refs|1), frequently used without any distinct relative force, θεῶν ὅτεῳ δή}}**to some one or other** of the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86; ὅτευ δὴ χρήματος δεησόμενον [prev. author] 3.121; ᾗ τινι δὴ γνώμῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87, etc.; also ὅ τι δήκοτε πρήξοντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.134; ὅστις δήποτ᾽ ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 273c; ὡς ἀπετύγχαν᾽ ὁτουδήποτε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.167; ὁτῳδήτινι τρόπῳ “PFay.” 21.11 (2nd c.AD)}}; so
**IV.2.b** ὁστισοῦν, ὁτιοῦν **anybody (anything) whatsoever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198b, etc.; μετὰ ὁτουοῦν τρόπου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.27; ὁτῳοῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175a}}; εἷς ὁστισοῦν **any** one **person**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286a31: frequently with _negative_, μηδ᾽ ἂν ὁστισοῦν τυγχάνῃ ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 5e, compare “Phd.” 78d, etc.}}; οὐδ᾽ ὁτιοῦν not **the least mite**, nothing **whatsoever**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 344, “Pl.” 385; μηδοτιοῦν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 64}}: rarely, = **whoever (whatever**), as subject of a verb, ὁτιοῦν ἔτυχε τῶν ἐπὶ μέρους (variant{ὅτι ἄν}) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 391a22.}}
**IV.2.c** ὁστισδηποτοῦν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.8, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.164.}}
**IV.2.d** so also ὅστις alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 282d, etc.: with _negative_, μηδὲ οἵτινες}} none **at all**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.9; οὐδ᾽ ἧστινος ἂν ἀσχολίας τὸ πρᾶγμα προσεδεῖτο 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.14.6.}}
**IV.3** ὅστις ποτε **whoever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 160 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65.}}
**IV.4** ὅστις περ (compare ὅσπερ), mostly in _neuter_, ὅ τι πέρ ἐστ᾽ ὄφελος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 53, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 492e: in _masculine_, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.225.}}
**IV.5** ὅστις τε, where τε is otiose as in ὅστε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.43, +others}}
**V** _neuter_ ὅ τι used _absolutely_ as a _conjunction_, see at {ὅ τι}.
**VI** ἐξ ὅτου **from which time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 345, “Tr.” 326, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 528, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.16, etc.; ἐξ ὅτου περ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 596}}; ἀπ᾽ ὅτευ **since**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7, compare “SIG” 45.18 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}; so ἕως ὅτου **until..,** {{NT|NT.Luke.13.8.}}
**VI.2** **from what cause**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 671, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 639.}}

3756:
** οὐ**,
the negative of **fact** and **statement**, as μή of **will** and **thought**; οὐ denies, μή rejects; οὐ is absolute, μή relative; οὐ objective, μή subjective. —The same differences hold for all compounds of οὐ and μή, and some examples of οὐδέ and οὐδείς are included below.—As to the Form, see infr. G. USAG{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}}
**I** as the negative of single words,
**II** as the negative of the sentence.
**I** οὐ adhering to single words so as to form a quasi-_compound_ with them:—with Verbs: οὐ δίδωμι **withhold**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.296}}; οὐκ εἰῶ **prevent**, {{Refs|2.132, 4.55, +others}}; οὐκ ἐθέλω **refuse**, {{Refs|1.112, 3.289, +others}}; οὔ φημι **deny**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|7.393, 23.668, +others (In most of these uses μή can replace οὐ when the _construction_ requires it, e.g. εἰ μή φησι ταῦτα ἀληθῆ εἶναι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 34}}; but sometimes οὐ is retained, εἰ δ᾽ ἂν.. οὐκ ἐθέλωσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.289; εἰ δέ κ᾽.. ου᾽κ εἰῶσι 20.139; ἐὰν οὐ φάσκῃ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.76}}; ἐάντε.. οὐ (variant{μή}) φῆτε ἐάντε φῆτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25b):—with Participles: οὐκ ἐθέλων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.224, 300, 6.165, etc.:— with Adjectives: οὐκ ἀέκοντε 5.366, 768, +others; οὐ πολλήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.7, etc.:— with Adverbs: οὐχ ἥκιστα [prev. author] 1.68, etc.: rarely with Verbal Nouns (see. below 11.10).}} —On the use of οὐ in contrasts, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II** as negativing the whole sentence,
**II.1** οὐ is frequently used alone, sometimes with the ellipsis of a definite Verb, οὔκ (i.e. ἀποκερῇ), ἄν γε ἐμοὶ πείθῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89b}}: sometimes as negativing the preceding sentence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 850, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.19: as a Particle of solemn denial frequently with μά (which see)}} and the _accusative_; sometimes without μά, οὐ τὸν πάντων θεῶν θεὸν πρόμον Ἅλιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 660 (Lyric poetry), compare 1088 (Lyric poetry), “El.” 1063 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 758.}}
**II.2** with _indicative_ of statement, τὴν δ᾽ ἐγὼ οὐ λύσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.29, compare 114, 495}}; οὐ φθίνει Κροίσου φιλόφρων ἀρετά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.94; ἔνθα κεν οὔ τιν᾽ ἀδάκρυτόν γ᾽ ἐνόησας Ἀργείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.61; οὔ κεν.. ἔπαξε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.25; οὐκ ἂν ὑπεξέφυγε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.369.}}
**II.3** with _subjunctive_ in _future_ sense, only in _Epic dialect_, οὐ γάρ τίς με βίῃ γε ἑκὼν ἀέκοντα δίηται {{Refs|7.197; οὐκ ἄν τοι χραίς μῃ κίθαρις 3.54, compare 11.387.}}
**II.4** with _optative_ in potential sense (without ἄν or κεν), also _Epic dialect_, ὃ οὐ δύο γ᾽ ἄνδρε φέροιεν {{Refs|5.303, 20.286.}}
**II.5** with _optative_ and ἄν, κείνοισι δ᾽ ἂν οὔ τις.. μαχέοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.271, compare 301, 2.250, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.63, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 155 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 310, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 403, etc.}}
**II.6** in dependent clauses οὐ is used,
**II.6.a** with ὅτι or ὡς, after Verbs of saying, knowing, and showing, ἐκ μέν τοι ἐρέω.. ὡς ἐγὼ οὔ τι ἑκὼν κατερύκομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.377, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 561, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.8, etc.}}: so with _indicative_ or _optative_ and ἄν, ἀπελογοῦντο ὡς οὐκ ἄν ποτε οὕτω μωροὶ ἦσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330a; ὡς δὲ οὐκ ἂν δικαίως αὐτοὺς δέχοισθε μαθεῖν χρή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.3, etc.: with _optative_ representing _indicative_ in indirect speech, ἔλεξε παιδὶ σῷ.. ὡς.. Ἕλληνες οὐ μενοῖεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 358, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 346, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b, etc.: for μή}} in such sentences, see at {μή} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.}}
**II.6.b** in all causal sentences, and in temporal and Relat. sentences unless there is conditional or final meaning, χωσαμένη, ὅ οἱ οὔ τι θαλύσια.. ῥέξε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.534; ἄχθεται ὅτι οὐ κάρτα θεραπεύεται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80; διότι οὐκ ἦσαν δίκαι, οὐ δυνατοὶ ἦμεν παρ᾽ αὐτῶν ἃ ὤφειλον πράξασθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.3; μή με κτεῖν᾽, ἐπεὶ οὐχ ὁμογάστριος Ἕκτορός εἰμι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.95, etc.; νῦν δὲ ἐπειδὴ οὐκ ἐθέλεις.., εἶμι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c; ἐπειδὴ τὸ χωρίον οὐχ ἡλίσκετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.102; νηπιάχοις οἷς οὔ τι μέλει πολεμήϊα ἔργα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.338, etc.}}: in causal relative sentences, οἵτινές σε οὐχὶ ἐσώσαμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 46a}}; especially in the combinations, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅστις οὐ.., as οὐκ ἔστ᾽ ἐραστὴς ὅστις οὐκ ἀεὶ φιλεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1051, compare “Hec.” 298; οὔτις ἔσθ᾽ ὃς οὔ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 725}}; οὐδείς ἐστιν ὅστις οὐ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.180.}}
**II.6.c** after ὥστε with _indicative_ or _optative_ with ἄν, ὥστ᾽ οὐ δυνατόν σ᾽ εἵργειν ἔσται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 384, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 98, “OT” 411; οὕτως αὐτοὺς ἀγαπῶμεν.. ὥστε.. οὐκ ἂν ἐθελήσαιμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.45; οὐκ ἂν ὡρκίζομεν αὐτὸν ὥστε τῆς εἰρήνης ἂν διημαρτήκει καὶ οὐκ ἂν ἀμφότερ᾽ εἶχε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.30: ὥστε οὐ with}}_infinitive_ is almost invariably due to indirect speech, ὥστ᾽ οὐκ αἰσχύνεσθαι (for οὐκ αἰσχύνονται) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 19.308, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.40, 8.76, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.6, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.27 (conjecture Reiske).}}—Rarely not in indirect speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 780, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1358, “Hel.” 108, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.2, 9.48.}}
**II.7** in a conditional clause μή is necessary, except,
**II.7.a** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, when the εἰ}} clause precedes the apodosis and the verb is _indicative_, εἰ δέ μοι οὐκ ἐπέεσσ᾽ ἐπιπείσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.162, compare 178, 20.129, 24.296, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.274, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.160, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.382, 13.144 (9.410 is an exception).}}
**II.7.b** when the εἰ clause is really causal, as after Verbs expressing surprise or emotion, μὴ θαυμάσῃς, εἰ πολλὰ τῶν εἰρημένων οὐ πρέπει σοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.44; κατοικτῖραι.., εἰ.. οὐδεὶς ἐς ἑκατοστὸν ἔτος περιέσται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1242; so also δεινὸν γὰρ ἂν εἴη πρῆγμα, εἰ Σάκας μὲν καταστρεψάμενοι δούλους ἔχομεν, Ἕλληνας δὲ οὐ τιμωρησόμεθα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.102, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.8 (probably), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.55; οὐκ αἰσχρόν, εἰ τὸ μὲν Ἀργείων πλῆθος οὐκ ἐφοβήθη τὴν Λακεδαιμονίων ἀρχήν, ὑμεῖς δ᾽ ὄντες Ἀθηναῖοι βάρβαρον ἄνθρωπον.. φοβήσεσθ; [prev. author] 15.23, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.13.}}
**II.7.c** when οὐ belongs closely to the next word (see. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus I)}}, or is quoted unchanged, εἰ, ὡς νῦν φήσει, οὐ παρεσκευάσατο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.29 codices}}; εἰ δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί (i.e. ὥσπερ λέγεις), τίνι τρόπῳ διεφθάρη **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 347.}}
**II.8** οὐ is used with _infinitive_ in indirect speech, when it represents the _indicative_ of orat. recta, φαμὲν δέ οἱ οὐ τελέεσθαι [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.664, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.174, 21.316, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1389, etc.; λέγοντες οὐκ εἶναι αὐτόνομοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.67, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.18; οἶμαι.. οὐκ ὀλίγον ἔργον αὐτὸ εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369b, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1051, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, etc.; ἡγήσαντο ἡμᾶς οὐ περιόψεσθαι [prev. work] 39. (For the occasional use of μή, see at {μή} 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5c}}]; sometimes we have οὐ and μή in consecutive clauses, οἶμαι σοῦ κάκιον οὐδὲν ἂν τούτων κρατύνειν μηδ᾽ ἐπιθύνειν χερί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1058s q.; αὐτὸ ἡγοῦμαι οὐ διδακτὸν εἶναι μηδὲ.. παρασκευαστόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319b.)}}
**II.9** οὐ is used with the _participle_, when it can be resolved into a finite sentence with οὐ, as after Verbs of knowing and showing, τὸν κατθανόνθ᾽ ὁρῶντες οὐ τιμώμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 316; κατενόησαν οὐ πολλοὺς τοὺς Θηβαίους ὄντας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2. 3; ἔργῳ δηλώσω οὐ παραγενόμενος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.8, etc.}}; or into a causal sentence, τῶν βαρβάρων οἱ πολλοὶ ἐν τῇ θαλάσσῃ διεφθάρησαν νέειν οὐκ ἐπιστάμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.89; τὴν Μένδην πόλιν ἅτε οὐκ ἀπὸ ξυμβάσεως ἀνοιχθεῖσαν διήρπασαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.130}}; or into a concessive sentence, δόξω γυναῖκα καίπερ οὐκ ἔχων ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 352, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 377, etc.: regularly with ὡς}} and _participle_, ὡς οὐχὶ συνδράσουσα νουθετεῖς τάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 1025, etc.; ἐθορυβεῖτε ὡς οὐ ποιήσοντες ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.73, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 884, “Aj.” 682, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.99, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2, 5, 28, 68, 90; ὥσπερ οὐ πάντας τούτῳ τῷ τεκμηρίῳ χρωμένους 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 90, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.11}}:—for exceptions, see at {μή} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 6.}}
**II.9.b** when the _participle_ is used with the _Article_, μή is generally used, unless there is a distinct reference to a fact, when οὐ is occasionally found, ἡμεῖς δὲ ἀπὸ τῆς οὐκ οὔσης ἔτι [πόλεως] ὁρμώμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74; τοὺς ἐν τῇ πόλει οὐδὲν εἰδότας [prev. author] 4.111; οἱ οὐκ ἐθέλοντες 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.26; τῶν οὐ βουλομένων 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.9; τοὺς οὐδὲν ἀδικοῦντας ἀκρίτους ἀπέκτειναν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.82, compare τὸν οὐδὲ συμπενθῆσαι τὰς τῆς πατρίδος συμφορὰς τολμήσαντα (preceded by τὸν.. μήτε ὅπλα θέμενον ὑπὲρ τῆς πατρίδος μήτε τὸ σῶμα παρασχόντα κτλ.) 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 43; τὸ οὐχ εὑρημένον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427e.}}
**II.10** Adjectives and abstract Substantives with the article commonly take μή (see. μή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 7) but οὐ}} is occasionally used, τὰς οὐκ ἀναγκαίας πόσεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.4; τοὺς οὐδένας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 371; τὸν οὐδέν [prev. author] “Ph.” 598 (whereas ὁ μηδείς, τὸ μηδέν is the rule)}}; τὴν τῶν γεφυρῶν οὐ διάλυσιν the **non**- dissolution of the bridges, the fact **of their not**being broken up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137; ἡ οὐ περιτείχισις [prev. author] 3.95; ἡ τῶν χωρίων οὐκ ἀπόδοσις [prev. author] 5.35, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 196 (_anapaest meter_)}}; so without the article, ἐν οὐ καιπῷ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 1287; οὐ πάλης ὕπο [prev. work] 455.}}
**II.11** for οὐ μή, see entry
**II.12** in questions οὐ ordinarily expects a positive answer, οὔ νυ καὶ ἄλλοι ἔασι..**;** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.165}}; οὐχ ὁράᾳς..**; dost** thou **not** see? {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.545; οὐκ.. ᾐσθόμη; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 956: so as a strong form of _imperative_,;_ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 273, “fragment” 310; πῶς δ᾽ οὐχ; [prev. author] “Supp.” 918, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1027; ἐμὸς μὲν οὐχί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 859: also in Prose, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, +others, NT.1Cor.5.12, etc.}}: the diphthong is genuine and always written ου (ουκ, ουδε, etc.) in early Inscrr., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.22, etc.; in 4th c.BC rarely written οκ, [prev. work] 22.1635.112,116,121}}; οὐ abbreviated ο, Suid. see at {Φιλοξένου γραμμάτιον}.
**H** ACCENTUATION. οὐ is oxytone _accusative_ to Hdn.Gr.1.494 (text doubtfulin 504): [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 166b6, referring to 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.328 τὸ μὲν ου (i.e. οὐ ={οὒ}) καταπύθεται ὄμβρῳ, says λύουσι.. τῇ προσῳδίᾳ λέγοντες τὸ ου ὀξύτερον (i.e. οὗ), compare 178b3. In codices the word is written oxytone when followed by a pause (see. supr. B), and is usually written without any accent in other cases.}}]
**H.I** οὐ in connexion with other Particles will be found in alphabetical order, οὐ γάρ, οὐ μή, etc.—The corresponding forms of μή should be compared.

3759:
** οὐαί**,
exclamation of pain and anger, **ah! woe!** with _nominative_, {{LXX|LXX.Amos.5.18, +others: with _vocative_, LXX.1Ki.13.30: with _dative_, οὐαί μοι, οὐαί σοι}}, **woe is** me! **woe to** thee!{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Num.21.29, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.19.1, “Mim.Oxy.” 413.184: with _accusative_, οὐαὶ οὐαὶ οὐαὶ τοὺς κατοικοῦντας ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς NT.Rev.8.13.}}
**II** **οὐαί·** φυλαί {{Refs 5th c.AD+|(5th c.AD: Cypria), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (Cf. _Doric dialect_ ὠβά.)}}

3761:
** οὐδέ**,
_negative_ Particle, related to μηδέ as οὐ to μή, partly _conjunction_, partly _adverb_:
**A** CONJUNCTION, **but not**, mostly answering to μέν (sometimes written divisim), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.138, 24.418; without μέν, 5.21, etc.: sometimes the first οὐδέ}}, **but not**, is followed by οὐδέ, **nor**, ἄλλοις μὲν πᾶσιν ἑήνδανεν, οὐδέ ποθ᾽ Ἥρῃ, οὐδὲ Ποσειδάων᾽, οὐδὲ γλαυκώπιδι κούρῃ {{Refs|24.25.}}
**A.II** more frequently **and not, nor**: sometimes without a _negative_ preceding, Κίρκη δ᾽ ὡς ἐνόησεν ἔμ᾽ ἥμενον, οὐδ᾽ ἐπὶ σίτῳ χεῖρας ἰάλλοντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.375; τραχὺς μόναρχος οὐδ᾽ ὑπεύθυνος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 326, compare 102, 259, +others}}; δεινὸν γάρ, οὐδὲ ῥητόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 756, compare 996, “OT” 398, 868 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97, etc.}}: after a _negative_ _compound_, ὃν ἠτίμησ᾽ Ἀγαμέμνων, οὐδ᾽ ἀπέλυσε θύγατρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.95; ἀνήμεροι γὰρ οὐδὲ πρόσπλατοι ξένοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 716; ἄστιπτος οὐδ᾽ οἰκουμένη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 2; ἄθικτος οὐδ᾽ οἰκητός [prev. author] “OC” 39.}}
**A.II.2** with a _negative_ preceding, **nor**, βρώμης δ᾽ οὐχ ἅπτεαι οὐ. ποτῆτος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.379; οὐκέτισοὶ.. μένος ἔμπεδον οὐ. τις ἀλκή 22.226; οὐκ ἔχων βάσιν οὐ. τιν᾽ ἐγχώρων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 692 (Lyric poetry), compare 681 (Lyric poetry), 905, 955, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.2, etc.: sometimes the preceding _negative_ is itself οὐδέ}}, = **and not**, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.222}}; οὐδέ, = **nor** may be repeated any number of times, e.g. three times in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1378.}} —Sts. the _negative_ follows the whole word-group instead of preceding it, σιδήρῳ δὲ οὐδ᾽ ἀργύρῳ χρέωνται οὐδέν but iron **or** silver use they not at all, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.215; Θεσσαλοῦ μὲν οὐδ᾽ Ἱππάρχου οὐδεὶς παῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.55; ἁπλοῦν μὲν οὐ. δίκαιον οὐδὲν ἂν εἰπεῖν ἔχοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.4: but οὐδὲ.. οὐδέ}} never means **neither.. nor** (like οὔτε.. οὔτε); where this combination occurs, the first οὐδέ is used without reference to the second, e.g. καὶ μὴν οὐδ᾽ ἡ ἐπιτείχισις οὐδὲ τὸ ναυτικὸν ἄξιον φοβηθῆναι and moreover we have **no** reason to fear their fortifications, **nor yet** their navy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142.}}
**A.III** οὐδέ may also follow οὔτε, by an _anacoluthon_, as in τε.., δέ.. (see. οὔτε {{Refs|11.3)}}; but οὔτε cannot follow οὐδέ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Cf. — μηδέ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 2.}}
**B** _adverb_, **not even**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus mostly with _adverbs_, οὐδ᾽ ῃβαιόν}} **not even** a little, **no not** a bit, **not at all**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.386; οὐ. τυτθόν 1.354; οὐ. μίνυνθα 20.27}}; so also ἐπεὶ οὔ οἱ ἔνι φρένες οὐδ᾽ ἠβαιαί he has **no** sense, **no not even** a little,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.141, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.288; τότε μὲν εὖ ζῶντες, νῦν δὲ οὐ. ζῶντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329a: frequently in _Attic dialect_, τούτῳ μὲν οὐ. διελέγετο}} he did **not even** exchange words with him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 425; οὐδ᾽, εἰ γέγονεν, οἶδα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.70, etc.}}: in the same sense, οὐ. γ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 97a, 97b, 106b; οὐ. γ᾽ αὖ [prev. author] “R.” 499a; οὐ. μήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.50, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ οὐ. μέν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.374, etc.: in _Attic dialect_ frequently with εἷς (whence οὐδείς), οὐδ᾽ ἂν εἷς θύσειεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 137: sometimes without elision, οὐδὲ εἷς [prev. work] 1182, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.45; οὐκ ἄλλ᾽ οὐ. ἕν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 138, compare “Ra.” 927; also οὐ. καθ᾽ ἕν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87; οὐ. παρ᾽ ἑνός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.10, etc.}} —This οὐδέ frequently follows καί, **and not even**, καὶ οὐδ᾽ αὐτοὶ αὖ μόνον, ἀλλὰ καὶ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.56, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.4, etc.}}; also ἀλλ᾽ οὐδέ, most frequently in phrase ἀλλ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ὧς.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.263, 9.351, etc.}}
**B.II** **also not, not.. either, nor yet**.., ὁ δίκαιος τοῦ δικαίου δοκεῖ τί σοι ἂν ἐθέλειν πλέον ἔχειν**;** Answ. οὐδαμῶς..; Qu. τί δ; τῆς δικαίας πράξεως**;** Answ. οὐ. τῆς δικαίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 349b, compare “Ap.” 19d, 21d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.4.}}
**C** Repetition of οὐδέ and combination with οὐ:
**C.I** in Relat. as well as antecedent clause, ὥσπερ οὐδ᾽ ηὔχετο, οὐδ᾽ ᾤετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.141a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.18.}}
**C.II** οὐ γὰρ οὐδέ, as ἀλλ᾽ οὐ γὰρ οὐ. νουθετεῖν ἔξεστί σε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 595, compare “Aj.” 1242, “OT” 287, etc.}}; οὐ. γὰρ οὐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.22, 6.130, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.32, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, etc.}}; οὐ. μὲν οὐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.703, etc.; οὐ μὰν οὐ. 23.441, etc.}}; compare οὐ with

3762:
** οὐδείς**,
_feminine_ οὐδεμίᾰ (never _nominative_ _accusative_ -μίη, -μίην, since οὐδεμιῇ is probably in {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.56}}, rarely οὐδ᾽ ἴα [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 69, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 4.40), _neuter_ οὐδέν (declined and accentuated like{εἷς}, μία, ἕν)}}], **not one**, i.e. **no one, none**, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, and 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (who prefer οὔτις) only in _neuter_ _nominative_ and _accusative_ οὐδέν}}, except in the phrase τὸ ὃν μένος οὐδενὶ εἴκων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.459, +others}}; οὐδείς is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 28; but all genders and cases are common in all other writers, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 33, etc.}}: rare in _plural_, **no set** of persons or things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.23 (uncertain conjecture), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 3.1; πρὸς οὐδένας τῶν Ἑλλήνων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.23 (variant), compare 19.31,66,312, 24.214, 27.7}}; οὐδένων εἰσὶ βελτίους, i.e. οὔ τινων ἄλλων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.17 (compare οὐδενὸς βελτίους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 324d): _dative_ _plural_ οὐδέσιν 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.24.3; for another sense of the _plural_, see below 11.3.}} —In _Ionic dialect_ the _plural_ is usually οὐδαμοί.
**2** οὐ. ὅστις οὐ **every one**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, etc.}}; οὐδὲν ὅ τι οὐ **every**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.97}}; this came to be regarded as one word, so that οὐδείς passed into the same case as ὅστις, οὐδένα ὅντινα οὐ κατέκλασε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117d; οὐδενὸς ὅτου οὐ πάντων ἂν.. πατὴρ εἴην [prev. author] “Prt.” 317c, compare 323b; οὐδενὶ ὅτῳ οὐκ ἀποκρινόμενος [prev. author] “Men.” 70c; so οὐ. ὃς οὐχὶ.. ὀνειδιεῖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 373; οὐδὲν γὰρ.. οὔτ᾽ αἰσχρὸν οὔτ᾽ ἄτιμόν ἐσθ᾽, ὁποῖον οὐ.. οὐκ ὄπωπ᾽ ἐγώ [prev. author] “Ant.” 4.}}
**3** later οὐδὲν ὅ τι without οὐ, = **nothing**, οὐδὲν ὅ τι παρήσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus “Praef.” p.137 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, +others}}
**4** ὅστις οὐδείς **not one**, ἐτεθνήκεσαν δὲ αὐτῶν μὲν ἀμφὶ τοὺς τετρακοσίους, Ρωμαίων δὲ ὅστις οὐδείς {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.20.}}
**II** **naught, good for naught**, ὦ νῦν μὲν οὐ. αὔριον δ᾽ ὑπέρμεγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 158, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 187.5}}; τὸ μὲν [γένος ἀνδρῶν] οὐδέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.3: frequently in _neuter_, οὐδὲν εἰδώς}} knowing **naught**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 141, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 391}}; οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ᾔστην οὐδὲν ἄλλο πλὴν δάκνειν knew **nothing** save how to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 19}}; οὐδὲν λέγειν to say **naught**, see at {λέγω} (B) III.6 ; τὸ οὐδ᾽ οὐδέν the absolute **nothing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180a.}}
**II.2** in _neuter_, of persons, οὐδέν εἰμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 951, etc.; οὐδὲν εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 144; πρὸς τὸν οὐδέν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 598; τὸ μηδὲν εἰς οὐδὲν ῥέπει [prev. author] “fragment” 532.2; ᾧ ἀνεμέσητον.. οὐδενὶ εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175e.}}
**II.3** in _plural_, οὐδένες ἐόντες ἐν οὐδαμοῖσι ἐοῦσι Ἕλλησι being **nobodies**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.58; ὄντες οὐδένες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 700, compare “IA” 371}}; ὁ μηδὲν ὢν κἀξ οὐδένων κεκλήσομαι uncertain conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ion” 594; οὐ γὰρ ἠξίου τοὺς μηδένας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1114.}}
**II.4** with Preps., παρ᾽ οὐδὲν εἶναι {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 983, etc.}}; παρ᾽ οὐδὲν ἄγειν, θέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 35, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 732; δι᾽ οὐδενὸς ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 584; ἐν οὐδενὸς εἶναι μέρει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.18.}}
**II.5** τὸ οὐδέν **naught, zero**, in Arith., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 2.6}}; used by Democritus as a name for Place, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 208.}}
**III** _neuter_ οὐδέν as _adverb_, **not at all, naught**, ἄριστον Ἀχαιῶν οὐ. ἔτισεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.412, compare 24.370, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.22, etc.; so οὐδέν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.42, etc.; οὐ. τι πάντως 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.65}}: in answers, **nothing, never mind, no matter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 64, “IT” 781, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 694}}; οὐδέν γε **not at all**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 1360, etc.}}; οὐδὲν μᾶλλον, οὐδὲν ἧσσον, οὐδὲν ὕστερος, see at {μάλα} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|11.5, ἥσσων 111, ὕστερος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus I.}}
**III.2** οὐδὲν ἄλλο ἤ, see at {ἄλλος} III. 2.
**B** REMARKS: the more emphatic and literal sense, **not even one**, i.e. **none whatever**, belongs to the full form, οὐδὲ εἷς, οὐδὲ μία, οὐδὲ ἕν, which is never elided, even in Comedy texts (see. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 927, “Lys.” 1045 (Lyric poetry), “Pl.” 138, 1115)}}, but frequently has a Particle inserted between,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare οὐδέ 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus — 2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus (in “Etymologicum Magnum” 639.17) and others wrongly assume οὐδείς as a _compound_ not of οὐδέ and εἷς, but of οὐ and δείς (which see). (Later οὐθείς, which see)}}

3766:
** οὐκοῦν**,
_Doric dialect_ **οὐκῶν**, _adverb_, composed, like{οὔκουν}, of οὐκ and οὖν, but differing in meaning and accent,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 257.18s qq., Hdn.Gr. 1.516, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.98B.}}
**I** in questions, inviting assent to an inference, or to an addition to what has already received assent, οὐκοῦν δοκεῖ σοι.. **;** you think **then, do you not**, that.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.15, “Mem.” 1.4.5, compare 4.2.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 332b, 360b -d, “Cra.” 416c, etc.: with hortatory}}_subjunctive_, οὐκοῦν καὶ ἄλλους σε φῶμεν δυνατὸν εἶναι ποιεῖν (i.e. ῥήτορας ἀγαθούς) **;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 449b: followed by οὐ}} when a _negative_ answer is invited, οὐκοῦν οὐκ ἂν εἴη τὸ μὴ λυπεῖσθαί ποτε ταὐτὸν τῷ χαίρειν **;**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 43d, compare “Phd.” 105e}}; οὐκοῦν οὐδ᾽ ἂν εἷς ἀντείποι **;** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 16.4.}}
**II** in affirmative sentences, **surely then**, οὐκοῦν, εἰ ταῦτα ἀληθῆ, πολλὴ ἐλπὶς ἀφικομένῳ οἷ ἐγὼ πορεύομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67b: with}}_subjunctive_ or _imperative_, οὐκοῦν διδάσκωμεν αὐτόν, ἀλλὰ μὴ λοιδορῶμεν let us teach him, **then**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “La.” 195a}}; οὐκοῦν.. ἱκανῶς ἐχέτω let this **then** suffice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 274b, compare 278b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 23.3; οὐκοῦν ἂν ἤδη.. λέγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 43}}: with a prohibition, οὐκοῦν μὴ.. αὐτομολήσῃς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.159}}; οὐκοῦν ὑπόλοιπον δουλεύειν slavery, **then**, is the only alternative, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.59.}}
**II.2** in replies, **very well, yes**, ἴωμεν.. Answ. οὐκοῦν ἐπειδὰν πνεῦμα τοὐκ πρῴρας ἀνῇ, τότε στελοῦμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 639}}; ἀμηχάνων ἐρᾷς. Answ. οὐκοῦν, ὅταν δὴ μὴ σθένω, πεπαύσομαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 91}}; ἥξει γὰρ αὐτά, κἂν ἐγὼ σιγῇ στέγω. Answ. οὐκοῦν ἅ γ᾽ ἥξει καὶ σὲ χρὴ λέγειν ἐμοί {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 342}}; ἀπόλωλας, ὦ κακόδαιμον. Answ. οὐκοῦν, ἢν λάχω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 364}}; ἴθι δὴ σκεψώμεθα.. Answ. οὐκοῦν χρή **yes**, let us do so, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 289d, compare 287c, “Sph.” 254d}}; **surely**, οὐκοῦν τρύγοιπος ταῦτα πάντ᾽ ἰάσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1087; οὐκοῦν κλεινὴ καὶ ἔπαινον ἔχουσ᾽.. ἀπέρχῃ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 817 (_anapaest meter_).}}

3767:
** οὖν**,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ **ὦν** (the latter in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.82, +others, but οὖν in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 18.29,37, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 4.18, +others)}}, _adverb_ **certainly, in fact**, confirming something, frequently in contrast with something which is not confirmed, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in combination with γε (see. γοῦν), γάρ, οὔτε or μήτε, ὡς, ἐπεί, μέν, etc.:
**1** **really**, φημὶ γὰρ οὖν κατανεῦσαι.. Κρονίωνα for I declare that Zeus did **really** promise.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.350, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 309b}}; τόφρα γὰρ οὖν ἑπόμεσθα.., ὄφρ᾽ for we followed them up to the **very** point, where.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.754, compare 15.232, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.123; εἰ δ᾽ οὖν τις ἀκτὶς ἡλίου νιν ἱστορεῖ.. ζῶντα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 676, compare 1042}}; ἐλέχθησαν λόγοι ἄπιστοι μὲν ἐνίοισι Ἑλλήνων, ἐλέχθησαν δ᾽ ὦν but they **really** were spoken, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80, compare 4.5, 6.82}}; Θηβαῖοι μὲν ταῦτα λέγουσι.., Πλαταιῆς δ᾽ οὐχ ὁμολογοῦσι.., ἐκ δ᾽ οὖν τῆς γῆς ἀνεχώρησαν **at all events** they **did** return, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5, compare 1.63, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315e; σωτηρίαν λεπτὴν μὲν.., μόνην δ᾽ οὖν [prev. author] “Lg.” 699b}}; so δ᾽ οὖν after a parenthesis; εἰ δή τις ὑμῶν οὕτως ἔχει,—οὐκ ἀξιῶ μὲν γὰρ ἔγωγε,—εἰ δ᾽ οὖν but if he is so, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ap.” 34d, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.76, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3}}; so ἀλλ᾽ οὖν.. γε but **at all events**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 84, “Ph.” 1305}}; ἔμπης οὖν ἐπιμεῖναι ἐς αὔριον to stay nevertheless **at least** till to-morrow, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.351}}; οὖν concessive, **I grant you**, τάχ᾽ οὖν τις ἄκων ἔσχε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 305: in apodosi after εἰ or ἐάν, εἰ καὶ σμικρά, ἀλλ᾽ ὦν ἴση γε ἡ χάρις.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.140, compare 9.48, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 498, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91b, etc.: after ἐπεί and ὡς, ἀλλ᾽ ἐπεὶ οὖν τὸ πρῶτον ἀνέκραγον but now that I have (emphatic) once spoken up, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.467, compare 17.226, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.333}}; Τληπόλεμος δ᾽, ἐπεὶ οὖν τράφ᾽ ἐνὶ μεγάρῳ εὐπήκτῳ, αὐτίκα.. κατέκτα **when once**, i.e. as soon as, he had grown up, {{Refs|2.661, compare 15.363, 16.394, +others}}; νεβροί, αἵ τ᾽ ἐπεὶ οὖν ἔκαμον.. ἑστᾶσ᾽ which, **as soon as** they are tired, stand still, {{Refs|4.244}}; to indicate that something foreshadowed has **actually** occurred, ἀγορήνδε καλέσσατο λαὸν Ἀχιλλεύς.., οἱ δ᾽ ἐπεὶ οὖν ἤγερθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.57, compare 3.340, +others: sometimes οὖν after ἐπεί or ὡς has either no force or approaches signification 11 or 111, οἱ δ᾽ ἐπεὶ οὖν παύσαντο πόνου 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.478, compare 19.213, 251, +others}}; τὸν δ᾽ ὡς οὖν ἐνόησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.21, +others}}; οὔτ᾽ οὖν.., οὔτε.. or οὔτε.., οὔτ᾽ οὖν.. both = **neither.. nor**, but preferred according as the first or second clause is to be marked by emphasis,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 17.20, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.200, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26, with 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.198s q., 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 271, etc.}}; so εἰ.., εἴτ᾽ οὖν.. if.., or if.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 140}}; εἴτ᾽ οὖν, εἴτε μὴ γενήσεται whether it shall be so, or no, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Heracl.” 149, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 491, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 560}}; ξεῖνος αἴτ᾽ ὦν ἀστός, i.e. αἴτε ξ. αἴτ᾽ ὦν ἀ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.78; and doubled, εἴτ᾽ οὖν ἀληθὲς εἴτ᾽ οὖν ψεῦδος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 34e, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 683: so also in parenth. Relat. clauses, ἢ σῖγ᾽, ἀτίμως, ὥσπερ οὖν ἀπώλετο πατήρ}} **even** as, **just** as, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 96, compare 888, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1307 (variant)}}; εἰ δ᾽ ἔστιν, ὥσπερ οὖν ἔστι, θεός if he is, as he **in fact** is, a god, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242e; οὗτος μὲν οἴεταί τι εἰδέναι οὐκ εἰδώς, ἐγὼ δέ, ὥσπερ οὖν οὐκ οἶδα, οὐδὲ οἴομαι [prev. author] “Ap.” 21d: for γὰρ οὖν, see at {γάρ} 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.5}}]; for μὲν οὖν, see at {μέν} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.2.}}
**2** added to _indefinite_ Prons. and _adverbs_, like Latin _cunque_, ὅστις **whoever**, ὁστισοῦν **whosoever**; ὅπως **how**, ὁπωσοῦν **howsoever**; ἄλλος ὁστισοῦν another, **be he who he may**; so ὁποιοσοῦν, ὁποιοστισοῦν, ὁποσοσοῦν, ὁπωσδηποτοῦν, ὁπητιοῦν, ὁποθενοῦν, etc., (see entry).
**II** to continue a narrative, **so, then**, καὶ τὰ μὲν οὖν.. θῆκαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.122}}; ὅτ᾽ οὖν since, **then**,.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 170, “El.” 38, 1318}}; ζεῖ οὖν ἐν τούτῳ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251c, compare “Prt.” 322b; εὐθὺς οὖν ὁ Κῦρος εἶπεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.22: in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and _Attic dialect_, μὲν οὖν (which see)}} is very common in this sense; so δ᾽ οὖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 34, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 114}}; οὖν is also used alone merely to resume after a parenthesis or long protasis, **well, as I was saying**, ὦ Λακεδαιμόνιοι, χρήσαντος τοῦ θεοῦ.., ὑμεας γὰρ πυνθάνομαι προεστάναι..,—ὑμέας ὦν.. προσκαλέομαι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.69, compare 4.75, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29d, “Smp.” 201d, etc.: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus so uses ὦν after a short protasis, 1.144, etc.}}
**II.2** ὦν is frequently inserted by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (sometimes without any discernible meaning) between the _preposition_ and its Verb (but only, it seems, in narrative with the _aorist_, which is always the _aorist_ of habitual action except in 2.172), ἐπεὰν δὲ ταῦτα ποιήσωσι, ἀπ᾽ ὦν ἔδωκαν [prev. work] 87; καὶ ἔπειτα ἀπ᾽ ὦν ἔδωκαν [prev. work] 88: after a}}_participle_, οἱ δὲ φέροντες ἐς τὴν ἀγορήν, ἀπ᾽ ὦν ἔδοντο{{Refs|[prev. work] 39; κατευξάμενοι, κοιλίην μὲν κείνην πᾶσαν ἐξ ὦν εἶλον [prev. work] 40; ἤν τις ψαύσῃ.., αὐτοῖσι τοῖσι ἱματίοισι ἀπ᾽ ὦν ἔβαψε ἑωυτόν [prev. work] 47; τοῦτον κατ᾽ ὦν κόψας [prev. work] 172}}; so in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, δι᾽ οὖν ἐφθάρησαν “Morb.” 1.14 (variant), +others; also ἐπ᾽ ὦν ἐπίομες οἶνον 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 124.3}}: this tmesis is rare in _Attic dialect_, ὥστε γε καὐτόν σε κατ᾽ οὖν ἔβαλεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1047}}; but occurs in later writers, Dorieus cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 3 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, “Anthologia Graeca” 12.226 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**III** in inferences, **then, therefore**, not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, rare in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, and usually in questions (see. below)}}; in a statement, {{Refs|“Eu.” 219}}; very common from {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} downwards; so καὶ σὺ οὖν you too **therefore**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.20; καὶ γὰρ οὖν [prev. author] “An.” 1.9.8}}; compare οὐ γὰρ οὖν, τοιγαροῦν: strengthened, δὴ οὖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 191c, etc.; οὖν δή [prev. author] “R.” 340e}}: in questions, τίς οὖν ὁ λύσων σ᾽ ἐστίν **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 771, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1191, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 906, 909}}, etc.; ἆρ᾽ οὖν δή **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 146a}}; τί οὖν δή **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 873 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 57a.}}

3768:
** οὔπω**
or **οὔ πω,** _Ionic dialect_ **οὔκω**, _adverb_ **not yet**, opposed to οὐκέτι (no longer, no more), usually with _present_ or past (especially _perfect_, or _aorist_ in _perfect_ sense) tenses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.799, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.335, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 521, “Sc.” 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322b, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 98, etc.}}; frequently with another word between, as οὐ γάρ πω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.262, 2.192; so οὔ τί κω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.110; οὔτι πω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 179, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 106, “El.” 513 (Lyric poetry), “OC” 1370; οὐ πέφυκέ πω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 27, compare “Eu.” 590, etc.}}
**2** sometimes merely as a stronger form of the _negative_, **not, not at all**, when it may be used with the _present_ or _future_, σοὶ δ᾽ οὔ πω.. θεοὶ κοτέουσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.143, compare 12.270, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.118, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 594; οὔ πω τλήσομ᾽.. ὁρᾶσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.306, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.358}}: with _aorist_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 241, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 105.}}

3769:
** οὐρά**,
_Ionic dialect_ **οὐρή**, ἡ, (akin to ὄρρος) **tail**, of a lion, οὐρῇ δὲ πλευράς τε καὶ ἰσχία.. μαστίεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.170}}; of a dog, οὐρῇ μέν ῥ᾽ ὅ γ᾽ ἔσηνε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.302}}; of the wolves and lions round the house of Circe, οὐρῇσιν μακρῇσι περισσαίνοντες {{Refs|10.215}}; of other animals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 512, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38, 47, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 689b30, +others}}; not used of birds (compare ὀρροπύγιον), {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 504a31.}}
**2** ={αἰδοῖον}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1078, _falsa lectio_ in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 12.}}
**II** of an army marching, **rearguard, rear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.38, etc.}}; ἡ οὐ. τοῦ κέρατος **rear-rank**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.5.5}}; κατ᾽ οὐράν τινος ἕπεσθαι to follow in his **rear**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.3.21, compare 2.4.3}}; ὁ κατ᾽ οὐ. **the rear-rank man**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 5.3.45}}; ἐπ᾽ οὐράν **to the rear**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ages.” 2.2; εἰς οὐράν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 16.33}}; ἐπ᾽ οὐρᾷ τῶν ἱππέων **in rear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.4}}; κατ᾽ οὐρὰν προσπίπτειν to attack **in rear**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.67.2.}}
**II.b** **left wing** of a phalanx (opposed to κεφαλή), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 7.3, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 8.3.}}
**II.2** ῥήματος οὐρή, i.e. its **echo,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca Appx. 4.155 (Euodus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.3** ἑπτὰ κλῶνας ἐλαίας ἄρας, τὰς μὲν ἓξ δῆσον οὐρὰν καὶ κεφαλὴν ἓν καθ᾽ ἕν, i.e. tie together the two **ends** of each twig separately, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.1250.}}

3770:
** οὐράν-ιος** [ᾰ], α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 715, “Ph.” 1729 (both Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247a (variant), “IG” 12(2).58b4 (Mytil.)}}:— **heavenly, dwelling in heaven**, γέννα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 165 (Lyric poetry); θεοί “hymnus ad Cererem” 55, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 90 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 758 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; οὐράνιαι **the goddesses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.38 codices; οὐράνιοι}}**the gods,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(1).40 (from Laconia)}}; of special gods, Θέμις οὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 30.1; Ζεύς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.56, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 55, etc.}}; αἱ οὐ. θεοί, Demeter and Kore, [{{Refs|“IG” 12 (5).655.6 (Syros, nr. Delos); Ἥρα “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 7034; Ἔρως [prev. work]3157; see at {Οὐρανία}.}}]
**2** generally, **in** or **of heaven**, ἀστήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.75; πόλος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 429 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐ. θεᾶς βρέτας **fallen from heaven**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 986; ἀστραπά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1466 (Lyric poetry); φῶς [prev. author] “Ant.” 944 (Lyric poetry); νεφέλαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 316}}; οὐ. ὕδατα, i.e. rain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).2, “Geoponica” 2.6.10}}; so τὰ οὐ. alone, = **rains**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.14.8}}; οὐ. ἄχος, of a storm, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 418}} (where it may be _metaphorically_, compare below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11); οὐ. σημεῖα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2}}; τὰ οὐ. **the phenomena of the heavens**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.11; οὐράνιά τε καὶ χθονοστιβῆ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 301.}}
**II** **reaching to heaven, high as heaven**, κίων, of Aetna, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.19; ἐλάτης οὐ. ἄκρος κλάδος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1064; πήδημα [prev. author] “El.” 860 (Lyric poetry)}}; σκέλος οὐ. ἐκλακτίζειν, ῥίπτειν, kick up **sky-high**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1492, 1530}}; of sounds, ὀμφὰν οὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 808 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 781; ἵππον οὐράνια βρέμοντα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 519 (Lyric poetry)}}: _metaphorically_, **mountainous, colossal**, οὐ. ἄχη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 573 (Lyric poetry); ἡμάρτηκεν οὐράνιόν γ᾽ ὅσον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1135.}}
**III** **sky-blue**, λίθος κυανωτάτη καὶ -ία ἰδεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.25.}}
**IV** οὐράνιον, τό, name of an eye-salve, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.106.}}
**V** _adverb_ -ίως **from the point of view of heaven**, opposed to γηΐνως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Sacr.” p.148 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}

3772:
** οὐρᾰνός**, ὁ,
_Doric dialect_ and _Boeotian dialect_ **ὠρανός** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 23.16, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.147, 5.144, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.79, “Hymnus ad Isim” 19}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **ὄρανος** (ὀράνω [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 37, 64, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 34, but ὠράνω 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.11 (assuming variant), 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 17 (assuming variant), and see at {Οὐρανία}): —never used in _plural_ by classical writers, see 1.4: (see. near the end):}}]
**I** **heaven**: in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}
**I.1** **vault** or **firmament of heaven, sky**, γαῖα.. ἐγείνατο ἶσον ἑαυτῇ οὐρανὸν ἀστερόεντα, ἵνα μιν περὶ πάντα καλύπτοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 127; ἔχει δέ τε κίονας αὐτὸς [Ἄτλας] μακράς, αἳ γαῖάν τε καὶ οὐρανὸν ἀμφὶς ἔχουσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.54, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 351; χάλκεος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.425; πολύχαλκος 5.504, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.2; σιδήρεος 15.329}}; wrapped in clouds, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.192, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.303}}; above the aether, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.458, 17.425, 19.351, cf. Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.3; even 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus}} continued to regard it as solid (στερέμνιον), {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.11.2 (“Vorsokr.” ip.209)}}; defined as αἰθέρος τὸ ἔσχατον by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.33, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 95 ff.}}; ἠέλιος δὲ οὐρανοῦ ἐξαπόλωλε, of an eclipse, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.357, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 845; ἐν δὲ τὰ τείρεα πάντα, τά τ᾽ οὐρανὸς ἐστεφάνωται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.485; Ἕσπερος, ὃς κάλλιστος ἐν οὐρανῷ ἵσταται ἀστήρ 22.318; οὐρανὸς ἀστερόεις 6.108, +others}}
**I.2** **heaven**, as the seat of the gods, outside or above this skyey vault, the portion of Zeus (see. Ὄλυμπος),{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.192, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.67, etc.; οὐ. Οὔλυμπός τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.497, 8.394; Οὔλυμ πός τε καὶ οὐ. 19.128}}; πύλαι οὐρανοῦ {{Refs|“Heaven” -gate, i. e. a thick cloud, which the Ὧραι lifted and put down like a trap-door, 5.749, 8.393}}; so, later, οἱ ἐξ οὐρανοῦ the gods of **heaven**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 897 (Lyric poetry)}}; οἱ ἐν οὐρανῷ θεοί (viz. Sun, etc.) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 508a; εὔχετο, χεῖρ᾽ ὀρέγων εἰς οὐ. ἀστερόεντα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.371, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.527}}; νὴ τὸν οὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 267, 366.}}
**I.3** in common language, **sky**, οὐδέ τις ἄλλη φαίνετο γαιάων, ἀλλ᾽ οὐ. ἠδὲ θάλασσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.302; σέλας δ᾽ εἰς οὐ. ἵκῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.509}}; κλέος οὐρανὸν ἵκει, κλέος οὐ. εὐρὺν ἱκάνει, renown reaches **to heaven**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 192, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.108}}; so ὀρυμαγδός, κνίση, σκόπελος οὐρανὸν ἷκεν or ἱκάνει, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.425, 1.317, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.73 (compare οὐράνιος 11, οὐρανομήκης): _metaphorically_, ὕβρις τε βίη τε σιδήρεον οὐ. ἵκει}} deeds of violence 'cry **to heaven'**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|15.329, 17.565; γῇ τε κοὐρανῷ λέξαι.. τύχας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 57, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 79.1}}; πρὸς οὐρανὸν βιβάζειν τι to exalt **to heaven**, {{Refs|S “OC” 381}}; πρὸς τὸν οὐ. ἥλλοντο leaped up **on high**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.11; πρὸς τὸν οὐ. βλέπειν [prev. author] “Oec.” 19.9.}}
**I.4** in Philos., **the heavens, universe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 269d, “Ti.” 32b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 278b21, “Metaphorical” 990a20, +others: _plural_ in “VT”, οἱ οὐρανοί}} the **heavens**, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.96(97).6, 148.4, +others}}
**I.5** **a region of heaven, climate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.142.}}
**I.6** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus name of 10, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 59.}}
**II** **anything shaped like the vault of heaven**, as,
**II.1** **vaulted roof** or **ceiling**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.2** **roof of the mouth, palate**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 492a20, “PA” 660a14, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.344b, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.104 (Marc. argument).}}
**II.3** **lid**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Matro Parodius “Convivium - Corpusculum poesis epicae ludibundae” 12.}}
**II.4** **tent, pavilion**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 13.166b.}}
**III** _proper name_, “Uranos”, son of Erebos and Gaia, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 127s q}}.; but husband of Gaia, parent of Cronos and the Titans (compare Οὐρανίδης),{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 106, “Hymni Homerici” 30.17, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 207. (Acc. to 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 400a7, from ὅρος and ἄνω,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 396c. This must be wrong, but the true etymology is doubtful.)}}

3775:
** οὖς**
(_nominative_ _singular_ frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” Il(2).161 B 126, +others (Delos, 3rd c.BC), see entry near the end), τό, _genitive_ ὠτός, _dative_ ὠτί: _plural_ _nominative_ ὦτα, _genitive_ ὤτων, _dative_ ὠσί (ὤτοις condemned by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 186):— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has only _accusative_ _sing._ and _dative_ _plural_ (see. below)}}; the other cases he forms as if from οὖας (which is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.14), _genitive_ οὔατος, _plural_ _nominative_ and _accusative_ οὔατα (also in 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 21, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 8, +others, “SIG” 1025.62 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC))}}, _dative_ οὔασι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.442 (ὠσίν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.200)}}: Hellenistic _nominative_ _singular_ **ὦς** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.33 (3rd c.BC), “PGrenf.” 1.12.29, 2.15 ii I (2nd c.BC), “IG” 7.3498.19 (Oropus, 2nd c.BC), Roussel “Cultes Egyptiens” 217 (Delos, 2nd c.BC), “PStrassb.” 87.14 (2nd c.BC)}}: also _Doric dialect_ **ὦς** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.32}}; _plural_ ὤϝαθ᾽ conjecture for ὦτά θ᾽ in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 41}}:—**ear**, Ἄντιφον αὖ παρὰ οὖς ἔλασε ξίφει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.109; [κηρὸν] ἐπ᾽ ὠσὶν ἄλειψ᾽ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” [same place]}}; αἲ γὰρ δή μοι ἀπ᾽ οὔατος ὧδε γένοιτο oh may I never hear of such a thing! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.272; αἲ γὰρ ἀπ᾽ οὔατος εἴη 22.454; ἀμφὶ κτύπος οὔατα βάλλει 10.535}}; ὀρθὰ ἱστάναι τὰ ὦτα, of horses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.129, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 27, etc.; ἐν τοῖσι ὠσὶ.. οἰκέει ὁ θυμός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.39, compare 1.8}}; βοᾷ ἐν ὠσὶ κέλαδος rings in **the ear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 605; φθόγγος βάλλει δι᾽ ὤτων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1188, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 56 (Lyric poetry); δι᾽ ὠτὸς παῦρα ἐννέπειν πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1437 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὀξὺν δι᾽ ὤτων κέλαδον ἐνσείσας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 737, compare “OT” 1387; δι᾽ ὤτων ἦν λόγος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1139, compare “Rh.” 294, 566; so ἁμῖν τοῦτο δι᾽ ὠτὸς ἔγεντο 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.27; λόγους ψιθύρους εἰς ὦτα φέρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 149 (_anapaest meter_); εἰς οὖς ἑκάστῳ.. ηὔδα λόγους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1091, compare “Hipp.” 932; προσκύψας μοι μικρὸν πρὸς τὸ οὖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275e}}; ἐπ᾽ (ἐς conjecture Dawes) οὔατα λάθριος εἶπεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 105}}; reversely, παρέχειν τὰ ὦτα to lend the **ears**, i. e. to attend, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 396d, etc.; so ἐπισχέσθαι τὰ ὦτα [prev. author] “Smp.” 216a; παραβάλλειν [prev. author] “R.” 531a, cf. Callias Epigrammaticus “anonymous fragment” 375; τὰ ὦτα ἐξεπετάννυτο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1347; ὦτα χορηγεῖν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.232f; ἀποκλείειν τὰ ὦτα [prev. work]143f; οἱ ὦτα ἔχοντες}} those who have **ears** to hear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1113c: _metaphorically_, of spies in Persia, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.10s q., 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 23, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b30; τὸ τῶν λεγομένων ὤτων καὶ προσαγωγέων γένος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.522f}}; τὰ ὦτα ἐπὶ τῶν ὤμων ἔχοντες, of persons who slink away ashamed (hanging their ears like dogs), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 613c}}: proverbial, see at {λύκο; τεθλασμένος οὔατα πυγμαῖς}, of a boxer, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.45 (compare ὠτοκάταξις)}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα τὰ ὦτα καθεύδειν sleep **soundly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae 54 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II** from resemblance to **an ear**,
**II.1** **handle**, especially of pitchers, cups, etc., οὔατα δ᾽ αὐτοῦ τέσσαρ᾽ ἔσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.633, compare 18.378, 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.536a, “IG” 11(2).161 B 126 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 2.23, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.87; [ποτήριον] ὦτα συντεθλασμένον 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 270.3.}}
**II.2** in Architecture texts, ={παρωτίς}{{Refs|4, “IG” 12.372.201, compare 319.6.}}
**II.3** οὖς Ἀφροδίτης, a kind of **shell-fish**, Antig.Car. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.88a}}; οὖς θαλάττιον, ={ἀγρία λεπάς}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 529b16.}}
**II.4** τὰ ὦτα (οὔατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus) τῆς καρδίας}} the **auricles** of the heart, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 6.15, compare 2.615K.}}
**II.5** name of part of a bandage, Heraclas cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.15 at Title Page [Written ὀ̄ς in “IG” 12}}. [prev. cited]] (Cogn. with Latin _auris_, Lithuanian _ausìs_, O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **éar**, etc.; details obscure; _nominative_ _singular_ οὖς perhaps from *ο(ϝ)ος (contained in ἀμφώης).)

3776:
** οὐσί-α**,
_Ionic dialect_ **-ιη**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.92, 6.86.ά, “SIG” 167.26 (Mylasa, 4th c.BC)}}; _Doric dialect_ **ἐσσία**, **ὠσία** (which see): ἡ: (ὀντ-, _participle_ of εἰμί **sum**):—**that which is one's own, one's substance, property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same places], 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 911 (assuming variant), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 337, “Hel.” 1253 (_plural_, “fragment” 354 (assuming variant)), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 729, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 551b, “SIG”}}[same place], etc.; opposed to τὰ σώματα (civil status), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74; καλῶς.. ἐπεμελήθη τῶν οὐσιῶν ὑπὲρ τοῦ δημάρχου “BSA” 24.154 (Attica, 4th c.BC)}}; εἰ ἐκεκτήμην οὐ. if I had been a man of **substance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.11; ὑπὲρ τὴν οὐ. δαπανᾶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.7; πατρῴαν οὐ. κατεσθίειν 4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.32, compare 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 45 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; φανερὰ οὐσία real **property, immovables**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.118}}; opposed to ἀφανής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.4}}; frequently of **estates** in Egypt, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 6.23 (2nd c.BC, _plural_), “BGU” 650.3 (1st c.AD), “OGI” 665.30 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**II** in Philos., like _Ionic dialect_ φύσις (with which it is interchanged in various uses, e. g. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359a, 359b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 646a25, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.1.1)}}, **stable being, immutable reality**, opposed to γένεσις, ὅτιπερ πρὸς γένεσιν οὐσία, τοῦτο πρὸς πίστιν ἀλήθεια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 29c, compare “Sph.” 232c; ὧν κίνησις γένεσιν παραλαβοῦσα ἀέναον οὐ. ἐπόρισεν [prev. author] “Lg.” 966e; γένεσις μὲν τὸ σπέρμα, οὐ. δὲ τὸ τέλος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 641b32, compare 640a18, etc.}}; ὁδὸς εἰς οὐσίαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1003b7}}: hence, **being** in the abstract, opposed to non-being (τὸ μὴ εἶναι), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 185c.}}
**II.2** **substance, essence**, opposed to πάθη ('modes'), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthphr.” 11a; πάθη οὐσίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1003b7}}; opposed to συμβεβηκότα ('accidents'), {{Refs|[prev. author] “APo.” 83a24, “PA” 643a27; ἡ φύσις [τῆς ψυχῆς] καὶ ἡ οὐ., εἶθ᾽ ὅσα συμβέβηκε περὶ αὐτήν [prev. author] “de An.” 402a8.}}
**II.3** **true nature** of that which is a member of a kind, defined as ὃ τυγχάνει ἕκαστον ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 65d}}; as τὸ ὅ ἐστι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 92d; as τὸ τί ἐστι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 90b30}}; τὸ εἶναί τε καὶ τὴν οὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 509b}}; expressed in a formula or definition, ψυχῆς οὐ. τε καὶ λόγον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 245e; τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι οὗ ὁ λόγος ὁρισμός, καὶ τοῦτο οὐ. λέγεται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1017b22; μόνης τῆς οὐ. ἐστὶν ὁ ὁρισμός [prev. work] 1031a1.}}
**II.4** the possession of such a nature, **substantiality**, ἔτι ἐπέκεινα τῆς οὐ. πρεσβείᾳ.. ὑπερέχοντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 509b.}}
**II.5** in the concrete, **the primary real**, the **substratum** underlying all change and process in nature, applied by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} to the atoms of Democritus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“fragment” 208; to τὰ ἁπλᾶ σώματα [prev. author] “Cael.” 298a29, compare “Metaphorical” 1017b10; πᾶσαι αἱ φυσικαὶ οὐ. ἢ σώματα ἢ μετὰ σωμάτων γίγνονται [prev. author] “Cael.” 298b3, +others; ταὐτὸν σῶμα καὶ οὐσίαν ὁριζόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 246a; but also, νοητὰ ἄττα καὶ ἀσώματα εἴδη.. τὴν ἀληθινὴν οὐ. [prev. work]b.}}
**II.6** in Logic, **substance** as the leading category, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 1b26, “Metaphorical” 1045b29}}; αἱ πρῶται οὐ. (individuals), αἱ δεύτεραι οὐ. (species and genera), {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cat.” 2b5, 2a15 (but ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ ὁ ἵππος.. οὐκ ἔστιν οὐ. ἀλλὰ σύνολόν τι [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1035b29, compare σύνθετος or συνθέτη οὐ. [prev. work] 1043a30, “de An.” 412a16); ἡ μὲν ψυχὴ οὐ. ἡ πρώτη, τὸ δὲ σῶμα ὕλη [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1037a5; ἡ ψυχὴ οὐ. ὡς εἶδος [prev. author] “de An.” 412a19}}; ἡ οὐ. ἐντελέχεια{{Refs|[prev. work] 21; [ψυχὴ] οὐ. τοῦ ἐμψύχου [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1035b15}}; of the abstract objects of mathematics, μονὰς οὐ. ἄθετος, στιγμὴ δὲ οὐ. θετός {{Refs|[prev. author] “APo.” 87a36.}}
**II.7** after [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus and 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus in various uses, as ἡ ἄποιος οὐ., ={ἡ ὕλη}, 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.24}}]; κατὰ οὐσίαν, opposed to κατὰ δύναμιν ἢ ἐνέργειαν, Polystr.p.12 W.; πᾶς νοῦς ἀμέριστός ἐστιν οὐ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 171, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.4.5, 2.6.1, 4.7.8, 6.1.2, +others}}
**II.8** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus name for I, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 6.}}
**III** name of a plaster, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.15,45.}}
**IV** αἱ οὐ. **fireresisting substances**, Zos.Alch.p.168 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; of the four σώματα (copper, tin, lead, iron), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pseudo-Democritus Alchemista cited in [same work] p.167 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**V** in Magic, **a material thing by which a connexion is established between the person to be acted upon and the supernatural agent**, e.g. a hair, λαβὼν βελόνην διείρων τὴν οὐ. εἰς αὐτήν [{{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.2949, compare “PMag.Osl.” 1.73; mould from a tomb, “PMag.Par.” 1.435; κυνοκεφάλου οὐ.,.. κυνὸς οὐ., ={κόπρος} (compare 2460), [prev. work]2687, etc.}}]

3777:
** οὔτε**,
_adverb_, (οὐ, τε) joining _negative_ clauses, as τε joins positive, but rare in the simple sense **and not**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.265 (variant), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155 (variant)}}; οὔτε γὰρ ἐκείνους διδόναι, Latin _neque enim_, [{{Refs|[prev. author] 1.3 (probably _falsa lectio_ for{οὐδὲ})}}]; and occasionally in later writers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 208a8, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 27, 53, etc.}}
**II** mostly repeated, οὔτε.., οὔτε.. **neither.., nor**.., Latin _neque.._, **neque**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently joins another Particle with the first or second οὔτε}}, as οὔτ᾽ ἂρ.., οὔτε..; οὔτ᾽ ἂρ.., οὔτ᾽ ἂρ..; οὔτ᾽ ἄρ τε.., οὔτ᾽ ἄρα.. [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.89; οὔτ᾽ οὖν, see at {οὖν} 1; οὔτε.. οὖν.., οὔτ᾽ ἄρα.. 20.7}}]; οὔτ᾽ ἄρ.., οὔτε τι.., or οὔτε τι.., οὔτε..,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.115, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.202}}; so too οὔτε.., οὔτε μὴν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.12; οὔτε.., οὔτ᾽ αὖ.., see below 3.}}
**II.2** frequently used to divide up a general negation into two or more parts, ὡς δ᾽ ἐν ὀνείρῳ οὐ δύναται φεύγοντα διώκειν, οὔτ᾽ ἂρ ὁ τὸν δύναται ὑποφεύγειν οὔθ᾽ ὁ διώκειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.200; thrice repeated, οὔ μοι Τρώων.. μέλει ἄλγος.., οὔτ᾽ αὐτῆς Ἑκάβης οὔτε Πριάμοιο ἄνακτος οὔτε κασιγνήτων 6.450; οὐκ ἔπειθεν οὔτε τοὺς στρατηγοὺς οὔτε τοὺς στρατιώτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4}}: without a _negative_ preceding, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.490, 2.202, etc.}}
**II.3** within one of the two clauses distinguished by οὔτε a subordinate part may be introduced by οὐδέ, οὔτε γὰρ ἐκ σκίλλης ῥόδα φύεται οὐδ᾽ ὑάκινθος (οὐθ᾽ codices), οὐ δέ ποτ᾽ ἐκ δούλης τέκνον ἐλευθέριον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 537; οὔτε.. ἀπέφηνεν οὐδὲ παρέσχηται μάρτυρας, οὔτ᾽ αὖ τὸν ἀριθμὸν.. ἐπανέφερεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.49: sometimes after several clauses distinguished by οὔτε the last is introduced emphatic by οὐδέ, οὔτε φάρμακα οὔτε καύσεις οὔτε τομαὶ οὐδ᾽ αὖ ἐπῳδαί}} **nor yet** incantations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 426b, compare 499b (so μηδέ}} after clauses with μήτε, μήτε παιδεία μήτε δικαστήρια μήτε νόμοι μηδὲ ἀνάγκη μηδεμία {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 327d)}}; so οὐδέ (μηδέ) may sometimes follow a single οὔτε (μήτε), οὐδέ ποτέ σφιν οὔτε τι πημανθῆναι ἔπι δέος, οὐδ᾽ ἀπολέσθαι **neither** to suffer misery, **nor yet** to die, see reading in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.563, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.83, “I.” 2.44, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1139, 1141 (assuming variant), 1297 (conjecture), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19d: in many of these places, however, the readings vary, and editors have altered οὐδέ into οὔτε}}; but this cannot be done in some cases, as οὔτ᾽ ἂν ὑπό γε ἑνὸς.. πάθοι, ἴσως δ᾽ οὐδὲ ὑπὸ πλεόνων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “La.” 182b: so when οὔτε is followed by οὐδὲ μέν, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.207}}; by οὐδὲ μήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.27}}; οὐδ᾽ αὖ, see above—But οὔτε (μήτε) cannot be used simply answering to οὐδέ (μηδέ), see at {μηδέ} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 2.}}
**II.4** οὔτε may be followed by a Posit. clause with τε, οὔτ᾽ αὐτὸς κτενέει, ἀπό τ᾽ ἄλλους πάντας ἐρύξει he **both** will **not** kill **and** will defend, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.156, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 246, 262, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.48, etc.}}: sometimes the _negative_ is added **after** the τε, οὔτ᾽ ὦν.. καρπὸν ἔδωκαν ἄρουραι, δένδρεά τ᾽ οὐκ ἐθέλει.. φέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.40, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 763, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 302; κυάμους δὲ οὔτε τι μάλα σπείρουσι, τούς τε γενομένους οὔτε τρώγουσι οὔτε ἕψοντες πατέονται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37: the combination οὔτε.., καί}}.. is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 591}}, but is found in later writers, as {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 2.4, Chor.in “Rev.Phil.” 1877.218.}}
**II.5** οὔτε is frequently, by anacoluthon, followed not by a second οὔτε, but by some other Particle, as by οὐδέ, see above 3; by δέ alone, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.368, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 388e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.16.}}
**II.5.b** in Poets, οὐ sometimes follows without any conjunctive Particle, οὐκ ἦν ἀλέξημ᾽ οὐδὲν οὔτε βρώσιμον, οὐ χριστόν, οὔτε πιστόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 479}}; οὔτε πλινθυφεῖς δόμους.. ᾖσαν, οὐ ξυλουργίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 450, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.139f; οὔτε βλάστας.. πατρός, οὐ μητρὸς εἶχον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 972, compare “Ant.” 249, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 41}}: so also in the Prose of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἐς ποταμὸν οὔτε ἐνουρέουσι οὔτε ἐμπτύουσι, οὐ χεῖρας ἐναπονίζονται, οὐδέ.. 1.138.}}
**II.5.c** in Poets also οὔτε is sometimes replaced by οὐ, οὐ νιφετὸς οὔτ᾽ ἂρ χειμὼν πολὺς οὔτε ποτ᾽ ὄμβρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.566; οὐ γὰρ ἂν εἰδείης ἀνδρὸς νόον οὔτε γυναικός 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 125 (uncertain reading), compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.115, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.136, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 588 (Lyric poetry, assuming variant), etc.}}
**II.5.d** the former οὔτε is sometimes omitted, ναυσὶ δ᾽ οὔτε πεζὸς ἰών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.29}}; νόσοι δ᾽ οὔτε γῆρας[{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 41; Πάρις γὰρ οὔτε συντελὴς πόλις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 532, compare “Ch.” 294; and see at {μήτε} 2.}}]
**II.6** when οὔτε and μήτε correspond, each retains its proper sense, ἀναιδὴς οὔτ᾽ εἰμὶ μήτε γενοίμην **neither** am I shameless, **nor** may I become so, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.68, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.128.}}

3778:
** οὗτος**, αὕτη, τοῦτο,
_genitive_ τούτου, ταύτης, τούτου, etc.: the dual _feminine_ never in _Attic dialect_, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, [near the start]:—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_, **this**, common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards}}
**A** ORIGIN and FORMS: οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο probably arose from a reduplication of the _demonstrative_ ὁ, ἡ, τό with insertion of -υ- (= Sanskrit Particle **u**), e.g. ταῦτα from τα-υ-τα: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ _feminine_ τούτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; _nominative_ _plural_ τοῦτοι, ταῦται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 111.23: the former occurs 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 24, “GDI” 3045 B 6 (from Selinus), “SIG” 339.16 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., the latter is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 97, certain in “SIG” 241 B 117 (Delph., 4th c.BC): in _Boeotian dialect_ all forms begin with οὑτ-}}, as _genitive_ _singular_ _neuter_ οὕτω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.359.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ _singular_ _feminine_ οὕταν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.80; _accusative_ _plural_ _neuter_ οὗτα “SIG” 1185.20 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC), etc.: _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ _Attic dialect_ τούτων, Cretan dialect ταυτᾶν “Leg.Gort.” 5.19; _neuter_ ταύτων “Michel” 1334.10 (Elis, 4th c.BC).—In _Ionic dialect_ sometimes written ταότην, ταο̄τα, “SIG” 283.19 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 46.7 (Halic., 5th c.BC), +others}} —In _Attic dialect_ οὗτος was frequently strengthened by the _demonstrative_ -ί, οὑτοσί, αὑτηί, τουτί, _genitive_ τουτουί, _dative_ τουτῳί, _accusative_ τουτονί; _plural_ _nominative_ οὑτοιί, _neuter_ ταυτί, etc., **this man here**: sometimes a Particle is inserted between the _pronoun_ and -ί, as αὑτηγί for αὑτηί γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}; τουτογί for τουτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Av.” 894, +others}}; ταυταγί for ταυτί γε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 492, “Pax” 1057, +others}}; τουτοδί for τουτὶ δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 227}}; τουτουμενί for τουτουὶ μέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 965.}} —In codices the ν ἐφελκυστικόν is sometimes added in the forms οὑτοσίν, οὑτωσίν, and οὑτοσίν is said to be _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24, 82.11}}. [This ι is always long, and a long vowel or diphthong before it becomes short, as αὑτη?~Xί, τουτω?~Xί, οὑτοῐί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 201, “Pl.” 44, “Ach.” 40, etc.]}}
**B** USAGE in regard to CONCOR{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator οὗτος}} is frequently used as a _pronoun_ _substantive_: hence _neuter_ is followed by _genitive_, κατὰ τοῦτο τῆς ἀκροπόλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ὕβρεως ἐλήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἥκεις μανίας [prev. author] 36.48; ταῦτα τῶν μαθημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278b: but quite as frequently as}} _adjective_, in which case its _substantive_ commonly takes the _Article_, οὗτος ὁ ἀνήρ or ὁ ἀνὴρ οὗτος.—But the _Article_ is absent,
**B.1** always in _Epic dialect_ Poets (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.114), οὗτος ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.471, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, etc.}}: sometimes also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 122 (Lyric poetry), 495, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 406, “OC” 471, 1177: once in an Inscription, τοπεῖα: τούτων τὰ ἡμίσεα τοπείων “IG” 22.1622.135 (4th c.BC).}}
**B.2** sometimes when the Noun is so specified that the _Article_ is not needed, ἐς γῆν ταύτην.., ἥντινα νῦν Σκύθαι νέμονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ταύτας ἃς οἱ πατέρες παρέδοσαν μελέτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.85, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449d, etc.; πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος, ὃν θρηνεῖς ἀεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530.}}
**B.3** when οὗτος is used in local sense, **here**, see below with {{Refs|1.5.}}
**B.4** when the Noun with which οὗτος agrees stands as its Predicate, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι πρόφασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034}}; δικαστοῦ αὕτη ἀρετή [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a: this exception extends to cases in which the Predicate is not so distinctly separated from the Subject, αἰτίαι μὲν αὗται προυγεγένηντο}} **these** were the grievances which already existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66}}; ταύτην φήμην παρέδοσαν **this** was the report which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c: frequently with a _superlative_, κίνησις αὕτη μεγίστη δὴ.. ἐγένετο}} **this** was notably the greatest movement which.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 3.113: with πρῶτος [prev. author] 1.55, 98, 6.31, NT.Luke.2.2.}}
**B.5** when _3rd pers._ is used for _2nd pers._ to express contempt, οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὑτοσὶ ἀνήρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467b, 489b, etc.}}
**B.II** though οὗτος usually agrees with the Noun that serves as Predicate, it is not rare to find it in the _neuter_, μανία δὲ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; τοῦτο γάρ εἰσι.. εὔθυναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.82, etc.}}: and in _plural_, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα ἀρχή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13; ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ προδότης [prev. author] 2.166}}: so with an explanatory clause added, τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὁ συκοφάντης, αἰτιᾶσθαι μὲν πάντα ἐξελέγξαι δὲ μηδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.34.}}
**B.II.2** so also with a Noun in apposition, τούτοισιν μὲν ταῦτα μέλει, κίθαρις καὶ ἀοιδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.159; τούτου τιμῶμαι, ἐν πρυτανείῳ σιτήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 323.3, etc.}}
**B.II.3** the _neuter_ also may refer to a _masculine_ or _feminine_ Noun, καρπὸν φορέει κυάμῳ ἴσον: τοῦτο ἐπεὰν γένηται πέπον κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10, etc.}}
**B.II.4** the _neuter_ is also used of classes of persons, μελιτοπῶλαι καὶ τυροπῶλαι: τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a}}; or of an abstract fact, οὐκ Ἰοφῶν ζ;—τοῦτο γάρ τοι καὶ μόνον ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ λοιπὸν ἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 73.}}
**B.III** with Prons.,
**B.III.1** personal, οὗτος σύ, in local sense, see below
**B.III.2** _interrogative_, τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; what is **this that.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1173 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ant.” 7}}; ποίοισι τούτοι; for ποῖά ἐστι ταῦτα οἷς [ἔχεις ἐλπίδα]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 388, compare “Ant.” 1049}}; Νέστορ᾽ ἔρειο ὅν τινα τοῦτον ἄγει whom he brings **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.612.}}
**B.III.3** with οἷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.377, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c.}}
**B.III.4** _possessive_, πατὴρ σὸς οὗτος **this** father of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 530, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.30.}}
**B.III.5** _demonstrative_, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος, τὸν σὺ ζητέεις, where ἐκεῖνος is the Predicate, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622, compare “Or.” 804; αὐτὸ τοῦτο, see at {αὐτός} I.7}}]; τοῦτον τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνδρα **this** same man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128.}}
**B.III.5.b** exceptionally, Διφίλου οὗτος ὅδ᾽ ἐστὶ τύπος {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).300 (from Paros).}}
**B.III.6** ἄλλος τις οὗτος ἀνέστη another man **here**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380.}}
**B.IV** with Numerals, τέθνηκε ταῦτα τρία ἔτη **these** three years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10 codices}}; [στρατείαν] ἑνδέκατον μῆνα τουτονὶ ποιεῖται for **these** eleven months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2, compare 3.4; τριακοστὴν ταύτην ἡμέραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 27; ταύτας τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 463b, etc.}}
**C** SIGNIFICATION AND SPECIAL IDIOMS:
**C.I** **this**, to designate **the nearer**, opposed to ἐκεῖνος, **that, the more remote**, ταῦτα, like τὰ ἐνταῦθα, **things round and about us, earthly things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e (variant)}}; compare ὅδε [near the start]: but οὗτος sometimes indicates that which is not really nearest, but most important, δεῖ.. τὸ βέλτιστον ἀεί, μὴ τὸ ῥᾷστον λέγειν: ἐπὶ ἐκεῖνο μὲν γὰρ ἡ φύσις αὐτὴ βαδιεῖται, ἐπὶ τοῦτο δὲ κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, compare 51.3 and 18.}}
**C.I.2** when, of two things, one precedes and the other follows, ὅδε properly refers to what follows, οὗτος to what precedes, οὐκ ἔστι σοι ταῦτ᾽, ἀλλά σοι τάδ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 787, compare ὅδε III. 2: frequently, however, where there are not two things, οὗτος}} refers to what follows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.377, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; οὔκουν.. τοῦτο γιγνώσκεις, ὅτι..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 379, etc.}}
**C.I.3** οὗτος is used _emphatic_, generally in contempt, while ἐκεῖνος denotes praise, ὁ πάντ᾽ ἄναλκις οὗτος, i.e. Aegisthus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 301; τούτους τοὺς συκοφάντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a; so 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “de Coron.” uses οὗτος of Aeschines, ἐκεῖνος}} of Philip; but οὗτος is used of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, 4.3.}}
**C.I.3.b** of what is familiar, τούτους τοὺς πολυτελεῖς χιτῶνας, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8; οἱ τὰς τελετὰς.. οὗτοι καταστήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, compare “Men.” 80a}}; τὸ θυλακῶδες τοῦτο the **familiar** bag-like thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, compare 3.18.11, 4.7.1; οἱ τὰς κόρας ταύτας ὠνούμενοι τοῖς παισίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.153.}}
**C.I.4** in _Attic dialect_ law-language, οὗτος is commonly applied to **the opponent**, whether plaintiff (as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.130)}} or defendant (as in {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1)}}; so, in the political speeches of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, οὗτοι}} are **the opposite party**, {{Refs|4.1, 8.7}}, etc.; but in the forensic speeches, οὗτοι frequently means **the judges, the court**, {{Refs|21.134, 36.47.}}
**C.I.5** much like an _adverb_, in local sense (compare ὅδε [near the start]), τίς δ᾽ οὗτος κατὰ νῆας.. ἔρχεα; who art thou **here** that comest.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.82}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, τίς οὑτοσ; who's this **here?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1048}}; πολλὰ ὁρῶ ταῦτα πρόβατα I see many sheep **here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9 (as variant): with _pronoun_ of 2 _person_, οὗτος σύ}} **ho** you! you **there!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 532, 1121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1280, etc.: and then οὗτος alone like a _vocative_, οὗτος, τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 240, “Nu.” 220, +others}}: with a _proper name_, ὦ οὗτος, Αἴας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος, Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1364: with _vocative_, βέντισθ᾽ οὗτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.76:—the _feminine_ is rarer, αὕτη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 922; αὕτη σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 610.}} —This phrase mostly implies anger, impatience, or scorn.
**C.II** simply as antecedent to ὅς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1180, etc.: frequently following _relative_ clause, ἅ γ᾽ ἔλαβες,.. μεθεῖναι ταῦτα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1247, compare 1319, “Ant.” 183, 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}
**C.III** = τοιοῦτος, οὗτος ἐγὼ ταχυτᾶτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; σὺ τοίνυν οὗτος εὑρέθης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 173.}}
**C.IV** after a parenthesis, the Subject, though already named, is frequently _emphatic_ repeated by οὗτος, οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐδὲ Ἀριστέης.., οὐδὲ οὗτος προσωτέρω.. ἔφησε ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 81 (assuming variant), 1.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, etc.}}
**C.V** καὶ οὗτος is added to heighten the force of a previous word, ξυνεστῶτες.. ναυτικῷ ἀγῶνι, καὶ τούτῳ πρὸς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 6.11, etc.; so οὐδὲ τούτου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100}}; see infr. VIII.2.
**C.VI** repeated, where for the second we should merely say **he** or **it**, τοῖσιν τούτου τοῦτον μέλεσιν.. κελαδοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1526, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d.}}
**C.VII** ταῦτα is used in some special phrases,
**C.VII.1** ταῦτ᾽, ὦ δέσποτα **yes** Sir (i. e. ἔστι ταῦτα, ταῦτα δράσω, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 142, “Pax” 275, compare “Eq.” III; so ταῦτα δή [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ταῦτά νυν [prev. author] “V.” 1008}}; so also ἦν ταῦτα **even so, true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 417.}}
**C.VII.2** ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ὑπάρξει **so** it shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a.}}
**C.VII.3** καὶ ταῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα so much for **that**, frequently in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c.}}
**C.VII.4** ταῦτα at end of a formula in epitaphs, etc., probably short for ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει or ὁ βίος ταῦτά ἐστιν, e.g. οὐδὶς (= -εὶς) ἀθάνατος: ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.420; Προκόπι ταῦτα [prev. work]1824; χαίρεται (= -τε) ταῦτα [prev. work]1479, etc.: similarly perhaps in a letter, ἂμ μὴ πέμψῃς, οὐ μὴ φάγω, οὐ μὴ πείνω. ταῦτα “POxy.” 119.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**C.VIII** Adverbial usages:
**C.VIII.1** ταῦτα_absolutely_, **therefore, that is why**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.694; ταῦτ᾽ ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 90, “Nu.” 319, 335, 394, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; ταῦτα δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a; ταῦτ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1358, etc.}}; αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: τοῦτο}} is rare in this sense, τοῦτ᾽ ἀφικόμην, ὅπως.. εὖ πράξαιμί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1005}}; αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο **just because of this**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204a.}}
**C.VIII.1.b** πρὸς ταῦτα **so then, therefore**, properly used in indignant defiance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 992, 1043, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971, 1115, 1313, “OT” 426, “OC” 455, etc.}}
**C.VIII.2** καὶ ταῦτα, adding a circumstance heightening the force of what has been said, **and that**, ἄνδρα γενναῖον θανεῖν, καὶ ταῦτα πρὸς γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 627}}: but mostly with a _participle_, ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας ἄστυ.., καὶ ταῦθ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν οὐδὲν ἐξειδὼς πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 704, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, etc.}}; or with a _participle_ omitted, ἥτις.. τὴν τεκοῦσαν ὕβρισεν, καὶ ταῦτα τηλικοῦτος (i.e. οὖσα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 614; so καὶ ταῦτα μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 400b.}}
**C.VIII.2.b** καὶ ταῦτα **anyhow, no matter what happens** (or **happened**), ἐπεχείρησας, οὐδὲν ὢν καὶ ταῦτα you tried, but were no good **anyhow**, i.e. try as you might, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341c, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5.}}
**C.VIII.3** τοῦτο μέν.., τοῦτο δέ.. **on the one hand.., on the other.., partly.., partly**.., very frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 1.161, +others}}; τοῦτο μέν is sometimes answered by δέ only,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.76, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670, “OC” 440}}; by δὲ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; by ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61}}; by ἀλλά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13}}; by εἶτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1345}}; by τοῦτ᾽ αὖθις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 165.}}
**C.VIII.4** _dative_ _feminine_ ταύτῃ,
**C.VIII.4.a** **on this spot, here**, ταύτῃ μὲν.., τῇδε δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1331; ἀλλ᾽ ἐὰν ταύτῃ γε νικᾷ, ταυτῃὶ πεπλήξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, compare “Th.” 1221.}}
**C.VIII.4.b** **in this point, herein**, μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 572, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.12, etc.}}
**C.VIII.4.c** **in this way, thus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 191, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1300, etc.; οὐ.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐστί πω ταύτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 843; ἀλλ᾽ οὔτι ταύτῃ ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 365, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 511: antecedent to ὥσπερ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c}}; to ὅπῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.2; οὕτω τε καὶ ταύτῃ γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d; καὶ οὕτω καὶ ταύτῃ ἂν ἔχοι [prev. work] 714d}}; ταύτῃ καλεῖσθαι, etc., like οὕτω κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b.}}
**C.VIII.5** ἐκ τούτου or τούτων **thereupon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6, “Oec.” 2.1}}; **therefore**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.3.5.}}
**C.VIII.6** ἐν τούτῳ **in that case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c.}}
**C.VIII.6.b** **in the meantime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27.}}
**C.VIII.7** πρὸς τούτοις (-οισι) **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540.}}

3779:
** οὕτως**,
and before a consonant **οὕτω** (but sometimes οὕτως before a consonant, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 63, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 522c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.34, “PPetr.” 2p.20 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 124.18 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}, and οὕτω before a vowel is found in _Epic dialect_ Poets and _Ionic dialect_ Prose, see near the end); in _Attic dialect_ strengthened
**A** οὑτωσί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 503d, etc.; οὑτωσίν Hdn.Gr. 1.509.2}}; see at {οὗτος Α}:— _adverb_ of οὗτος, **in this way** or **manner, so, thus**: οὕτως is antecedent to ὡς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.178, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.148, etc.}}; in _Attic dialect_ also οὕτως ὥσπερ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 475, etc.}}; ὥσπερ.., οὕτω καὶ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.21}}; ὡσαύτως, ὥσπερ.., οὕτω καὶ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 534a}}; also οὕτως, ὅπως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 330, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.2}}; _poetry_ also ὥστε.., οὕτω δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 116 (Lyric poetry): οὕτως}} is frequently left out after ὡς, ὡς ἔδοξεν αὐτοῖς, καὶ ἐποίουν ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.1, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.45f —In Prose, the _relative_ _pronoun_ frequently follows instead of ὥστε, see infr. III: when two modes are opposed, they are frequently expressed by οὕτω and ἐκείνως, τότε μὲν ἐκείνως, νῦν δ᾽ οὕτω 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.172; οὕτω ῥᾷον ἢ 'κείνως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 370a, etc.}} —Sts. οὕτω or -ως refers to what follows, **thus, as follows**, οὕτω χρὴ ποιεῖν: ἐὰν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.2; οὑτωσὶ δὲ λογίζεσθε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.244; οὕτω πως ὑπείληφα [prev. work]269; οὑτωσί πως}} followed by a quotation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28c}}; καὶ οὕτως even **so**, even **on this supposition**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10; οὐδ᾽ οὕτως [prev. author] 2.76, 6.89, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.14, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.3: strengthened οὕτω δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.158; οὕτω γὰρ δή τοι 15.201}}; οὕτω δή τι, see infr. III; also οὕτω που **so** I ween,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.116, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.262, etc.; οὕτω πῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.373}}; ὣς ὁ μὲν οὕτως ἐστὶ σόος **so in this way** is he saved, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.300:}} —Phrases:
**A.1** οὕτω δὴ ἔσται **so** it shall be, ratifying what goes before,{{Refs|11.348; ἔσσεται οὕτως 16.31, etc.: in Prose οὕτως}} alone in answers, **even so, just so**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.9; so ἢ οὐχ οὕτω;—οὕτω μὲν οὖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 551b, etc.}}
**A.2** _emphatic_ with the _imperative_, **just, without more ado**, ἔρρ᾽ οὕτως {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.498, compare 21.184, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.218, 17.447}}; but, οὕτω νῦν ἀπόπεμπε **as you say**, {{Refs|5.146.}}
**A.3** in wishes or prayers, **so**, i.e. if you grant my prayer, οὕτως ἔρως σοι.. τελεσφόρος γένοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 714; οὕτω τί σοι δοίησαν αἱ Μοῦσαι.. τοῦτον.. δεῖρον 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.1}}; also in protestations, **so**, i.e. only if what I say is true, οὕτως ὀναίμην τῶν τέκνων, μισῶ τὸν ἄνδρ᾽ (as in Engl., **so** help me God, **so** mote I thrive, etc.) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 469, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 530, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7. 25, 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.13}}; οὕτω.. νομιζοίμην σοφός, ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 520.}}
**A.4** in colloquial phrase, beginning a story, οὕτω ποτ᾽ ἦν μῦς καὶ γαλῆ **there** were once on a time.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 1182; οὕτως ἦν νεανίσκος [prev. author] “Lys.” 785; ἦν οὕτω δὴ παῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237b.}}
**A.5** οὕτω with _genitive_, τούτων μὲν οὕτω **so much** for this, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 950}}; οὕτω καὶ τῶν οἰκονομικῶν (variant{τῷ -κῷ}) **so** also of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1253b27}}; ὥσπερ Χαλκὶς.. τῆς Εὐβοίας.. κεῖται, οὕτω Χερρονήσου.. ἡ Καρδιανῶν πόλις as Chalcis in respect of Euboea.., **so** Cardia in respect of the Chersonese, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.182}}; so οὕτως ἔχω τινός or περί τινος, see at {ἔχω} (A) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus II. 2; also for εἰς τοῦτο, οὕτω δὲ τάρβους.. ἀφικόμην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 361 codices (uncertain reading).}}
**A.6** ὁ οὕτω καλούμενος, ὀνομαζόμενος, the **so**-called.., τῶν Ῥιπαίων οὕτω καλουμένων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.1; τοῦ καὶ ὀνομασθέντος οὕτω ῥήτορος 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.11}}; Ποταμὸς δῆμος οὕτω καλούμενος P., a deme of **that** name, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.22.}}
**A.7** οὕτω, or οὕτω δή, frequently introduces the apodosis after a protasis, ἐπειδὴ περιελήλυθε ὁ πόλεμος.., οὕτω δὴ Γέλωνος μνῆστις γέγονε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.158, compare 150, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.131, 2.12, 19, etc.}}; especially after parts., ἐν κλιβάνῳ πνίξαντες, οὕτω τρώγουσι, i.e. ἐπειδὰν πνίξωσιν, οὕτω.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92, compare 100; also οὕτω δή [prev. author] 7.174; τὰ ἄλλα καταστρεψάμενος, οὕτως.. στρατεῦσαι ὕστερον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.96; εἰς τὰ σκληρότατα ἀποβλέποντες, οὕτως ἂν μᾶλλον συννοήσαιμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 44e, compare “Grg.” 457d, 507e, “Ap.” 29b; so ἔπειτα οὕτως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.4}}: so also after the _genitive_ _absolutely_, ὡς.. τῶν ἡγεμόνων ὑμῖν μὴ μεμπτῶν γεγενημένων, οὕτω τὴν γνώμην ἔχετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.11, “An.” 1.3.6, etc.}}
**A.II** sometimes in a really inferential sense, as we say **so** for **therefore**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 677; οὕτω δή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc.}}
**A.III** **to such an extent, so, so much, so very, so excessively**, καλὸς οὕτω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.169}}; πρυμνόθεν οὕτως **so** entirely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1061 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.8; οὕτως τι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 63: frequently followed by ὡς or ὥστε, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.3, etc.: sometimes the _relative_ ὅς takes the place of ὥστε, κρήνη οὕτω δή τι ἐοῦσα πικρή, ἣ.. κιρνᾷ (i. e. ὥστε κιρνᾶν) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.52; οὐκ ἔστιν οὕτω μῶρος ὃς θανεῖν ἐρᾷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 220}}; τίς δ᾽ οὕτως ἄνους ὃς.. **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.44}}; also δυσχείμερος αὕτη ἡ.. χώρη οὕτω δή τί ἐστι, ἔνθα (i. e. ὥστε ἐνταῦθα) τοὺς μὲν ὀκτὼ τῶν μηνῶν ἀφόρητος οἷος γίνεται κρυμός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28: sometimes no connecting Particle is used, αἱ [κεφαλαὶ] οὕτω δή τι ἰσχυραί, μόγις ἂν λίθῳ παίσας διαρρήξειας}} **so excessively** hard, you could scarcely break them, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.12.}}
**A.III.2** with _superlative_ _adjective_, τῆς οὕτω μεγίστης ἐγχειρήσεως ἀποσφαλείς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus 5.19.}}
**A.IV** sometimes, like{αὔτως}, with a diminishing power, **so, merely so, simply**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always μὰψ οὕτω, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.120 (for without μάψ he always uses αὔτως),compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5; μὴ διὰ μέθης.. ἀλλ᾽ οὕτω πίνοντας πρὸς ἡδονήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176e, compare “Grg.” 494e, “Phdr.” 235c, “Tht.” 147c, 158b, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 67, etc.; ἐν συνουσίᾳ καὶ διατριβῇ οὕτως ἰδίᾳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.71, compare 1.20}}; τοὺς ὀδόντας.. οὕτως ἂν τοῖς δακτύλοις αὐτοῖς.. παρατρίβειν, i.e. without a dentifrice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Diocl. fragment 141; so οὕτω γε ἀπὸ στόματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142d; οὕτω ποτέ [prev. author] “Ly.” 216c; οὕτω πως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.20}}; also, **off-hand, at once**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 464b, etc.; ἁπλῶς οὕτως [prev. author] “Lg.” 633c}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὕτως ἄπει **; so, without a word more.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1067}}; ἢ στραφεὶς οὕτως ἴω **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 315, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 374 (Lyric poetry); ἐφεξῆς οὑτωσὶ καθεζόμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.119}}; ὡς οὕτω γ᾽ ἀκοῦσαι **at first** hearing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3b; so ὥς γε οὑτωσὶ ἀκοῦσαι [prev. author] “Ly.” 216a; ἀκούειν μὲν οὕτως ἁπλῶς [prev. author] “Phlb.” 12c}}; οὐ.. οὕτως ἄπει, = **impune**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 680.}}
**A.V** as Hebraism, οὕτως, = **such a person (thing**), οὕτως οὐδέποτε εἴδαμεν {{LXX+NT|NT.Mark.2.12, cf. LXX.Gen.29.26, NT.Matt.9.33, NT.Luke.2.48, +others}}
**B** Position of οὕτω or οὕτως, mostly **before** the word which it limits, but in Poets sometimes **after**, καλὸς οὕτω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.169; οὐδέ τι λίην οὕτω νώνυμός ἐστιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.239}}; ἔρημος οὕτω, ἄγαν οὕτω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 487, 598}}: rarely at the end of a clause, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.255, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.170 (uncertain reading)}}: sometimes separated from the word which it limits, οὕτως ἔχει τι δεινόν **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 104; οὕτως ἐπὶ δεινὰς ἁρπαγάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 391d, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11}}; οὕτω δ᾽ ἦν ὁ Φίλιππος ἐν φόβῳ.., ὥστε.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.33.}}
**C** Prosody. The last syllable of οὕτω is short before a vowel in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.169, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.315}}. The ι of οὑτωσί is always long, see at {οὗτος} [near the start]

3780:
Included with: ** οὐ**,
the negative of **fact** and **statement**, as μή of **will** and **thought**; οὐ denies, μή rejects; οὐ is absolute, μή relative; οὐ objective, μή subjective. —The same differences hold for all compounds of οὐ and μή, and some examples of οὐδέ and οὐδείς are included below.—As to the Form, see infr. G. USAG{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}}
**I** as the negative of single words,
**II** as the negative of the sentence.
**I** οὐ adhering to single words so as to form a quasi-_compound_ with them:—with Verbs: οὐ δίδωμι **withhold**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.296}}; οὐκ εἰῶ **prevent**, {{Refs|2.132, 4.55, +others}}; οὐκ ἐθέλω **refuse**, {{Refs|1.112, 3.289, +others}}; οὔ φημι **deny**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|7.393, 23.668, +others (In most of these uses μή can replace οὐ when the _construction_ requires it, e.g. εἰ μή φησι ταῦτα ἀληθῆ εἶναι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 34}}; but sometimes οὐ is retained, εἰ δ᾽ ἂν.. οὐκ ἐθέλωσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.289; εἰ δέ κ᾽.. ου᾽κ εἰῶσι 20.139; ἐὰν οὐ φάσκῃ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.76}}; ἐάντε.. οὐ (variant{μή}) φῆτε ἐάντε φῆτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25b):—with Participles: οὐκ ἐθέλων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.224, 300, 6.165, etc.:— with Adjectives: οὐκ ἀέκοντε 5.366, 768, +others; οὐ πολλήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.7, etc.:— with Adverbs: οὐχ ἥκιστα [prev. author] 1.68, etc.: rarely with Verbal Nouns (see. below 11.10).}} —On the use of οὐ in contrasts, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II** as negativing the whole sentence,
**II.1** οὐ is frequently used alone, sometimes with the ellipsis of a definite Verb, οὔκ (i.e. ἀποκερῇ), ἄν γε ἐμοὶ πείθῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89b}}: sometimes as negativing the preceding sentence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 850, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.19: as a Particle of solemn denial frequently with μά (which see)}} and the _accusative_; sometimes without μά, οὐ τὸν πάντων θεῶν θεὸν πρόμον Ἅλιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 660 (Lyric poetry), compare 1088 (Lyric poetry), “El.” 1063 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 758.}}
**II.2** with _indicative_ of statement, τὴν δ᾽ ἐγὼ οὐ λύσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.29, compare 114, 495}}; οὐ φθίνει Κροίσου φιλόφρων ἀρετά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.94; ἔνθα κεν οὔ τιν᾽ ἀδάκρυτόν γ᾽ ἐνόησας Ἀργείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.61; οὔ κεν.. ἔπαξε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.25; οὐκ ἂν ὑπεξέφυγε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.369.}}
**II.3** with _subjunctive_ in _future_ sense, only in _Epic dialect_, οὐ γάρ τίς με βίῃ γε ἑκὼν ἀέκοντα δίηται {{Refs|7.197; οὐκ ἄν τοι χραίς μῃ κίθαρις 3.54, compare 11.387.}}
**II.4** with _optative_ in potential sense (without ἄν or κεν), also _Epic dialect_, ὃ οὐ δύο γ᾽ ἄνδρε φέροιεν {{Refs|5.303, 20.286.}}
**II.5** with _optative_ and ἄν, κείνοισι δ᾽ ἂν οὔ τις.. μαχέοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.271, compare 301, 2.250, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.63, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 155 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 310, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 403, etc.}}
**II.6** in dependent clauses οὐ is used,
**II.6.a** with ὅτι or ὡς, after Verbs of saying, knowing, and showing, ἐκ μέν τοι ἐρέω.. ὡς ἐγὼ οὔ τι ἑκὼν κατερύκομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.377, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 561, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.8, etc.}}: so with _indicative_ or _optative_ and ἄν, ἀπελογοῦντο ὡς οὐκ ἄν ποτε οὕτω μωροὶ ἦσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330a; ὡς δὲ οὐκ ἂν δικαίως αὐτοὺς δέχοισθε μαθεῖν χρή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.3, etc.: with _optative_ representing _indicative_ in indirect speech, ἔλεξε παιδὶ σῷ.. ὡς.. Ἕλληνες οὐ μενοῖεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 358, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 346, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b, etc.: for μή}} in such sentences, see at {μή} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.}}
**II.6.b** in all causal sentences, and in temporal and Relat. sentences unless there is conditional or final meaning, χωσαμένη, ὅ οἱ οὔ τι θαλύσια.. ῥέξε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.534; ἄχθεται ὅτι οὐ κάρτα θεραπεύεται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80; διότι οὐκ ἦσαν δίκαι, οὐ δυνατοὶ ἦμεν παρ᾽ αὐτῶν ἃ ὤφειλον πράξασθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.3; μή με κτεῖν᾽, ἐπεὶ οὐχ ὁμογάστριος Ἕκτορός εἰμι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.95, etc.; νῦν δὲ ἐπειδὴ οὐκ ἐθέλεις.., εἶμι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c; ἐπειδὴ τὸ χωρίον οὐχ ἡλίσκετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.102; νηπιάχοις οἷς οὔ τι μέλει πολεμήϊα ἔργα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.338, etc.}}: in causal relative sentences, οἵτινές σε οὐχὶ ἐσώσαμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 46a}}; especially in the combinations, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅστις οὐ.., as οὐκ ἔστ᾽ ἐραστὴς ὅστις οὐκ ἀεὶ φιλεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1051, compare “Hec.” 298; οὔτις ἔσθ᾽ ὃς οὔ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 725}}; οὐδείς ἐστιν ὅστις οὐ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.180.}}
**II.6.c** after ὥστε with _indicative_ or _optative_ with ἄν, ὥστ᾽ οὐ δυνατόν σ᾽ εἵργειν ἔσται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 384, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 98, “OT” 411; οὕτως αὐτοὺς ἀγαπῶμεν.. ὥστε.. οὐκ ἂν ἐθελήσαιμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.45; οὐκ ἂν ὡρκίζομεν αὐτὸν ὥστε τῆς εἰρήνης ἂν διημαρτήκει καὶ οὐκ ἂν ἀμφότερ᾽ εἶχε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.30: ὥστε οὐ with}}_infinitive_ is almost invariably due to indirect speech, ὥστ᾽ οὐκ αἰσχύνεσθαι (for οὐκ αἰσχύνονται) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 19.308, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.40, 8.76, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.6, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.27 (conjecture Reiske).}}—Rarely not in indirect speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 780, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1358, “Hel.” 108, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.2, 9.48.}}
**II.7** in a conditional clause μή is necessary, except,
**II.7.a** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, when the εἰ}} clause precedes the apodosis and the verb is _indicative_, εἰ δέ μοι οὐκ ἐπέεσσ᾽ ἐπιπείσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.162, compare 178, 20.129, 24.296, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.274, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.160, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.382, 13.144 (9.410 is an exception).}}
**II.7.b** when the εἰ clause is really causal, as after Verbs expressing surprise or emotion, μὴ θαυμάσῃς, εἰ πολλὰ τῶν εἰρημένων οὐ πρέπει σοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.44; κατοικτῖραι.., εἰ.. οὐδεὶς ἐς ἑκατοστὸν ἔτος περιέσται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1242; so also δεινὸν γὰρ ἂν εἴη πρῆγμα, εἰ Σάκας μὲν καταστρεψάμενοι δούλους ἔχομεν, Ἕλληνας δὲ οὐ τιμωρησόμεθα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.102, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.8 (probably), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.55; οὐκ αἰσχρόν, εἰ τὸ μὲν Ἀργείων πλῆθος οὐκ ἐφοβήθη τὴν Λακεδαιμονίων ἀρχήν, ὑμεῖς δ᾽ ὄντες Ἀθηναῖοι βάρβαρον ἄνθρωπον.. φοβήσεσθ; [prev. author] 15.23, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.13.}}
**II.7.c** when οὐ belongs closely to the next word (see. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus I)}}, or is quoted unchanged, εἰ, ὡς νῦν φήσει, οὐ παρεσκευάσατο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.29 codices}}; εἰ δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί (i.e. ὥσπερ λέγεις), τίνι τρόπῳ διεφθάρη **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 347.}}
**II.8** οὐ is used with _infinitive_ in indirect speech, when it represents the _indicative_ of orat. recta, φαμὲν δέ οἱ οὐ τελέεσθαι [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.664, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.174, 21.316, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1389, etc.; λέγοντες οὐκ εἶναι αὐτόνομοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.67, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.18; οἶμαι.. οὐκ ὀλίγον ἔργον αὐτὸ εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369b, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1051, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, etc.; ἡγήσαντο ἡμᾶς οὐ περιόψεσθαι [prev. work] 39. (For the occasional use of μή, see at {μή} 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5c}}]; sometimes we have οὐ and μή in consecutive clauses, οἶμαι σοῦ κάκιον οὐδὲν ἂν τούτων κρατύνειν μηδ᾽ ἐπιθύνειν χερί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1058s q.; αὐτὸ ἡγοῦμαι οὐ διδακτὸν εἶναι μηδὲ.. παρασκευαστόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319b.)}}
**II.9** οὐ is used with the _participle_, when it can be resolved into a finite sentence with οὐ, as after Verbs of knowing and showing, τὸν κατθανόνθ᾽ ὁρῶντες οὐ τιμώμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 316; κατενόησαν οὐ πολλοὺς τοὺς Θηβαίους ὄντας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2. 3; ἔργῳ δηλώσω οὐ παραγενόμενος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.8, etc.}}; or into a causal sentence, τῶν βαρβάρων οἱ πολλοὶ ἐν τῇ θαλάσσῃ διεφθάρησαν νέειν οὐκ ἐπιστάμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.89; τὴν Μένδην πόλιν ἅτε οὐκ ἀπὸ ξυμβάσεως ἀνοιχθεῖσαν διήρπασαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.130}}; or into a concessive sentence, δόξω γυναῖκα καίπερ οὐκ ἔχων ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 352, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 377, etc.: regularly with ὡς}} and _participle_, ὡς οὐχὶ συνδράσουσα νουθετεῖς τάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 1025, etc.; ἐθορυβεῖτε ὡς οὐ ποιήσοντες ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.73, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 884, “Aj.” 682, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.99, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2, 5, 28, 68, 90; ὥσπερ οὐ πάντας τούτῳ τῷ τεκμηρίῳ χρωμένους 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 90, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.11}}:—for exceptions, see at {μή} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 6.}}
**II.9.b** when the _participle_ is used with the _Article_, μή is generally used, unless there is a distinct reference to a fact, when οὐ is occasionally found, ἡμεῖς δὲ ἀπὸ τῆς οὐκ οὔσης ἔτι [πόλεως] ὁρμώμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74; τοὺς ἐν τῇ πόλει οὐδὲν εἰδότας [prev. author] 4.111; οἱ οὐκ ἐθέλοντες 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.26; τῶν οὐ βουλομένων 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.9; τοὺς οὐδὲν ἀδικοῦντας ἀκρίτους ἀπέκτειναν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.82, compare τὸν οὐδὲ συμπενθῆσαι τὰς τῆς πατρίδος συμφορὰς τολμήσαντα (preceded by τὸν.. μήτε ὅπλα θέμενον ὑπὲρ τῆς πατρίδος μήτε τὸ σῶμα παρασχόντα κτλ.) 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 43; τὸ οὐχ εὑρημένον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427e.}}
**II.10** Adjectives and abstract Substantives with the article commonly take μή (see. μή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 7) but οὐ}} is occasionally used, τὰς οὐκ ἀναγκαίας πόσεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.4; τοὺς οὐδένας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 371; τὸν οὐδέν [prev. author] “Ph.” 598 (whereas ὁ μηδείς, τὸ μηδέν is the rule)}}; τὴν τῶν γεφυρῶν οὐ διάλυσιν the **non**- dissolution of the bridges, the fact **of their not**being broken up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137; ἡ οὐ περιτείχισις [prev. author] 3.95; ἡ τῶν χωρίων οὐκ ἀπόδοσις [prev. author] 5.35, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 196 (_anapaest meter_)}}; so without the article, ἐν οὐ καιπῷ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 1287; οὐ πάλης ὕπο [prev. work] 455.}}
**II.11** for οὐ μή, see entry
**II.12** in questions οὐ ordinarily expects a positive answer, οὔ νυ καὶ ἄλλοι ἔασι..**;** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.165}}; οὐχ ὁράᾳς..**; dost** thou **not** see? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.545; οὐκ.. ᾐσθόμη; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 956}}: so as a strong form of _imperative_,;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 273, “fragment” 310; πῶς δ᾽ οὐχ; [prev. author] “Supp.” 918, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1027}}; ἐμὸς μὲν **οὐχί** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 859: also in Prose, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, +others, NT.1Cor.5.12, etc.}}: the diphthong is genuine and always written ου (ουκ, ουδε, etc.) in early Inscrr., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.22, etc.; in 4th c.BC rarely written οκ, [prev. work] 22.1635.112,116,121}}; οὐ abbreviated ο, Suid. see at {Φιλοξένου γραμμάτιον}.
**H** ACCENTUATION. οὐ is oxytone _accusative_ to Hdn.Gr.1.494 (text doubtfulin 504): [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 166b6, referring to 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.328 τὸ μὲν ου (i.e. οὐ ={οὒ}) καταπύθεται ὄμβρῳ, says λύουσι.. τῇ προσῳδίᾳ λέγοντες τὸ ου ὀξύτερον (i.e. οὗ), compare 178b3. In codices the word is written oxytone when followed by a pause (see. supr. B), and is usually written without any accent in other cases.}}]
**H.I** οὐ in connexion with other Particles will be found in alphabetical order, οὐ γάρ, οὐ μή, etc.—The corresponding forms of μή should be compared.

3782:
** ὀφειλ-ή**, ἡ,
**debt**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Vect.” (cited in “Etymologicum Magnum” 644.3), “Ostr.Bodl.” i 311 (3rd c.BC), “BGU” 1158.18 (1st c.BC), 112.11 (1st c.AD), +others, NT.Matt.18.32.}}
**2** **one's due,** {{NT|NT.Rom.13.7, NT.1Cor.7.3.}}

3784:
** ὀφείλ-ω**,
_imperfect_ ὤφειλον; _Epic dialect_ **ὀφέλλω** (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(2).67.7 (Mytil.), and 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, [prev. work] 5(2).343.27 (Orchom. 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 4th c.BC))}}_imperfect_ ὤφελλον or ὄφελλον, see infr. II. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2, 3 (the _Attic dialect_ or _Ionic dialect_ ὀφείλετ᾽, ὄφειλον in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.686, 688, 698, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 174 is probably due to the Copyists)}}: _future_ ὀφειλήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.28, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.7, also ὀφειλέσω “TAM” 2.431, +others}}: _aorist 1_ ὠφείλησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 115, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.5}} (ἐπ-): _perfect_ ὠφείληκα: _pluperfect_ **-ήκειν**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45. 33:}}_aorist 2_ ὤφελον (see. below {{Refs|11.2, 3)}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ _participle_ ὀφειληθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.63}}. (Cretan dialect **ὀφήλω** {{Refs|“GDI” 5015.21}}, written **ὀπέλο** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Leg.Gort.” 10.20, +others, 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **ὀφέλλω** (see. above) and **ὀφήλω** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 306.40 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}: in early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. written both ὀφελ **-IG** 12.91.8{{Refs|+others, and ὀφειλ- [prev. work]109.9, +others)}}:—**owe, have to pay** or **account for**, τὸ καὶ μοιχάγρι᾽ ὀφέλλει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.332}}; ὅτι μοι.. ζωάγρι᾽ ὀφέλλεις{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 462; χρεῖος, τό ῥά οἱ πᾶς δῆμος ὄφελλεν 21.17; πολέσιν γὰρ Ἐπειοὶ χρεῖος ὄφειλον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.688; ζημίην ὀ. τῷ θεῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52, etc.: _metaphorically_, μητέρα μοι ζώουσαν ὀφέλλετε 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 126}}; τί ὀφείλω**;** what **do I owe?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 21}}; ὀ. ἀργύριον, χρέα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 115, “Nu.” 117; ὀ. ἢ θεῷ θυσίας ἢ ἀνθρώπῳ χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331b; ὀ. τινὶ δρᾶν τι [prev. work] 332a: with _dative_ only, ὀ. τινί}} **to be debtor** to another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1135, “Lys.” 581, etc.; τρίτον δὲ χαίρειν, εἶτ᾽ ὀ. μηδενί 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 163}}: _absolutely_, **to be in debt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 485, etc.}}; οἱ ὀφείλοντες **debtors**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1167b21, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.832a}}:—_passive_, **to be due**, ἔνθα χρεῖός μοι ὀφέλλεται (variant{ὀφείλεται}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.367; χρεῖος ὀφείλετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.686, 698; ἢν.. ὀφείληταί τί μοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 484; μισθὸς τοῖς στρατιώταις ὠφείλετο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; τὸ ὀφειλόμενον **a debt**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.7.34; -όμενα ἀποδιδόντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.99, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331e.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ὀ. μέλος τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).3; πολλὰ δώμασιν καλά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 287}}; ὀ. χάριν, see at {χάρις} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.2; Ἀπόλλωνι χαριστήρια 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.28; τὴν ψυχὴν πᾶσιν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 10.5}}:—_passive_, ὀφείλεταί τινι ἐκ θεῶν κλέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 315; ὀ. τινὶ εὐεργεσία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137; ἀντὶ χαρίτων ἔχθραι ὀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.32; τοῖς μὲν ἐχθροῖς βλάβην ὀ., τοῖς δὲ φίλοις ὠφελίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 335e, compare 332b}}; τοὐφειλόμενον πράσσουσα Δίκη **what is due**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 310.}}
**3** as a legal term, **to be bound to render**, εὐθύνας ὤφειλον [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.73 codices (_falsa lectio_ for{ὦφλον}): hence, like{ὀφλισκάνω}}}], **incur** a penalty, ζημίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.10; διπλῆν τὴν βλάβην [prev. author] 1.32, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 360; τὴν τοιαύτην δίκην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 909a, compare 774b, 774d, 844e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.77; ἁμαρτίαν ὀ. Μηνὶ Τυράννῳ “IG” 3.74.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**4** in _passive_, of persons, **to be due** or **liable to**, θανάτῳ πάντες ὀφειλόμεθα {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 122, cf. LXX.Wis.12.20, “IG” 3.1381; but τοιαύταις χερσὶν ὀφειλόμεθα}}**our help is due,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 9.283 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** with _infinitive_, **to be bound, to be obliged** to do, ὀφέλλετε ταῦτα πένεσθαι **ye are bound, ye ought to**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.200, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41, 42, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 682, 712, etc.}}; and of things, **ought** to be, ὁ λόγος οὐκ ἀκριβῶς ὀ. λέγεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1104a2}}:—_passive_, δράσαντι γάρ τοι καὶ παθεῖν ὀφείλεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 456}}; σοὶ τοῦτ᾽ ὀφείλεται παθεῖν **it is** thy **destiny** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1421, compare “El.” 1173; ὡς πᾶσιν ἡμῖν κατθανεῖν ὀφείλεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 419, compare 782, “Or.” 1245, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.11; see above 1.4.}}
**II.2** in this _signification_ _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ὤφελλον or ὄφελλον and _aorist_ ὤφελον or ὄφελον are used of that which one has not, but **ought to have**, done (**ought** being the pret. of **owe**), ὤφελεν ἀθανάτοισιν εὔχεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.546; νῦν ὄφελεν πονέεσθαι λισσόμενος 10.117, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.472.}}
**II.3** these tenses are also used, followed by _present_ or _aorist_ _infinitive_, in wishes that something were or had been in present or past, ἀνδρὸς.. ὤφελλον ἀμείνονος εἶναι ἄκοιτις I **ought to** be.., **would that** I were.. ! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.350}}; τὴν ὄφελ᾽ ἐν νήεσσι κατακτάμεν Ἄρτεμις **would that** Artemis had slain her !, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.59, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.97; τιμήν πέρ μοι ὄφελλεν Ὀλύμπιος ἐγγυαλίξαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.353: frequently preceded by εἴθε (_Epic dialect_ αἴθε), ὡς, ὡς δή, which express the wish still more strongly, αἴθ᾽ ὄφελες ἄγονός τ᾽ ἔμεναι ἄγαμός τ᾽ ἀπολέσθαι}} **O that thou hadst!**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.40, compare 1.415, etc.; αἴθ᾽ ὤφελλες.. σημαίνειν 14.84; αἴθ᾽ ὤφελλ᾽ ὁ ξεῖνος.. ὀλέσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.401; αἴθ᾽ ἅμα πάντες.. ὠφέλετε.. ἐπὶ νηυσὶ πεφάσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.254: with ὡς, ὡς ὄφελον.. ἑλέσθαι}} **O that I had.. !**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.380; θανέειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.274; ὡς πρὶν ὤφελλον ὀλέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.764, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.68; ὡς ὤφελες αὐτόθ᾽ ὀλέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.428; ὡς.. ὤφελες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.184}}; ὡς ὄφελεν.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.173, etc.: strengthened, ὡς δὴ ἔγωγ᾽ ὄφελον.. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.217: also with _negative_, μὴ ὄφελες λίσσεσθαι}}.. **would thou hadst** never.. !, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.698; ἣ μὴ ὤφελλε γενέσθαι 17.686; τὼ μὴ γείνασθαι ὄφελλον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.312; ὡς μὴ ὤφελλε τεκέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.481; ὡς δὴ μὴ ὄφελον νικᾶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.548.—So in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ὤφελον.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1157}}; ὤφελες.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 865}}; ὤφελε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.4, etc.: also, as in _Epic dialect_, εἴθ᾽ ὤφελες.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1021}}; εἴθ᾽ ὤφελ᾽.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 41, etc.}}; εἰ γὰρ ὤφελον.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ec.” 380, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432c, “Cri.” 44d}}; ὡς ὤφελες.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 955: with _negative_, μήποτ᾽ ὤφελον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 969, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 880 (_anapaest meter_), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.288}}; ὡς μήποτ᾽ ὤφελον.. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 286; ὡς μηδὲ νῦν ὤφελον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.78: without augment in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἶδον.. τὸ μὴ ἰδεῖν ὄφελον (variant{ὤ-}) 1.111, compare 3.65: sometimes in Trag. (Lyric poetry and _anapaest meter_), εἴθ᾽ ὄφελε.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 915}}]; ὄφελε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1192}}; μήποτ᾽ ὄφελον.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1413. (In this signification ὤφειλον}} is used in late _Epic dialect_, ὡς μὴ ὤφειλες ἱκέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 5.194, but ὤφελλον should be read in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 174 and ὤφελε in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1291.)}}
**II.3.b** with _indicative_, ὤφελε μηδ᾽ ἐγένοντο θοαὶ νέες {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 19.1, compare 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 10.378, etc.}}
**II.3.c** ὄφελον (_adverb_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 142.9, “Etymologicum Magnum” 643.48)}} in this _signification_: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ὤμοι ἐγών, ὄφελόν με.. ὀλέσθαι {{NT+1st c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 1159: even with 2 _person_ of Verb, ὄφελον ἐβασιλεύσατε NT.1Cor.4.8, cf. NT.2Cor.11.1, NT.Gal.5.12, NT.Rev.3.15, LXX.Job.14.13, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.156a; ὄφελον δυνήσῃ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 1 (as a solecism): with 3 _person_, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.18.15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 38.47: with 1 _person_ _plural_, ὄφελον ἀπεθάνομεν LXX.Exo.16.3}}; ὤφελον (sic) εἰ ἐδυνάμεθα πέτασθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 17.10 (2nd c.AD): with}}_infinitive_, ὄφελομ μὲν ἡ θεὸς.. στερῆσαι.. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 315.16 (Pessinus, 2nd c.BC).}}
**III** _impersonal_ ὀφείλει, **it behoves**, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.6}}; ὄφελλέ με μήτε.. εἰσοράαν κτλ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.678: so _person_ in}}_participle_, _absolutely_, αἱ ὀφείλουσαι ἱερουργίαι τῶν θεῶν the **due** services of the gods, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 294.24 (2nd c.AD)}}; κατὰ τὸν ὀφείλοντα καιρόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.79}}. (ὦφλον, ὤφληκα, _aorist_ and _perfect_ of ὀφλισκάνω, were probably originally _aorist_ and _perfect_ of ὀφείλω: ὄφελον in signf. II. {{Refs|3c}} may be originally _neuter_ _participle_ of ὤφελε (_signification_ {{Refs|111) with omission of ἐστί.)}}

