 ὀ. ἀργύριον, χρέα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 115, “Nu.” 117; ὀ. ἢ θεῷ θυσίας ἢ ἀνθρώπῳ χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331b; ὀ. τινὶ δρᾶν τι [prev. work] 332a: with _dative_ only, ὀ. τινί}} **to be debtor** to another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1135, “Lys.” 581, etc.; τρίτον δὲ χαίρειν, εἶτ᾽ ὀ. μηδενί 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 163}}: _absolutely_, **to be in debt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 485, etc.}}; οἱ ὀφείλοντες **debtors**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1167b21, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.832a}}:—_passive_, **to be due**, ἔνθα χρεῖός μοι ὀφέλλεται (variant{ὀφείλεται}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.367; χρεῖος ὀφείλετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.686, 698; ἢν.. ὀφείληταί τί μοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 484; μισθὸς τοῖς στρατιώταις ὠφείλετο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; τὸ ὀφειλόμενον **a debt**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.7.34; -όμενα ἀποδιδόντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.99, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331e.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ὀ. μέλος τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).3; πολλὰ δώμασιν καλά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 287}}; ὀ. χάριν, see at {χάρις} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.2; Ἀπόλλωνι χαριστήρια 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.28; τὴν ψυχὴν πᾶσιν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 10.5}}:—_passive_, ὀφείλεταί τινι ἐκ θεῶν κλέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 315; ὀ. τινὶ εὐεργεσία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137; ἀντὶ χαρίτων ἔχθραι ὀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.32; τοῖς μὲν ἐχθροῖς βλάβην ὀ., τοῖς δὲ φίλοις ὠφελίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 335e, compare 332b}}; τοὐφειλόμενον πράσσουσα Δίκη **what is due**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 310.}}
**3** as a legal term, **to be bound to render**, εὐθύνας ὤφειλον [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.73 codices (_falsa lectio_ for{ὦφλον}): hence, like{ὀφλισκάνω}}}], **incur** a penalty, ζημίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.10; διπλῆν τὴν βλάβην [prev. author] 1.32, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 360; τὴν τοιαύτην δίκην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 909a, compare 774b, 774d, 844e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.77; ἁμαρτίαν ὀ. Μηνὶ Τυράννῳ “IG” 3.74.15 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**4** in _passive_, of persons, **to be due** or **liable to**, θανάτῳ πάντες ὀφειλόμεθα {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 122, cf. LXX.Wis.12.20, “IG” 3.1381; but τοιαύταις χερσὶν ὀφειλόμεθα}}**our help is due,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 9.283 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** with _infinitive_, **to be bound, to be obliged** to do, ὀφέλλετε ταῦτα πένεσθαι **ye are bound, ye ought to**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.200, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41, 42, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 682, 712, etc.}}; and of things, **ought** to be, ὁ λόγος οὐκ ἀκριβῶς ὀ. λέγεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1104a2}}:—_passive_, δράσαντι γάρ τοι καὶ παθεῖν ὀφείλεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 456}}; σοὶ τοῦτ᾽ ὀφείλεται παθεῖν **it is** thy **destiny** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1421, compare “El.” 1173; ὡς πᾶσιν ἡμῖν κατθανεῖν ὀφείλεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 419, compare 782, “Or.” 1245, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.11; see above 1.4.}}
**II.2** in this _signification_ _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ὤφελλον or ὄφελλον and _aorist_ ὤφελον or ὄφελον are used of that which one has not, but **ought to have**, done (**ought** being the pret. of **owe**), ὤφελεν ἀθανάτοισιν εὔχεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.546; νῦν ὄφελεν πονέεσθαι λισσόμενος 10.117, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.472.}}
**II.3** these tenses are also used, followed by _present_ or _aorist_ _infinitive_, in wishes that something were or had been in present or past, ἀνδρὸς.. ὤφελλον ἀμείνονος εἶναι ἄκοιτις I **ought to** be.., **would that** I were.. ! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.350}}; τὴν ὄφελ᾽ ἐν νήεσσι κατακτάμεν Ἄρτεμις **would that** Artemis had slain her !, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.59, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.97; τιμήν πέρ μοι ὄφελλεν Ὀλύμπιος ἐγγυαλίξαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.353: frequently preceded by εἴθε (_Epic dialect_ αἴθε), ὡς, ὡς δή, which express the wish still more strongly, αἴθ᾽ ὄφελες ἄγονός τ᾽ ἔμεναι ἄγαμός τ᾽ ἀπολέσθαι}} **O that thou hadst!**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.40, compare 1.415, etc.; αἴθ᾽ ὤφελλες.. σημαίνειν 14.84; αἴθ᾽ ὤφελλ᾽ ὁ ξεῖνος.. ὀλέσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.401; αἴθ᾽ ἅμα πάντες.. ὠφέλετε.. ἐπὶ νηυσὶ πεφάσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.254: with ὡς, ὡς ὄφελον.. ἑλέσθαι}} **O that I had.. !**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.380; θανέειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.274; ὡς πρὶν ὤφελλον ὀλέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.764, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.68; ὡς ὤφελες αὐτόθ᾽ ὀλέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.428; ὡς.. ὤφελες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.184}}; ὡς ὄφελεν.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.173, etc.: strengthened, ὡς δὴ ἔγωγ᾽ ὄφελον.. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.217: also with _negative_, μὴ ὄφελες λίσσεσθαι}}.. **would thou hadst** never.. !, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.698; ἣ μὴ ὤφελλε γενέσθαι 17.686; τὼ μὴ γείνασθαι ὄφελλον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.312; ὡς μὴ ὤφελλε τεκέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.481; ὡς δὴ μὴ ὄφελον νικᾶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.548.—So in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ὤφελον.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1157}}; ὤφελες.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 865}}; ὤφελε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.4, etc.: also, as in _Epic dialect_, εἴθ᾽ ὤφελες.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1021}}; εἴθ᾽ ὤφελ᾽.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 41, etc.}}; εἰ γὰρ ὤφελον.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ec.” 380, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432c, “Cri.” 44d}}; ὡς ὤφελες.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 955: with _negative_, μήποτ᾽ ὤφελον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 969, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 880 (_anapaest meter_), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.288}}; ὡς μήποτ᾽ ὤφελον.. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 286; ὡς μηδὲ νῦν ὤφελον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.78: without augment in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἶδον.. τὸ μὴ ἰδεῖν ὄφελον (variant{ὤ-}) 1.111, compare 3.65: sometimes in Trag. (Lyric poetry and _anapaest meter_), εἴθ᾽ ὄφελε.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 915}}]; ὄφελε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1192}}; μήποτ᾽ ὄφελον.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1413. (In this signification ὤφειλον}} is used in late _Epic dialect_, ὡς μὴ ὤφειλες ἱκέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 5.194, but ὤφελλον should be read in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 174 and ὤφελε in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1291.)}}
**II.3.b** with _indicative_, ὤφελε μηδ᾽ ἐγένοντο θοαὶ νέες {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 19.1, compare 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 10.378, etc.}}
**II.3.c** ὄφελον (_adverb_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 142.9, “Etymologicum Magnum” 643.48)}} in this _signification_: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ὤμοι ἐγών, ὄφελόν με.. ὀλέσθαι {{NT+1st c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 1159: even with 2 _person_ of Verb, ὄφελον ἐβασιλεύσατε NT.1Cor.4.8, cf. NT.2Cor.11.1, NT.Gal.5.12, NT.Rev.3.15, LXX.Job.14.13, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.156a; ὄφελον δυνήσῃ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 1 (as a solecism): with 3 _person_, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.18.15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 38.47: with 1 _person_ _plural_, ὄφελον ἀπεθάνομεν LXX.Exo.16.3}}; ὤφελον (sic) εἰ ἐδυνάμεθα πέτασθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 17.10 (2nd c.AD): with}}_infinitive_, ὄφελομ μὲν ἡ θεὸς.. στερῆσαι.. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 315.16 (Pessinus, 2nd c.BC).}}
**III** _impersonal_ ὀφείλει, **it behoves**, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.6}}; ὄφελλέ με μήτε.. εἰσοράαν κτλ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.678: so _person_ in}}_participle_, _absolutely_, αἱ ὀφείλουσαι ἱερουργίαι τῶν θεῶν the **due** services of the gods, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 294.24 (2nd c.AD)}}; κατὰ τὸν ὀφείλοντα καιρόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.79}}. (ὦφλον, ὤφληκα, _aorist_ and _perfect_ of ὀφλισκάνω, were probably originally _aorist_ and _perfect_ of ὀφείλω: ὄφελον in signf. II. {{Refs|3c}} may be originally _neuter_ _participle_ of ὤφελε (_signification_ {{Refs|111) with omission of ἐστί.)}}

3786:
** ὄφελος**, τό,
(ὀφέλλω B) only in _nominative_ and _accusative_ _singular_, **furtherance, advantage, help**, αἴ κ᾽ ὄφελός τι γενώμεθα in case we can be of any **use**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.236}}; ὅς τοι πόλλ᾽ ὄ. γένετο who was a **help** to thee in many matters, {{Refs|17.152}}; οὐδὲν σοί γ᾽ ὄ. not as any **good** to thee,; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 504e, compare 513e}}; τί ζῆν ὄ. ᾧ μή 'στι τὸ ζῆν εἰδέναι **;** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 104.}}
**2** with _genitive_, φυγᾶς ὄ. εἴ τί μοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 737 (Lyric poetry); τῶν ὄ. ἐστι οὐδέν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.68. ; οἷσι.. οὐδὲν ὄ. ἐστιν οὔτε χρημάτων οὔτε τῶν ἄλλων οὐδενὸς ἄτερ τῆς ὑγιείης 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 3.69; τί τῆς εὐμορφίας ὄ.; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 548}}; ἐλευθερίας οὐδὲν ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.17; ἐάν τι ἡμῶν ὡς νομοθετῶν ὄ. ᾖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 530c; ἀνὴρ ὅτου τι καὶ σμικρὸν ὄ. ἐστιν [prev. author] “Ap.” 28b; γεωργοῦ ἀργοῦ οὐδὲν ὄφελος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.18; μαγείρῳ μαχαίρας οὐδὲν ὄ. ἥτις μὴ τέμνει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.46; τί δ᾽ ὄ. εὖ λαλοῦντος, ἂν κακῶς φρον; 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 821.}}
**3** ὅ τι πέρ ἐστ᾽ ὄ. ἐν τῇ πόλει all **that is best**, all the best people, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 53, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herod.” 8: with _genitive_, ὅ τι περ ὄ. τοῦ στρατεύματος}} **the serviceable part** (or perhaps **the flower**) of the army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.6, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.18}}; ὅ τι περ.. ἀπληστίας ὄ. the **flower (acme**) of gluttony, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 55.}}
**4** with _genitive_ objecti, **help against** a thing, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 518.}}

3788:
** ὀφθαλμός**, ὁ,
(ὄπωπ-α, ὀφ-θῆναι, see at {ὄψ} B) **eye**, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} mostly in _plural_; ὀφθαλμοὶ δ᾽ ὡς εἰ κέρα ἕστασαν.. ἀτρέμας ἐν βλεφάροισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.211: _sing._, παίειν τινὰ ἐς τὸν ὀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.22}}: the _plural_ continued most common, but the dual also occurs, as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 362: _plural_ is used in many phrases, ἐλθέμεν ἐς ὀφθαλμούς τινος}} before one's **eyes**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.204; οὐδ᾽ Ἀχιλῆος ὀφθαλμοὺς εἴσειμι [prev. work] 463}}; ὀφθαλμοῖσιν ἰδεῖν, ὁρᾶσθαι, etc.,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.275, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.47, etc.}}; but ἐν ὀφθαλμοῖσιν ὁρᾶν, νοεῖν, to see before one's **eyes**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.459, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.312}}; ἔχειν ἐν ὀ. to have before one's **eyes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.29}}; τὰ ἐν ὀ. what is before one's **eyes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174c}}; τὸ ἐν τοῖς ὀ. δὴ γελοῖον what was ridiculous **to the eye**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 452d}}; ἐπίπροσθε τῶν ὀ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 213a; πρὸ τῶν ὀ. προφαίνεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.148; ἐπ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 20}}; γενέσθαι τινὶ ἐξ ὀφθαλμῶν to get out of any one's **sight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106; ἐξ ὀ. ἀποπέμψασθαι [prev. author] 1.120; ἐξ ὀ. ποιεῖν 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.20}}; κατ᾽ ὀφθαλμοὺς λέγειν τινί to tell one **to one's face**, opposed to εἰς οὖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 626}}; τυράννου κατ᾽ ὀ. κατηγορεῖν to accuse him to his **face**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.14: _sing._ in the phrase πρὸς ὀφθαλμὸν ἐπιχεῖν, μίσγειν}}, **by eye,** {{Refs|PHolm. 7.23, “PLeid.X.” 62}}; **eyes** were painted on the bows of vessels, βλοσυροῖς κατὰ πρῷραν ὀφθαλμοῖς οἷον βλέπει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.19, compare “IG” 22.1607.24, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.86}}; whence the joke in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 97.}}
**2** στέρησις ὀφθαλμῶν temporary loss of **sight**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).400.}}
**II** in _singular_, **the eye** of a master or ruler, πάντα ἰδὼν Διὸς ὀ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 267; Δίκης ὀ. ὃς τὰ πάνθ᾽ ὁρᾷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 179; δεσπότου ὀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 12.20}}; ἀκοίμητος ὀ., of God, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 3}}; so a king is called ὀ. οἴκων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 934 (so ὄμμα “Pers.” 169)}}; and in Persia ὀφθαλμὸς βασιλέως **the king's eye** was a confidential officer, through whom he beheld his kingdom and subjects, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 979 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 92, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.10f, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b29, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.642}}; compare οὖς.
**III** **the eye of heaven**, ἑσπέρας ὀ., νυκτὸς ὀ., of the moon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.20, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 390}}; also οὐράνιος ὀ., of the sun, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 5.}}
**IV** **the dearest, best**, as the eye is the most precious part of the body, hence of men, ὀ. Σικελίας, στρατιᾶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.10, 6.16}}; also, **light, cheer, comfort**, μέγας [γ᾽] ὀ. οἱ πατρὸς τάφοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 987, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 406.}}
**V** **eye** or **bud** of a plant or tree, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 43, Ion Eleg. 1.6, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 26, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.10, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.8.5, etc.}}
**VI** **a surgical bandage covering one or both eyes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.29, 30.}}
**VII** Architecture texts, in dual, the **disks** forming the centres of the volutes of an Ionic capital, {{Refs|“IG” 12.374.291.}}

3789:
** ὄφις**, ὁ,
_genitive_ ὄφεως, _poetry_ also ὄφεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 703, “Ba.” 1026, 1331}}; _Doric dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ὄφιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 322, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.81, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 82}}:—**serpent**, αἰόλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.208; γλαυκῶπα ποικιλόνωτον ὄφιν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.249, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 544, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1328, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 112d, “R.” 358b, etc.}}; ὁ ψυχρὸς ὄ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.58}}; equivalent to δράκων in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 322, 825: _metaphorically_, πτηνὸν ἀργηστὴν ὄφιν, of an arrow, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 181.}}
**II** like{δράκων}, **a serpent-like bracelet**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 387, 4th c.BC: Nicostratus Philosophus Comedy texts 33, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 22}}; ὄφεις is _Attic dialect_ for ψέλλια _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus p.288 P.}}
**II.2** τρικάρηνος ὄ. ὁ χάλκεος dedicated at Delphi (= {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 31), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.81.}}
**III** the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Serpens”, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 82, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.18.}}
**IV** **a creeping plant**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.114.}}
**V** a kind of **fish**, see at {ὀφίδιον} {{Refs|11.}}
**VI** **guinea-worm** (elsewhere δρακόντιον), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “Interrog.” 65.}}
**VII** ={ὀφίασις} I, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus 6.4, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.192}}. [The first syllable is sometimes made long in the older Poets, αἰόλον ὄφιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.208, compare 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 49.6; so ὀφιοέσσης 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 78. It was then pronounced (and perhaps written) ὄπφις, ὀπφιοέσσης, see Eust. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place]}} —The _ultima _ of the _nominative_ and _accusative_ ὄφις, ὄφιν is commonly long, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 334, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 928, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1269, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 4.22}}; short only in later Poets, as {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.128, 1398, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 578.]}}

3790:
** ὀφρῦς**, ύος, ἡ,
_accusative_ ὀφρῦν, in late Poets ὀφρύα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.186 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus), 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.405, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 4.361: _accusative_ _plural_ ὀφρύας (in the fourth foot) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.389}}; but ὀφρῦς (before caesura) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.740, and so in _Attic dialect_ (see. below). [ῡ in _nominative_ and _accusative_, which are accented ὀφρῦς, -ῦν by Hdn.Gr. 2.937: the accentuation ὀφρύς, ὀφρύν}} may be admitted in late writers: compounds have ῠ, εὔοφρυς, λεύκοφρυς, etc.] (Cf. Sanskrit _bhrūs_, _genitive_ **bhruvas**, Slavonic _br[ucaron]see[icaron]_, O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **brú** 'brow'.):—**brow, eyebrow**, τὸν.. ὑπ᾽ ὀφρύος οὖτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.493}}; ἡ ὀ. ἡ δεξιά, ἡ ἀριστερά, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 671b32, compare “Pr.” 878b28: elsewhere in _plural_, ὑπ᾽ ὀφρύσι δάκρυα λεῖβον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.88, +others; ὑπ᾽ ὀ. πῦρ ἀμάρυσσεν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 827, etc.: frequently of signs, ἐπ᾽ ὀφρύσι νεῦσε Κρονίων, i.e. ἐπένευσε ὀφρύσι}}, **nodded assent**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.528, etc.}}; ἡ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ὀ. νεῦσε **nodded** to him to do a thing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.164}}; ἀνὰ δ᾽ ὀφρύσι νεῦον ἑκάστῳ **made a sign** not to do,{{Refs|9.468; ὀφρύσι νευστάζων 12.194: in various phrases expressing emotions, τὰς ὀ. ἀνασπᾶν}}, in token of grief, τὰς ὀφρῦς ἀνεσπακώς, ὥσπερ τι δεινὸν ἀγγελῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1069; ἀνασπάσας τις τὰς ὀφρῦς οἴμοι λαλεῖ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 556.3}}; of pride (compare ὀφρυόομαι), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.314; οἱ τὰς ὀφρῦς αἴροντες 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 39; ὀφρῦν ἐπαίρειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1040, compare 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 13; τὰς ὀ. ἔχειν ἐπάνω τῆς κορυφῆς 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 16.6; ὑπὲρ αὐτοὺς κροτάφους ὑπεραίρειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 54; ὀφρῦς ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 925; ὀφρῦν ἐφέλκεσθαι “Anthologia Graeca” 7.440.6 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus, interpolated ?)}}; ἐρύσσαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.215 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus); ἀνελκταῖς ὀφρύσι σεμνός 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 355: contrariwise, τὰς ὀφρῦς συνάγειν}} knit **the brows, frown**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 582, “Pl.” 756, etc.; τὰς ὀ. συνέλκειν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 307; συσπᾶν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 7; κατεσπακώς 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.3: on the other hand, καταβαλεῖν, λῦσαι, μεθεῖναι τὰς ὀ. or τὴν ὀ.}}, let down or unknit **the brow**, become calm or cheerful again, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 167, “Hipp.” 290, “IA” 648; ὀ. μὴ καθειμένη 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.58; σχάζεσθαι τὰς ὀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 32; καθέσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1062f}}: the brow was also the seat of smiles and joy, ἀγανᾷ χλοαρὸν γελάσσαις ὀφρύϊ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.38, compare “hymnus ad Cererem” 358; or gravity, στυγνὸν ὀφρύων νέφος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” [172]; ὁρᾶτε ὡς σπουδαῖαι μὲν αὐτοῦ αἱ ὀφρύες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.3}}; on their physiognomical character, see {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 491b14, “Phgn.” 812b26.}}
**2** ὀφρῦς alone, **scorn, pride,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 7.409 (Antip.), 9.43 (Parmenio Epigrammaticus), 10.122 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), etc.}}
**II** from like ness of shape, **brow of** **a hill, crag**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.151, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.106}}; **embankment**, ὀ. ἀπότομος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 36.8.3}}; **overhanging bank** of a river, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.33.7, etc.; ἐπ᾽ ὀφρύων ποταμοῦ “PAmh.” 2.68.9 (1st c.AD)}}; of the sea, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.178, etc.}}; of a ditch, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.3.7 (compare ὀφρύη)}}; of the **rim** of joint-cavities, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 1.15, +others}}; of the woodwork enclosing the bore of a torsion-engine, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 57.7}}: in Architecture texts, **architrave**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Procop.Gaz. p.157 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**III** a plant, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 26.164.}}

3791:
** ὀχλ-έω**,
(ὄχλος) **move, disturb**, ψηφῖδες ἅπασαι ὀχλεῦνται all the pebbles **are rolled** or **swept away** by the water, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.261.}}
**II** generally, **trouble, importune**, with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.41; ὀχλεῖς μάτην με 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1001}}; **disturb** citizens by false alarms, Aen. Tact.{{Refs|6.1: _absolutely_}}**to be troublesome** or **irksome**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.1.3, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 446, “OGI” 262.22 (from Baetocaece)}}; ὀ. πρὸς αὐγάς impede the sight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.147 = “Coac.” 191 (variant): frequently in Papyrus, “POxy.” 269 ii 4 (1st c.AD)}}, etc.:—_passive_, **to be troubled**, ὑπέρ τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1167a10, compare 1171b19; ἀσθενείᾳ σώματος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus “Fragmenta” 188; ὑπὸ ὑδέρου “Hippiatrica” 38; τὴν ἀκοήν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.18}}; compare ἐνοχλέω: later with _infinitive_, μὴ ὀχλοῦ δὲ πέμπειν τι ἡμῖν don't **trouble to..,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 1481.6 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** in _passive_, **to be crowded**, ὁδὸς.. ἥτις οὐ πολὺ ὀχλεῖται {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 15 (nisi πολυοχλεῖται to be read).}}

3793:
** ὄχλος**, ὁ,
**crowd, throng**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.85, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 42 (_anapaest meter_), etc.; ἐσὄχλον ἕρπειν παρθένοισιν οὐ καλόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 108, compare “Heracl.” 44}}; ὁ ὄ. τῶν στρατιωτῶν the **mass** of the soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.26, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.64, 7.62; μηδένα ὄ. Πελοποννησίων νεῶν [prev. author] 2.88}}; ὄχλῳ in **numbers** (for an army), {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.80; ὁ μισθοφόρος ὄ. [prev. author] 3.109, compare 4.56}}; οἱ τοιοῦτοι ὄ. **undisciplined masses** like these,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 126; ὄ. μᾶλλον ἢ στρατός 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.7.1}}; of **the camp-followers**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.26, 4.3.26, etc.}}
**2** in political sense, **populace, mob**, opposed to δῆμος (**people**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 304d; πρὸς ὄχλον ζῶν [prev. author] “Ax.” 368d; οἱ ὁμότιμοι ὤκνουν τὴν τοῦ ὄ. ἰσομοιρίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.21}}; δικαστηρίων καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὄ. and **popular assemblies** (in a contemptuous sense), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 455a, compare “Euthd.” 290a: proverbial, δι᾽ ὄχλου ἤδη τοῦτό γε}} this is already **in the mouths of the people**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 10, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.13.1, 4.9.2.}}
**3** generally, **mass, multitude**, ὄ. τὸν πλεῖστον λόγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 827; τὸν πλεῖστον ὄ. τῶν πραχθέντων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.192}}; ἵππων ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 191 (Lyric poetry); ἄκριτος ἄστρων ὄ. 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 19.5 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; σαρκῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 75e}}: in _plural_, **the masses**, καχεξία τις ὑποδέδυκε τοὺς ὄχλους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 24.4, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 161.1, 466.4; πιθανώτεροι οἱ ἀπαίδευτοι ἐν τοῖς ὄ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1395b28.}}
**II** **annoyance, trouble**, σχολὴν ὄ. τε μέτριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 635, etc.}}; ὄχλον παρέχειν to give **trouble**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 337, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84d}}; δι᾽ ὄχλου εἶναι, γενέσθαι, to be or become **troublesome**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 888, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.73, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.103a; μάταιον ὄ. τοὺς λόγους νομίσητε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.214; οἱ δὲ ἀντιλέγοντες ὄ. ἄλλως καὶ βασκανία κατεφαίνετο [prev. author] 19.24.}}

3796:
** ὀψέ**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **ὄψι** (which see), _adverb_ **after a long time, at length, late**, ἔκ τε καὶ ὀ. τελεῖ, opposed to αὐτίκα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.161; ὀ. κακῶς ἔλθοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.534, etc.}}; ὀ. διδάσκεσθαι, μανθάνειν, to be **late** in learning, learn **too late**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1425, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1264; ὀψέ γε φρονεῖς εὖ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 99; also ὀ. δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.399, etc.; ὀ. γοῦν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus [same place]; ὀ. περ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.80.}}
**b** ὀ. ἀφ᾽ οὗ.. **it is not long** since.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.14.}}
**2** **late in the day, at even**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.232, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.272, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.106, etc.}}; ὀφλεῖν.. ὀ. ὁδοῦ incur a penalty for **being out late at night**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 433a (uncertain)}}; **late in the season**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 485}}; ὀ. ἦν, ὀ. ἐγίγνετο, it was, it was getting, **late**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.16, 3.4.36}}; ἡ μάχη ἐτελεύτα ἐς (variant{ἕως}) ὀ. did not end till **late**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.108; so ἐς ὀψέ [prev. author] 8.23}}; but εἰς ὀ. ψηφίζεσθαι continue voting till **late in the day**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.15.}}
**3** with _genitive_, ὀ. τῆς ἡμέρας **late** in the day, ἤδη γὰρ τῆς ἡμέρας ὀ. ἦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.93, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.23; τῆς δ᾽ ὥρας ἐγίγνετ᾽ ὀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.84}}; ὀ. τῆς ἡλικίας **late** in life, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 14, compare “Am.” 37.}}
**4** as _preposition_ with _genitive_, ὀ. τούτων **after** these things, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.10, compare 4.18}}; so perhaps ὀ. σαββάτων **after** the sabbath day, [{{NT|NT.Matt.28.1.—For the _comparative_ and _superlative_ _adverbs_ see at {ὄψιος}.}}]

3799:
** ὄψις**, ἡ,
_genitive_ εως, _Ionic dialect_ ιος, (ὄπωπα):
**I** objective, **aspect, appearance** of a person or thing, πατρὸς φίλου ὄψιν ἀτυχθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.468; εἰσορόων ὄ. τ᾽ ἀγαθὴν καὶ μῦθον ἀκούων 24.632, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1412 (_anapaest meter_); δῶρον, οὐ σπουδαῖον εἰς ὄ. [prev. author] “OC” 577}}; πλείω τὴν ὄ. παρείχετο made the **appearance** greater, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46; ἀξιόλογον ὄ. παρέχεται τὸ δένδρος “PCair.Zen.” 157.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὥστε ὄ. καλὴν φαίνεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.9.9; εἰκάζεσθαι ἀπὸ τῆς φανερᾶς ὄ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10; τὴν ὄ. τοῦ σώματος προορᾶν [prev. author] 7.44}}: the _accusative_ is used _absolutely_, **in appearance**, τῷ ὄψιν ἐειδόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.15; στρογγύλος τὴν ὄ. 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 4; ἀστειότατον τὴν ὄ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 59}}; καλός τε κἀγαθὸς τὴν ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 127b}}; ἀπὸ τῆς ὄ. Ἑλληνικός to judge from his **looks**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 33.2; so ἐκ τῆς ὄ. “POxy.” 37 ii 3 (1st c.AD).}}
**I.b** **countenance, face**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 905, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240d, 254b, etc.}}; διοίδησις ὅλης ὄ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 58.83, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.44, 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.7}}; οὐκ ἄξιον ἀπ᾽ ὄψεως οὔτε φιλεῖν οὔτε μισεῖν οὐδένα by the **face** or **look** merely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.19}}; ἀδήλως τῇ ὄ. πλασάμενος so that nothing could be learnt from his **countenance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.58; τίνι δεδούλωταί ποτ; ὄψει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 541.2: _plural_, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 98.6, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 41.38.}}
**I.c** **visual impression** or **image** of an object, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 193c.}}
**I.2** **thing seen, sight**, φοβερὰν ὄ. προσιδέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 48 (_anapaest meter_), compare “Supp.” 567 (Lyric poetry); ὁρῶ Πυλάδην.., ἡδεῖαν ὄ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 727, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 887d, etc.}}; ἄλλην ὄ. οἰκοδομημάτων other architectural **sights**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.136}}; τὰ δὲ χρήματα.. ἔστιν ὄψις mere **outside show**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 33.2; πολλὴν ὄ. παρασχόντα ἔπειτα μηδὲν ὠφελῆσαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 44}}; of scenic representations, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1453b1, +others; ὁ τῆς ὄ. κόσμος [prev. work] 1449b33: _plural_, [prev. work] 1462a16.}}
**I.3** **vision, apparition**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.39, etc.; ὄ. ἐν τῷ ὕπνῳ [prev. author] 3.30, +others; ὄ. ἐνυπνίου [prev. author] 8.54; ὄ. ὀνείρου [prev. author] 1.38; ὄ. ἔννυχοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 645, compare “Ag.” 425 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 413, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 72 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 42(1).121.11 (Epid., 4th c.BC), etc.}}
**II** subjective, **power of sight** or **seeing, vision**, ὄψει τινὰ ἰδεῖν, ἐσιδεῖν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.205, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 25.94, compare 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 55, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 11, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 4.10, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75, etc.}}; τῆς ἐμῆς ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.99, 147; τῆς ὄ. στερηθῆναι [prev. author] 9.93, etc.}}; οὐ τὸ δρασθὲν πιστότερον ὄψει λαβόντες ἢ τὸ ἀκουσθέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38; οὐ καθορωμένους τῇ ὄ. νυκτὸς οὔσης [prev. work] 112.}}
**II.b** **act of seeing** or **looking**, ἡ εἰς τὸ ἄνω ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 396b}}; **sense of sight**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 428a6, “Mete.” 369b9}}; τὰ διὰ τῆς ὄ., of pleasures, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1118a3, etc.}}
**II.c** _plural_, **organs of sight, eyes**, ὄψεις μαρᾶναι to quench **the orbs of sight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1328, compare “Ant.” 52, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 26; τὸ κάλλος πάντων εἷλκε τὰς ὄ. ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.9}}; ἀσθενῖ (={-εῖ}) τὰς ὄ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“POxy.” 911.6 (3rd c.AD): so in _sing._, ἐστερήθη τῆς μιᾶς ὄ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.79.12}}: _singular_ in collective sense, **the eyes**, [ἰχθῦς] λευκὴν ἔχοντες τὴν ὄ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 602a11, compare “PA” 656b29.}}
**II.d** in Medicine texts, **iris** of the eye, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.19}} (but **eye-ball** or **eye**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prog.” 7 (_plural_))}}; also, **pupil**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 23.}}
**II.e** of the **visual rays** which were supposed to proceed from the eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 45c, 46b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 343a13, 370a19: in other places 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} controverted this Empedoclean theory, {{Refs|“Sens.” 437b14.}}
**II.2** **view, sight**, ἀπικνέεσθαι ἐς ὄψιν τινί come into one's **sight**, i.e. **presence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136}}; εἰς ὄψιν τινὸς or τινὶ ἥκειν, μολεῖν, ἐλθεῖν, περᾶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 215, “Pers.” 183, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 173 (_anapaest meter_), “Or.” 513}}; καλέσαι τινὰ ἐς ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106}}; ἀποφαίνειν τί τινι ἐς ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.81; λυπηρὰς τῇ ὄ. ἀχθηδόνας προστιθέμενοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37; ἐν ὄ. τοῦ δήμου 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 12}}; κατ᾽ ὄψιν **in person**, ὡς ἐνετειλάμην σοι κατ᾽ ὄ. as I enjoined you **when with you,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 1154.4 (1st c.AD), compare 117.3 (2nd-3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.2.b** **dignity, position**, κατὰ τὴν ἐμὴν ὄ. καὶ ὑπόλημψιν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.77.59 (6th c.AD)}}; ἡ τῶν γονέων ἡμῶν προτεραία ὄ. {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 2 iii6 (6th c.AD).}}
